{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_25","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have enough context to begin writing code and producing files.\n\nI'm writing the simulation core in Rust by hand. I've started defining the Hazard struct with fields for position, tool, work, drain, and a timer.\n\nThe Hazard struct now includes stage tracking, path data, grab state, quality metrics, and phase tags.\n\nDefined the hazard kind constants from incision through IV burn. Started the tool constants with scalpel.\n\nDefined the remaining tool constants from forceps through defib. Started sketching the `tool_tick` function for per-gesture hazard updates. For scalpel incisions, progress tracks distance along the hazard's axis.\n\nFleshed out the scalpel incision logic. The cursor must sit within 0.28 of the line and ahead of the current work point. Quality accumulates from lateral deviation. Healthy tissue damage triggers when deviation exceeds 0.7. Completion occurs once work reaches path length.\n\nStarted the forceps foreign object logic. A grab activates when the cursor is inside the radius and the tool is forceps. Once grabbed, the pull vector is projected along the hazard axis.\n\nFinished the forceps logic. Lateral deviation adds to quality and spawns a laceration if it exceeds 0.55. The grab drops when use is released before completion.\n\nStarted the suture logic for lacerations. The needle tracks along the axis length. Stitches register when the cursor crosses the axis near the needle and the side sign flips.\n\nThe last signed side for suture crossing is stored on the hazard's `aux` field. Suction pools drain work from pressure and distance. Laser polyps accumulate heat; exceeding 1.05 triggers burn damage and failure, while releasing between 0.8 and 1.02 completes with a rating based on proximity to 0.92.\n\nFinished the laser logic. Early release below 0.8 resets the hazard without completion.\n\nDefined gel as a coverage task. Work accumulates inside the radius and overspray adds to quality outside it. Completion depends on coverage ratio and overspray rating.\n\nStarted the scanner tool.\n\nScanner work accumulates while the cursor sits within 1.4 times the hazard radius. Completion reveals the hazard for eight seconds and marks it dead if it was a reveal-only type. Hidden foreign objects remain invisible to their own tool until the scanner sweep sets the revealed timer, which the bot then uses before forceps. IV port handling is next.\n\nIV port charging uses a two-second green band. Releasing inside it restores vitals and triggers a four-second cooldown. Releasing after the band causes an overdose.\n\nCryo behaves like laser but lasts one second. Exceeding the limit causes frostbite and a burn hazard.\n\nDressing is a gel-like line tool.\n\nBone requires forceps to rotate the fragment until angle drops below twelve degrees and distance falls below zero point three five. Then suturing needs two crossings.\n\nTumor requires scalpel ring work, then forceps, then gel.\n\nNodule moves every N beats. It rests and laser until its shell releases heat, then forceps within two beats or it steps away and spawns a laceration.\n\nSac swells over time. Laser the stem for 0.6 seconds or suction slowly; if aux hits max it bursts into a pool.\n\nEye needs two scalpel stages, then forceps, then gel.\n\nEmber is one hazard with three stages: cryo, scalpel crack, then forceps pieces.\n\nHeat ring is global and sweeps from 0 to 1.\n\nThe heat ring triggers scorch damage when a surgeon holds USE while the sweep crosses 0.5 and their cursor is inside the field. The bot releases USE between 0.35 and 0.7 to avoid this.\n\nTether requires scalpel work on three separate hazards.\n\nTendril only accepts forceps pulls during heart diastole (phase 0.35–0.65). Pulling during systole resets progress.\n\nA `Strain` director will manage phase-based hazard spawning and special motion updates instead of a fully separate simulation.\n\nDefined the `Strain` struct with fields for phase, beat wait, subphase, and flags.\n\nThe Introit phase wanders a single nodule that steps every three beats and leaves a laceration or pool. It splits into two nodules after the first is laser-popped and forceps-pulled. Once both are dead it spawns four small nodules. The final amen phase adds three tethers plus a large nodule. Hush unlocks when vitals fall below thirty-five or phase one begins.\n\nThe Introit nodule moves every 1.8 seconds. The bot must laser it during its rest window and pull it within two beats. If the nodule steps away mid-laser, the heat resets.\n\nIntroit nodules use a 0.9-second laser and 0.6-unit forceps pull to fit the 1.8-second rest window. The shell-popped state persists for two beats after the laser so the bot can still grab even if the nodule steps away. Started defining bot priority logic.\n\nThe bot priority function `pick_task` is now defined. It defibrillates first, releases the heat ring if it is approaching, and fires the gift gesture when the meter is full and vitals are low or strain is tense. Urgent drains like bleeding or bursting sacs are handled next, followed by hidden objects scanned first. The bot then targets the highest drain hazard that is actionable, then any remaining hazard, and finally idles at center with no button held. The gift gesture requires holding GIFT for about half a second until the next R-wave.\n\nThe gift meter requires one hundred points to trigger. Clean actions add four, Immortal adds eight, and on-beat adds two. Roughly fifteen immaculate actions fill it; early cases may not. The meter also refills when Hush wakes. A `Coverage` struct now tracks tool usage, hazard completions, strain phases, gifts, code actions, and ratings.\n\nCampaign tests require every non-zero coverage counter to be hit. Introit spans four phases, Lacrimosa four, Dies three, and Libera seven. All eleven tools, every hazard type, all three gifts, and all four ratings must appear. The bot may only reach IMMACULATE at skill 1.0.\n\nA unit test will construct actions with different errors to verify every rating is reachable. The campaign coverage records the bot's actual performance, so a deliberate sloppy gesture could hurt the rank. Instead, the test will drive both a sloppy and a perfect gesture from a case start.\n\nDefined the rank thresholds. ENCORE is 115% or above, S is 100%, A is 85%, B is 70%, and C is 50%. Scores below 50% still receive a C.\n\nBelow fifty percent par, the rank stamp will be blank even though the operation is still marked as won. Par values will start conservatively low so skill one can reach an S, then be tuned after a balance run.\n\nPar will be tuned to 80% of the minimum skill-1.0 score so every case reaches at least an S rank. Variance across seeds should stay low because hazard positions shift only slightly. Started writing an idle-loss test that loops through all eight cases.\n\nThe idle-loss test will run each case for up to twelve minutes. To keep the suite fast, every case now includes a draining hazard in its first phase so the bot dies within forty seconds.\n\nThe random-player test will run for four minutes per case. A bleeder must be sutured in three crossings, so random motion cannot clear it quickly. Stacked drains ensure the timer kills the bot before any operation completes. The worst-case simulation spans thirty-two minutes and roughly one million ticks.\n\nOnly one seed per random-player test is required, not ten. Skill zero always loses across all eight cases. Ten skill-one seeds will run, with an estimated two hundred sixteen thousand ticks total.\n\nCampaign simulations total roughly 216,000 ticks across ten seeds. The full test suite may reach 500,000 to 800,000 ticks. To keep the suite fast, history recording will be disabled during build and enabled only before the rewind test begins.\n\nDev mode will retain five seconds of history so rewind and snapshot save/load work in play. Tests can zero this window for speed. The clone cost is the main concern, since roughly three hundred full SimState copies are made per step and each includes the physics world.\n\nThe test suite will run as-is rather than optimizing SimState size first; the starter tests finish in under a second, so the larger simulation should still be manageable. A new `Shell` struct is being defined to hold screen state, input device, pause flags, and developer mode settings.\n\nThe Shell struct now holds fields for audio tuning, about data, save state, case selection, difficulty, and controls. It manages eleven screens including Title, Main, Difficulty, CaseFiles, CodeBlueSetup, Settings, About, Pause, Dev, Controls, Attract, TouchBlock, and Story. Headless tests will call Shell::tick directly, while draw() is needed only to capture the egui UI. The app's surgery frame updates the shell from device input and loads a case when the shell signals play.\n\nThe shell hides during operations unless paused. It draws via egui on the title screen and main menu, and renders the HUD during active cases. The attract scene loads when shell idle reaches ten seconds on those screens; touch-only mode triggers when a coarse pointer appears before any key or pad input, displaying a message and settings button while attract logic continues. A `prompt` helper maps actions to glyphs based on device type and Xbox status. The UI scales to a 10-foot zoom using the screen height divided by 1080 pixels.\n\nThe Mixer struct now carries per-bus gains for music, effects, and voices. Default levels are 0.10 for music, 0.80 for effects, and 0.70 for voices. Each voice is multiplied by its bus gain during rendering. The play method routes to the effects bus by default.\n\nA `cue_patch` helper will map cue identifiers to patches. The `voice_syllables` function splits text into syllables and assigns each a pitch based on its character and a formant based on its vowel. Patch gains two new fields, `formant` and `formant2`, both defaulting to zero. Voice rendering will apply a simple band-pass filter when either formant is active.\n\nThe band-pass filter will use a one-pole state update on Voice. Patch literals already rely on `..Patch::new()`, so adding the new formant fields to Patch and its constructor requires no manual updates.\n\nA `render_track` function will generate short sine melodies with bass for twelve tracks, including title, story, crisis, and case themes. The surgery view is being split into modules: plate.rs builds the mesh from plate ID and hazards, instruments.rs renders SDF tools at the cursor, strains.rs handles parasite SDFs, paper.rs draws story cards, fx.rs manages particles, and shots.rs derives camera from the frame.\n\nViewBuilder will hold a SurgeryView and emit it at the end of build when present. Scene lighting will be overridden at the end of the build if surgery is active. The SurgeryFrame will be constructed inside Sim::frame by retrieving the Surgery game state from the play context and snapshotting its frame.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-dfa7b544-6c62-477a-a92b-099ada180dbe-69","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_app/src/app.rs","offset":1550,"limit":120}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-dfa7b544-6c62-477a-a92b-099ada180dbe-70","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"fn render\\(|fn size\\(","glob":"**/pav_app/src/gfx.rs"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-dfa7b544-6c62-477a-a92b-099ada180dbe-71","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_app/src/input.rs","offset":1,"limit":100}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3281,"output_tokens":6578,"cache_read_input_tokens":129664,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"51a20800-0e01-4c3d-a9ad-40132030065b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-dfa7b544-6c62-477a-a92b-099ada180dbe-69","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1550→            crate::save::save_prefs(&self.prefs);\\n        }\\n        // The map: a tap on the minimap opens the big one; a tap or a swipe closes that.\\n        let sw = &mut self.touch.swipes;\\n        if sw.take_tap(egui::Id::new(\\\"minimap\\\")) {\\n            self.game_ui.map = true;\\n        }\\n        let big = egui::Id::new(\\\"big_map\\\");\\n        if sw.take_tap(big) || sw.is_gone(big, 0) {\\n            self.game_ui.map = false;\\n1560→            sw.restore(big);\\n        }\\n        if !self.prefs.tips_seen && sw.is_gone(egui::Id::new(\\\"touch_tips\\\"), 0) {\\n            self.prefs.tips_seen = true;\\n            crate::save::save_prefs(&self.prefs);\\n        }\\n        self.show_boot = show_boot;\\n        for a in guide_out {\\n            match a {\\n                crate::guide_ui::GuideAction::Go(key) => teleport = Some(crate::rooms::TeleportTarget::Room(key)),\\n1570→                crate::guide_ui::GuideAction::Toast(m) => self.toast = Some((m, Instant::now())),\\n            }\\n        }\\n        if let Some(m) = look_msg {\\n            self.toast = Some((m, Instant::now()));\\n        }\\n        self.look_ui.save_if_dirty(false);\\n        for c in game_cmds {\\n            host.shared.command(c);\\n        }\\n1580→        if ctl.paused != ctl_before.paused\\n            || ctl.speed != ctl_before.speed\\n            || ctl.step_requests != ctl_before.step_requests\\n            || ctl.rewind_speed != ctl_before.rewind_speed\\n        {\\n            let c = ctl;\\n            host.set_control(|x| {\\n                x.paused = c.paused;\\n                x.speed = c.speed;\\n                x.step_requests = c.step_requests;\\n1590→                x.rewind_speed = c.rewind_speed;\\n            });\\n        }\\n        if self.sim_config != config_before {\\n            let cfg = self.sim_config.clone();\\n            host.exec(move |sim| sim.config = cfg);\\n            self.config_push_tick = stats.tick.max(curr.tick);\\n        }\\n        if self.app_settings.vsync != app_before.vsync {\\n            gfx.set_vsync(self.app_settings.vsync);\\n1600→        }\\n        if self.audio_settings.master != master_before {\\n            if let Some(a) = &self.audio {\\n                a.set_master(self.audio_settings.master);\\n            }\\n        }\\n        state.platform_output(&gfx.window, out.platform_output);\\n        let ppp = out.pixels_per_point;\\n        let prims = self.egui_ctx.tessellate(out.shapes, ppp);\\n        let mut textures = out.textures_delta;\\n1610→\\n        #[cfg(target_arch = \\\"wasm32\\\")]\\n        if self.screenshot_requested {\\n            // Reading pixels back waits on the GPU, which the browser does not allow here.\\n            self.screenshot_requested = false;\\n            self.toast = Some((\\\"screenshots are not available in the browser\\\".into(), Instant::now()));\\n        }\\n        #[cfg(not(target_arch = \\\"wasm32\\\"))]\\n        if self.screenshot_requested {\\n            self.screenshot_requested = false;\\n1620→            let (w, h) = gfx.size();\\n            let dir = boot::exe_dir().join(\\\"screenshots\\\");\\n            let path = dir.join(format!(\\n                \\\"shardfall-{}.png\\\",\\n                web_time::SystemTime::now().duration_since(web_time::UNIX_EPOCH).map(|d| d.as_secs()).unwrap_or(0)\\n            ));\\n            let r = pav_render::capture::render_to_rgba(&mut gfx.renderer, &scene, w, h)\\n                .and_then(|px| pav_render::capture::save_png(&path, w, h, &px));\\n            self.toast = Some((\\n                match r {\\n1630→                    Ok(_) => format!(\\\"screenshot saved: {}\\\", path.display()),\\n                    Err(e) => format!(\\\"screenshot failed: {e:#}\\\"),\\n                },\\n                Instant::now(),\\n            ));\\n        }\\n\\n        let first = self.boot_done_at.is_none()\\n            && boot::diag().stages.lock().map(|s| !s.iter().any(|s| s.name == \\\"first frame\\\")).unwrap_or(false);\\n        let submitted = if first {\\n1640→            let submitted = stage(\\\"first frame\\\", || {\\n                let submitted = gfx.draw(&scene, Some((&prims, &mut textures, ppp)));\\n                Ok((submitted, format!(\\\"{} meshes, {} sdf\\\", gfx.renderer.stats.mesh_instances, gfx.renderer.stats.sdf_instances)))\\n            });\\n            self.boot_done_at = Some(Instant::now());\\n            log::info!(\\\"boot complete in {:.0} ms\\\", boot::diag().start.elapsed().as_secs_f64() * 1000.0);\\n            submitted.unwrap_or(false)\\n        } else {\\n            gfx.draw(&scene, Some((&prims, &mut textures, ppp)))\\n        };\\n1650→        #[cfg(not(target_arch = \\\"wasm32\\\"))]\\n        if submitted {\\n            if let Some(bridge) = &self.bridge {\\n                bridge.submitted(curr.live_edit_ticket);\\n            }\\n        }\\n        // Input latency: the frame showing the tick that consumed a press is now submitted.\\n        if submitted {\\n            let mut probe = host.shared.latency_probe.lock().unwrap();\\n            if let Some((pressed_at, ticked_at, tick)) = *probe {\\n1660→                if curr.tick >= tick {\\n                    let now = Instant::now();\\n                    self.latency.record(LatencySample {\\n                        to_tick: (ticked_at - pressed_at).as_secs_f32() * 1000.0,\\n                        to_screen: (now - ticked_at).as_secs_f32() * 1000.0,\\n                    });\\n                    *probe = None;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\",\"content_concise\":\"1550→            crate::save::save_prefs(&self.prefs);\\n        }\\n        // The map: a tap on the minimap opens the big one; a tap or a swipe closes 