lt() });\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/mod.rs\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":80,\"content\":\"    fn bot(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\"},{\"line_number\":157,\"content\":\"    pub fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\"},{\"line_number\":232,\"content\":\"    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\"},{\"line_number\":255,\"content\":\"    pub fn bot_input(&self) -> InputFrame {\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/tests/heroes.rs\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":22,\"content\":\"    sim.step(&InputFrame { cmd: Some(c), ..Default::default() });\"},{\"line_number\":167,\"content\":\"        sim.step(&InputFrame { held: buttons::PRIMARY, aim: Some(Vec3::new(0.0, 0.0, -4.0)), ..Default::default() });\"},{\"line_number\":180,\"content\":\"        sim.step(&InputFrame { held: b, pressed: b, ..Default::default() });\"},{\"line_number\":189,\"content\":\"        sim.step(&InputFrame { held: buttons::PRIMARY, aim: Some(Vec3::new(0.0, 0.0, -4.0)), ..Default::default() });\"},{\"line_number\":201,\"content\":\"        sim.step(&InputFrame { held: buttons::PRIMARY, ..Default::default() });\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"c9e93bc7-69e2-4361-83fe-6940d7d7d692"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_30","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Adding fields with Default is safe because most existing code uses `..Default::default()`.\n\nChecking ai.rs and the ARPG bot for full struct construction without Default. Will compile and fix any issues. Starting input changes and the surgery module. Designing bot and field logic in rules.rs with a tick_op function.\n\nThe `tick_op` function computes a scaled `rules_dt` using time scale and hush beat state. The heart ticks on this scaled delta. Surgeon cursors move using real `dt`. The tick then handles tremor, gifts, heat ring, strain motion, and tools.\n\nThe tick function applies tools, echo copies, and drains. Drains sum from active hazards multiplied by the tune drain rate, plus a 6-unit fever bonus when above 100. Heart vitals decrease by this total scaled by rules_dt unless the surgeon is immortal. IV heals are applied in the tool logic. Code, death, and timer checks run next. Phases advance once all hazards of that phase are dead or cleared.\n\nCase definitions will be parsed from TOML at build time into a `CaseDef` struct. The simulation will store a lightweight runtime case reference with only a phase index, looking up full static data via a stable `std::sync::LazyLock` lookup.\n\nThe case definition will be stored as `Option<Arc<CaseDef>>` inside `Surgery`. This makes snapshots cheap to serialize and clone. On load, the field is `None` and lazily rebuilt via an `ensure_case` method that fetches the definition by its ID.\n\nPhase transitions despawn cleared hazards and spawn fresh ones. Checkpoints save after spawning so retries restore the phase with its initial hazards. A `code` phase forces vitals to zero and enables scripted code mode. A `reveal` phase triggers a crossfade on the plate.\n\nThe bot's suture zigzag will use a laceration geometry with center, axis angle, and length. The needle position along the wound is sampled across the required stitch count. The bot oscillates across the wound using a side sign derived from the tick counter. It aims near the needle point and holds the use button. Stitch counts trigger when the cursor crosses the wound line.\n\nThe bot oscillates every 10 ticks. Stitch detection requires a sign change in the cursor's signed cross product with the wound axis, plus proximity checks to the needle and the line. The previous side is cached in the hazard's aux field. A minimum threshold of 0.05 prevents false triggers from zero initial values. The scalpel path will be an incision from a point along a direction vector for a set length.\n\nThe scalpel bot aims at a point ahead of the incision line and advances work by tracking cursor position along the line. It holds use and stays on the line for an immaculate rating.\n\nThe forceps bot targets an object until it is grabbed, then pulls along the axis while increasing pull distance. Work completes when it reaches one. Movement direction versus axis gives an immaculate angle rating.\n\nThe laser bot aims at the center and holds use until the hazard aux value reaches 0.90, then releases to trigger completion. It reads this stateless value directly from the hazard.\n\nFor gel, the bot will sit in the center or move in a small circle while inside the paint zone. Coverage increases only when the cursor is within the disk radius. The task completes after one second of time-in-radius, giving an immaculate rating. A more detailed variant could require the cursor to visit four quadrants.\n\nGel now requires the bot to circle at half radius and visit four quadrants. A u8 bitfield tracks visited quadrants; completion triggers when all bits are set, with quality based on time spent outside the zone.\n\nScanner work increases while the cursor stays inside the radius for 0.35 seconds. The bot can sit on the scanner during this sweep.\n\nWhen the IV tool is selected, the cursor will auto-move toward the port at 20 units per second.\n\nThe IV bot holds use until the hazard aux value reaches 0.85, then releases.\n\nDefib will keep a ghost heart phase advancing at the last BPM even when the ECG is flat. The bot holds DEFIB until charge reaches 1.2 and the ghost phase is within the window, then releases to trigger success.\n\nGift accumulates arm time while held; it fires on release if the phase is within the window or a beat occurred during the hold.\n\nGift will use a 100-meter channel. The bot holds GIFT until the next r-wave arrives while armed, then releases within an 110-millisecond window to trigger success.\n\nHush lasts six beats and scales rules_dt to thirty percent. On-beat actions during hush add a beat, capped at twelve.\n\nEcho lasts ten seconds. When slot zero completes an action, it finds the next hazard with the same tool and instantly completes it at the same rating. A ghost cursor position is stored for rendering. Echo also covers the gift.\n\nDuet activates both hush and echo together.\n\nTremor adds a deterministic sine offset to the cursor when the pet is active, precision is off, and echo is inactive.\n\nTremor will be applied as a display offset and aim error rather than baked into the cursor position every tick, preventing drift. The stored cursor represents the intended aim, and a `cursor_now()` helper adds the deterministic sine offset when reading the actual usable position for tools.\n\nStick input will slow and pull when the cursor is within 1.2 units of a valid target.\n\nWrong-tool clinks trigger only if the held tool does not match any hazard in radius but a nearby one exists within 0.8 units. A cooldown prevents repeated calls.\n\nChain multipliers scale at five, ten, and twenty clean ratings. Score equals base multiplied by chain multiplier, a beat bonus, and the tune multiplier.\n\nEnd bonus is vitals times twenty plus seconds left times five minus mistakes times one hundred. Each surgeon gets a personal score, chain, and seat colour. Team vitals and case win are shared. Rank is based on personal score versus par. Solo par matches one surgeon. The code blue director follows.\n\nCode blue director mode spawns a hazard from a set every few seconds, scaling the budget with surgeon count. The spawn rate increases every thirty seconds until the last thirty-second window, which holds a single introit nodule. The goal is to survive until the timer hits zero.\n\nIdle mode spawns bleeders that drain four vitals per second and expire after twenty-five seconds. A skill-one bot must clear them as they appear; otherwise death is fatal. At skill one the bot can keep up by clearing one hazard every two seconds over four minutes.\n\nHazard spawns will stack if the previous one is not cleared within eight seconds. The bot clears each hazard in two seconds, so idle mode only drains after the first hazard and then waits between waves. A four-minute run lasts about fourteen thousand ticks, which ten seeds can cover easily. The campaign may include code blue.\n\nThe campaign will exercise every tool, hazard, strain phase, gift, and Code. Case two provides the scripted Code, while Code Blue is a separate scene tested independently. Cases three and seven must remain fully intact. I am now writing the files in a compact style.\n\nStory data will be written as a Rust file and parsed from TOML to satisfy the lint. The script spans roughly one hundred sixty lines across all cases, with each case receiving a specific number of before and after lines.\n\nThe story script will total roughly two hundred lines, including in-op callouts. I will write the code files in a batch. I will update PROGRESS.md and mark checklist items as I go.\n\nThe code will be split into roughly five files: a ~1500-line `rules.rs`, an ~800-line `mod.rs`, a ~400-line `bot.rs`, a `content` TOML, and a ~200-line `story.rs`. A test will construct each case from its shipped start state and verify the initial mode is Chart, Op, or Story. It will also exercise driving the scalpel from case one.\n\nCase scenes will start directly in Op mode with phase zero spawned, so tests can use tools immediately without Chart. Campaigns will still run through story-chart-op-results. A `fresh(case, seed)` constructor will be added for test setup.\n\nI will download the AI hub session script, harness HTML, bridge, round logic, and README, plus the BarlowCondensed font, in parallel with writing the code.\n\nI will fetch the hub README while writing. The surgery module will be structured carefully to reduce bot bugs. Skill 1.0 gestures will use large success windows, while random tool players must sustain precise actions. I am now drafting a `random_loses` test that runs all eight cases with zero history.\n\nThe `random_loses` test will use a simple LCG for deterministic inputs, keeping the simulation RNG internal. It caps each case at three minutes and generates random tool selections and cursor positions within the valid bounds.\n\nThe random tool player test uses a simple LCG for deterministic inputs. It selects tools and cursor positions randomly and holds SNAP with occasional USE presses. The bot must sustain the correct tool on the target for sixty ticks to win. The probability of randomly landing within the required radius is roughly 0.3 percent. Combined with the one-in-eleven tool selection, this makes success effectively impossible. This confirms the test will reliably assert a loss.\n\nCharge tools will decay work when the cursor leaves their target, while suture stitches keep progress. This prevents random players from slowly accumulating charge across multiple targets.\n\nIdle mode drains vitals even without active surgeons. A case starting at 80 vitals and draining 2.5 per second kills the patient in about 32 seconds. The Code scene adds five seconds of grace before death, and successful defibrillation restores vitals to 30.\n\nEach failed code attempt consumes one charge. A successful defibrillation restores vitals to thirty and decrements the remaining count. If the five-second window is missed, death occurs immediately with no second chance on that flatline. The next code is only available after the patient drops to zero again.\n\nScripted defibrillation attempts in case two will be free and will not consume the code budget. If the player fails that scripted attempt, they die immediately even if other codes remain. A chief with zero codes will also die instantly at vitals zero unless a scripted stop occurs. I will add a `drive` test helper and write the shell script after the simulation compiles.\n\nI will add `#[serde(default)]` to RenderFrame and fix the resulting compile errors. I am defining a shared SurgeryFrame in `games/surgery/frame.rs` for both the view and simulation to use, but I must avoid a module cycle: frame.rs cannot reference `games::surgery::SurgeryFrame` while games already uses `frame::SimEvent`.\n\nRust allows circular module dependencies as long as types do not have infinite size. I will place SurgeryFrame in games/surgery/frame.rs and reference it from pav_core::frame via a type alias, accepting the circular dependency.\n\nThe `frame_of` helper will read runtime fields like hazards and heart directly from the simulation, so it can run on an immutable reference without loading the case definition. I will also export the game title and keep dev sliders in both the Surgery module and the Shell.\n\nDev sliders will change live simulation values. The shell will test its own tune state, while a simulation unit test verifies that drain multipliers affect vitals. Tunes can be applied immediately even while paused, since the simulation reads them each tick and stores the value.\n\nI will write the code in a few large batches. I will update PROGRESS.md now and seed the game title into a constant file first so it is not lost.\n\nStory content will be embedded as a large string in the Rust binary. Cases will be defined in Rust structs and written to matching TOML files for loading, with Rust serving as the single source of truth. I am drafting a `CaseFile` struct with fields for id, name, surgeon, plate, timer, par, vitals, and guide.\n\nThe `CaseFile` struct now includes patient, fate, gift, strain, partner, code_blue, and a vector of `PhaseFile` entries. A `SpawnFile` struct will define tool spawn points with position, radius, drain, hidden flag, axis, length, need, and tool type. Both structs will use `#[serde(default)]` for mechanical defaults. The plan is to write a Rust case builder that also emits matching TOML data.\n\nI will keep case definitions in Rust only, avoiding duplication. I considered generating TOML from a Python script or a test, but the plan is to write the data directly in `cases.rs` and let the loader parse it.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-c21a8b1a-efa0-40dd-bfa7-b8391e7158be-77","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"pub struct MeshData|impl MeshData|impl Default for MeshData","glob":"**/pav_render/src/mesh.rs"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-c21a8b1a-efa0-40dd-bfa7-b8391e7158be-78","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_render/src/scene.rs","offset":650,"limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2483,"output_tokens":6354,"cache_read_input_tokens":149760,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"f15b7522-599f-4a47-badf-407636fc39bb"}
