f, config: Arc<SimConfig>, dt: f32, ticket: u64) -> RenderFrame {\"},{\"line_number\":311,\"content\":\"        RenderFrame {\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-846f082e-62a7-400f-b62b-1c09bd9231ea-30","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→//! Pavilion simulation core: headless (no window, GPU or audio dependencies).\\n// `x as Real` casts are no-ops with f32 physics but required by the f64 switch.\\n#![allow(clippy::unnecessary_cast)]\\n\\npub mod ai;\\npub mod anim;\\npub mod animation_edit;\\npub mod animation_preview;\\npub mod arpg;\\n10→pub mod behaviors;\\npub mod catalogue;\\npub mod character;\\npub mod clip_browser;\\npub mod clips;\\npub mod color;\\npub mod course;\\npub mod creature_preview;\\npub mod creatures;\\npub mod destruct;\\n20→pub mod entity;\\npub mod feel;\\npub mod figure;\\npub mod frame;\\npub mod fxdef;\\npub mod games;\\npub mod guide;\\npub mod health;\\npub mod history;\\npub mod input;\\n30→pub mod issue;\\npub mod joints;\\npub mod level;\\npub mod material;\\npub mod moves;\\npub mod nav;\\npub mod params;\\npub mod parts;\\npub mod physics;\\npub mod projectile;\\n40→pub mod prop_instance;\\npub mod prop_preview;\\npub mod props;\\npub mod puppet;\\npub mod rig;\\npub mod rng;\\npub mod room;\\npub mod scenes;\\npub mod shape;\\npub mod sim;\\n50→pub mod softbody;\\npub mod statics;\\npub mod stealth;\\npub mod store;\\npub mod terrain;\\npub mod vehicle;\\npub mod world;\\npub mod zones;\\n\\npub use color::Color;\\n60→pub use entity::{Behavior, BodyKind, Entity, EntityId, Spawn};\\npub use frame::{RenderFrame, RenderObject, SimEvent};\\npub use glam;\\npub use input::InputFrame;\\npub use rapier;\\npub use shape::{Look, Shape, Visual};\\npub use sim::{Sim, SimConfig, SimState};\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→//! Pavilion simulation core: headless (no window, GPU or audio dependencies).\\n// `x as Real` casts are no-ops with f32 physics but required by the f64 switch.\\n#![allow(clippy::unnecessary_cast)]\\n\\npub mod ai;\\npub mod anim;\\npub mod animation_edit;\\npub mod animation_preview;\\npub mod arpg;\\n10→pub mod behaviors;\\npub mod catalogue;\\npub mod character;\\npub mod clip_browser;\\npub mod clips;\\npub mod color;\\npub mod course;\\npub mod creature_preview;\\npub mod creatures;\\npub mod destruct;\\n20→pub mod entity;\\npub mod feel;\\npub mod figure;\\npub mod frame;\\npub mod fxdef;\\npub mod games;\\npub mod guide;\\npub mod health;\\npub mod history;\\npub mod input;\\n30→pub mod issue;\\npub mod joints;\\npub mod level;\\npub mod material;\\npub mod moves;\\npub mod nav;\\npub mod params;\\npub mod parts;\\npub mod physics;\\npub mod projectile;\\n40→pub mod prop_instance;\\npub mod prop_preview;\\npub mod props;\\npub mod puppet;\\npub mod rig;\\npub mod rng;\\npub mod room;\\npub mod scenes;\\npub mod shape;\\npub mod sim;\\n50→pub mod softbody;\\npub mod statics;\\npub mod stealth;\\npub mod store;\\npub mod terrain;\\npub mod vehicle;\\npub mod world;\\npub mod zones;\\n\\npub use color::Color;\\n60→pub use entity::{Behavior, BodyKind, Entity, EntityId, Spawn};\\npub use frame::{RenderFrame, RenderObject, SimEvent};\\npub use glam;\\npub use input::InputFrame;\\npub use rapier;\\npub use shape::{Look, Shape, Visual};\\npub use sim::{Sim, SimConfig, SimState};\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/lib.rs\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"//! Pavilion simulation core: headless (no window, GPU or audio dependencies).\\n// `x as Real` casts are no-ops with f32 physics but required by the f64 switch.\\n#![allow(clippy::unnecessary_cast)]\\n\\npub mod ai;\\npub mod anim;\\npub mod animation_edit;\\npub mod animation_preview;\\npub mod arpg;\\npub mod behaviors;\\npub mod catalogue;\\npub mod character;\\npub mod clip_browser;\\npub mod clips;\\npub mod color;\\npub mod course;\\npub mod creature_preview;\\npub mod creatures;\\npub mod destruct;\\npub mod entity;\\npub mod feel;\\npub mod figure;\\npub mod frame;\\npub mod fxdef;\\npub mod games;\\npub mod guide;\\npub mod health;\\npub mod history;\\npub mod input;\\npub mod issue;\\npub mod joints;\\npub mod level;\\npub mod material;\\npub mod moves;\\npub mod nav;\\npub mod params;\\npub mod parts;\\npub mod physics;\\npub mod projectile;\\npub mod prop_instance;\\npub mod prop_preview;\\npub mod props;\\npub mod puppet;\\npub mod rig;\\npub mod rng;\\npub mod room;\\npub mod scenes;\\npub mod shape;\\npub mod sim;\\npub mod softbody;\\npub mod statics;\\npub mod stealth;\\npub mod store;\\npub mod terrain;\\npub mod vehicle;\\npub mod world;\\npub mod zones;\\n\\npub use color::Color;\\npub use entity::{Behavior, BodyKind, Entity, EntityId, Spawn};\\npub use frame::{RenderFrame, RenderObject, SimEvent};\\npub use glam;\\npub use input::InputFrame;\\npub use rapier;\\npub use shape::{Look, Shape, Visual};\\npub use sim::{Sim, SimConfig, SimState};\\n\",\"total_lines\":66}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"9c8f96c6-bf8d-44a3-97de-3be2ec9648c2"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_50","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Writing the core simulation files.\n\nVerifying the suction pool mechanics. At full pressure, 0.7 work completes in roughly 42 ticks. This confirms skill 1.0 can finish before the bot runs out of time.\n\nChecking the laser tool timing. The bot holds until auxiliary reaches 0.90, which takes about 62 ticks. It releases on the tick after that threshold. Because the bot reads state before the step, it sees the previous tick's value and releases immediately when the threshold is crossed. During that release tick, the auxiliary level stays flat since use_held is false.\n\nConfirmed the laser release window is safe. Even if the bot releases one tick late, auxiliary stays below 1.08 and avoids overheat.\n\nFor Skill 0, holding until 1.18 is fine. If the bot is slow, the hazard dies at 1.08 from overheat. The phase still advances because the hazard is dead.\n\nSkill 0 could clear heat hazards by overheating them. The orchestrator would count this as a cleared case with a sloppy rating and a vitals penalty. The bot might not stay near long enough to fill the heat pool before overheat occurs.\n\nSkill 0.4 is unplayable. Its noise and radius make it impossible to stay near the laser long enough to fill auxiliary. The release threshold of 1.068 sits below both the overheat limit and the safe window, so any missed attempt resets auxiliary to zero without completing the case.\n\nSuture noise of 0.79 may still allow occasional crossings, so early cases could finish slowly. The laceration drain is 0.7 per second; a 60-second delay drops vitals to 36 and is survivable, but 120 seconds kills the bot. Incision axis 0 means the cut runs along the +X direction.\n\nIncision targets must stay inside the gameplay bounds. A cut starting at x = -2 with axis 0 and length 2.4 ends at 0.4, so it remains fully inside [-9, 9]. The forceps pull uses the foreign axis direction, defaulting to 0.6 radians, and pulls from the grab point along that vector.\n\nForceps pulls end about 1.0 unit from the grab point. Tendril pulls span roughly 1.45 units. Suture needle crosses at ±0.5 side offsets along the 1.8-unit wound span. Gel quadrants require four bits and about 23 ticks of work. The bot circles at radius 0.45, completing a full rotation in about 31 ticks.\n\nBone rot requires holding forceps at the center while auxiliary is above 12. Below that threshold, the bot rotates left without using tools.\n\nIV requires holding until auxiliary reaches 0.88. That takes about 87 ticks. The release window is 0.75 to 1.05. Overdose occurs at 1.12.\n\nSkill 1 and IV will both release at 0.90 auxiliary, which falls safely inside the 0.75 to 1.05 window.\n\nSkill 0 can still clear IV by overdosing it at 1.18, though that counts as a sloppy heal.\n\nScanner behavior is confirmed: holding near a hidden hazard reveals it and switches tools, while foreign and nodule checks return early without completing.\n\nThe heat ring sweep will be implemented in the orchestrator by incrementing an angle each tick.\n\nThe heat ring sweep ends after one full rotation and counts coverage. Surgeons holding USE near the sweep line lose 12 vitals, reset their chain, and incur a mistake. The bot will drop USE during the sweep to avoid interrupting gestures. Wrong-tool cooldowns prevent spamming. Chain time accumulates between successes and resets on completion. Actions finished on beat add two points to the score meter.\n\nPartner slot 1 in case 7 will run bot logic on the next highest-priority hazard when the input slot is inactive.\n\nEcho auto-completion copies one completed action onto the next same-tool hazard with the same rating. It runs once per hazard and lasts ten seconds, so roughly two or three copies may occur.\n\nOne-hit mode will instantly finish an engaged hazard with an immaculate rating. Suture samples persist across ticks, so random zigzagging could theoretically stitch the laceration by chance. To prevent this, work decay will be added for unengaged hazards.\n\nI will add an `idle` field to the Hazard struct. When a surgeon is not engaged, work decays by 0.8 per second and auxiliary by 0.5 per second after 0.35 seconds of inactivity. Bone rot is excluded from this decay.\n\nThe new `idle` field will use `#[serde(default)]` to remain compatible with old snapshots. `blank()` and `make()` must be updated to initialize it.\n\nOverheating a heat tool kills the hazard, yet the phase still advances because the rating is sloppy and coverage increments. Heat rings are treated as non-blocking hazards so they do not stall phase progression.\n\nThe case structure is being defined as a list of phases. Each phase specifies spawns, a checkpoint flag, strain index, callout, reveal plate, scripted code flag, gift unlock bit, and extra wait time. Spawn data includes kind, position, hidden state, axis, and drain values.\n\nStrain cases with timed spawns will use a hybrid director. Cases 3, 4, 5, and 7 run through `strain_tick` for dynamic hazard spawning and phase transitions. Other cases follow the simple phase list. The introit section is next.\n\nWander spawns three drifting nodules and completes when all die. Split spawns two nodules that split into four once the laser finishes, filling the hush meter on the second wave. Smalls uses three quick nodules. Amen is a quiet suture laceration or final nodule. Hush is available from the start of split's second wave.\n\nNodules drift by adding a small sine offset to their x position each tick while staying inside bounds.\n\nLacrimosa uses six sacs per wave. A sac spawns a pool only if it bursts from overheat or auxiliary overflow. The bot will avoid both, so no pools appear and the phase completes when all sacs die. Later waves follow the same pattern.\n\nThe eye hazard has three stages: two scalpel strikes, then forceps. Gel is treated as a separate inflam after the eye.\n\nSacs accumulate auxiliary at 0.08 per second and burst into pools once they reach 1.15 while alive. The bot’s sequential laser time is too short to let any sac overflow, so no burst occurs.\n\nDies Irae spawns five embers in cryo, scalpel, and forceps order. Heat rings appear every fifth beat during this phase. It is unclear whether the embers merge.\n\nDies Irae will use two small embers followed by a large one. A furnace variant adds cryo, heat rings, and burn gel.\n\nLibera has five parts. A spawns nodules and pools. B uses sacs. C pairs embers with heat. D delivers scripted shocks that force vitals to zero; surviving fewer than three drops them again. E is a soloist scalpel incision, tendril, and dressing. The duet unlocks at E and the partner helps on the tendril.\n\nCode blue runs for 240 seconds. Every 20 seconds it spawns a rotating pair of hazards plus drainers. A small nodule strain may appear before the 30-second mark.\n\nCode blue ends when the timer reaches zero and vitals remain above zero. The player count is drawn from active surgeons at the start, with a minimum of one. Spawn counts scale up for larger groups.\n\nThe campaign flows through story blocks, chart, operation, results, and after-story segments before advancing to the next case. After case seven the final story block leads to victory.\n\nStory text will advance one line per tick. The bot may skip entire blocks instantly.\n\nStory lines advance one per confirm. The bot confirms every tick, so a full campaign takes about three hundred ticks.\n\nChart and results each need only one confirm to proceed.\n\nIn the single-case scene, the operation ends immediately with a win. Rank is computed before that win flag is set, so the bot never enters the results screen.\n\nThe title scene will run in Title mode without an operation, so a bot launched there never wins. The test harness loads cases via `Sim::new(\"surgery/case/0\", seed)`, with scene names mapping to specific modes: \"title\", \"campaign\", \"case/N\", \"codeblue\", \"attract\", and \"card\".\n\nThe scene name \"mirror\" maps to the client mirror. Resident difficulty is the default, and the bot can request SetDifficulty. The remaining resident codes are two, one for attending, and zero for chief. Timer values are generous so the bot can win: case 0 has 150 seconds and vitals of 72, case 1 has 180 seconds and vitals of 70, cases 2 through 7 and code blue each have 240 seconds. Idle death from drain is calculated at 1.15 per second, so a laceration and pool combination kills a 72-vital character in about 68 seconds. A bleeder was assigned to case 1.\n\nCase 0 laceration drain is raised to 1.6 and pool drain to 0.8 so idle death happens in about thirty seconds. Custom spawn drains override defaults.\n\nThe bot targets the highest-drain hazard first, then sorts by kind.\n\nOnly slot zero is filled unless a partner is present.\n\nAim is clamped to x[-9, 9] and z[-4, 4.2].\n\nTremor offsets the pet's hands by a small sine wave. The bot disables this during precision mode, so its aim stays exact. A successful IV injection triggers an eight-second cooldown to prevent infinite healing.\n\nIV hazards are not killed when hit, so the orchestrator must set them dead on completion. Pool hazards already clear themselves.\n\nSacs and heat tools clear themselves when suctioned or overheated. The finish() method marks the final stage done and advances the phase. Every stage completion triggers scoring, including intermediate ones, so multi-stage hazards earn multiple ratings. Echo completions also count toward the score.\n\nCoverage tracking will count tool completions, hazard clears, ratings, gift activations, strain phase entries, and code starts. A miscue counter will track chain breaks. An idle field is also being added.\n\nThe orchestrator will decay unengaged hazard work by 1.5 per second. Pools and inflams lose work when the surgeon does not target them. Suture samples will be zeroed if left idle.\n\nAn `idle` field will be added to the `Hazard` struct to track unengaged time. The bot returns an `InputFrame` that fills only `surgeons[0]`; partner control is handled internally when slot 1 is inactive. The heat tool is a keep tool with no accept logic, and gel overspray sets quality.\n\nPartner actions are deterministic because they derive from game state rather than input, so replaying inputs correctly reproduces them. Default serde attributes will be added to Surgery fields for forward compatibility. Mode constants will live in the kinds module or a dedicated mod. Story text will be compact but real, roughly two hundred lines total, structured as speaker and text pairs per line.\n\nA content lint function will scan all static strings for empty entries, TODO markers, and banned words. The checkpoint test will play until phase two, then use a dev kill to drop vitals and fail the code so the character dies; retrying restores vitals and the phase.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-b86e2476-a26f-4aba-8db1-6669f9359564-31","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/history.rs","limit":80}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-b86e2476-a26f-4aba-8db1-6669f9359564-32","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/sim.rs","offset":820,"limit":50}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":5359,"output_tokens":5850,"cache_read_input_tokens":76928,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"09ecbceb-726c-45e6-bd4b-741c98932688"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-b86e2476-a26f-4aba-8db1-6669f9359564-31","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→//! Time travel: periodic snapshots plus the input of every tick. Rewinding restores the nearest\\n//! earlier snapshot and re-simulates forward with the recorded inputs.\\n\\nuse std::collections::VecDeque;\\n\\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\\n\\nuse crate::input::InputFrame;\\nuse crate::sim::SimState;\\n10→\\npub struct History {\\n    pub snapshots: VecDeque<SimState>,\\n    /// (tick, input used to advance from `tick` to `tick + 1`)\\n    pub inputs: VecDeque<(u64, InputFrame)>,\\n    /// Ticks between snapshots.\\n    pub interval: u64,\\n    /// How far back rewind can go (ticks).\\n    pub window: u64,\\n    pub enabled: bool,\\n20→}\\n\\nimpl Default for History {\\n    fn default() -> Self {\\n        Self { snapshots: VecDeque::new(), inputs: VecDeque::new(), interval: 20, window: 60 * 30, enabled: true }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nimpl History {\\n    pub fn clear(&mut self) {\\n30→        self.snapshots.clear();\\n        self.inputs.clear();\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Call before stepping from `state.tick` with `input`.\\n    pub fn record(&mut self, state: &SimState, input: &InputFrame) {\\n        if !self.enabled {\\n            return;\\n        }\\n        if self.snapshots.back().is_none_or(|s| state.tick >= s.tick + self.interval) {\\n40→            self.snapshots.push_back(state.clone());\\n        }\\n        self.inputs.push_back((state.tick, *input));\\n        while self.snapshots.len() > 1 && self.snapshots[1].tick + self.window <= state.tick {\\n            self.snapshots.pop_front();\\n        }\\n        let oldest = self.snapshots.front().map(|s| s.tick).unwrap_or(0);\\n        while self.inputs.front().is_some_and(|(t, _)| *t < oldest) {\\n            self.inputs.pop_front();\\n        }\\n50→    }\\n\\n    pub fn oldest_tick(&self) -> Option<u64> {\\n        self.snapshots.front().map(|s| s.tick)\\n    }\\n\\n    pub fn newest_tick(&self) -> Option<u64> {\\n        self.inputs.back().map(|(t, _)| t + 1)\\n    }\\n\\n60→    pub fn input_at(&self, tick: u64) -> Option<InputFrame> {\\n        let front = self.inputs.front()?.0;\\n        let i = tick.checked_sub(front)? as usize;\\n        self.inputs.get(i).filter(|(t, _)| *t == tick).map(|(_, f)| *f)\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Forgets everything after `tick` (acting after a rewind starts a new timeline).\\n    pub fn truncate_after(&mut self, tick: u64) {\\n        self.snapshots.retain(|s| s.tick <= tick);\\n        while self.inputs.back().is_some_and(|(t, _)| *t >= tick) {\\n70→            self.inputs.pop_back();\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Rough memory use in bytes.\\n    pub fn approx_bytes(&self) -> usize {\\n        self.snapshots.iter().map(|s| s.approx_bytes()).sum::<usize>()\\n            + self.inputs.len() * std::mem::size_of::<(u64, InputFrame)>()\\n    }\\n}\\n80→\",\"content_concise\":\"1→//! Time travel: periodic snapshots plus the input of every tick. Rewinding restores the nearest\\n//! earlier snapshot and re-simulates forward with the recorded inputs.\\n\\nuse std::collections::VecDeque;\\n\\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\\n\\nuse crate::input::InputFrame;\\nuse crate::sim::SimState;\\n10→\\npub struct History {\\n    pub snapshots: VecDeque<SimState>,\\n    /// (tick, input used to advance from `tick` to `tick + 1`)\\n    pub inputs: VecDeque<(u64, InputFrame)>,\\n    /// Ticks between snapshots.\\n    pub interval: u64,\\n    /// How far back rewind can go (ticks).\\n    pub window: u64,\\n    pub enabled: bool,\\n20→}\\n\\nimpl Default for History {\\n    fn default() -> Self {\\n        Self { snapshots: VecDeque::new(), inputs: VecDeque::new(), interval: 20, window: 60 * 30, enabled: true }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nimpl History {\\n    pub fn clear(&mut self) {\\n30→        self.snapshots.clear();\\n        self.inputs.clear();\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Call before stepping from `state.tick` with `input`.\\n    pub fn record(&mut self, state: &SimState, input: &InputFrame) {\\n        if !self.enabled {\\n            return;\\n        }\\n        if self.snapshots.back().is_none_or(|s| state.tick >= s.tick + self.interval) {\\n40→            self.snapshots.push_back(state.clone());\\n        }\\n        self.inputs.push_back((state.tick, *input));\\n        while self.snapshots.len() > 1 && self.snapshots[1].tick + self.window <= state.tick {\\n            self.snapshots.pop_front();\\n        }\\n        let oldest = self.snapshots.front().map(|s| s.tick).unwrap_or(0);\\n        while self.inputs.front().is_some_and(|(t, _)| *t < oldest) {\\n            self.inputs.pop_front();\\n        }\\n50→    }\\n\\n    pub fn oldest_tick(&self) -> Option<u64> {\\n        self.snapshots.front().map(|s| s.tick)\\n    }\\n\\n    pub fn newest_tick(&self) -> Option<u64> {\\n        self.inputs.back().map(|(t, _)| t + 1)\\n    }\\n\\n60→    pub fn input_at(&self, tick: u64) -> Option<InputFrame> {\\n        let front = self.inputs.front()?.0;\\n        let i = tick.checked_sub(front)? as usize;\\n        self.inputs.get(i).filter(|(t, _)| *t == tick).map(|(_, f)| *f)\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Forgets everything after `tick` (acting after a rewind starts a new timeline).\\n    pub fn truncate_after(&mut self, tick: u64) {\\n        self.snapshots.retain(|s| s.tick <= tick);\\n        while self.inputs.back().is_some_and(|(t, _)| *t >= tick) {\\n70→            self.inputs.pop_back();\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Rough memory use in bytes.\\n    pub fn approx_bytes(&self) -> usize {\\n        self.snapshots.iter().map(|s| s.approx_bytes()).sum::<usize>()\\n            + self.inputs.len() * std::mem::size_of::<(u64, InputFrame)>()\\n    }\\n}\\n80→\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/history.rs\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"//! Time travel: periodic snapshots plus the input of every tick. Rewinding restores the nearest\\n//! earlier snapshot and re-simulates forward with the recorded inputs.\\n\\nuse std::collections::VecDeque;\\n\\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\\n\\nuse crate::input::InputFrame;\\nuse crate::sim::SimState;\\n\\npub struct History {\\n    pub snapshots: VecDeque<SimState>,\\n    /// (tick, input used to advance from `tick` to `tick + 1`)\\n    pub inputs: VecDeque<(u64, InputFrame)>,\\n    /// Ticks between snapshots.\\n    pub interval: u64,\\n    /// How far back rewind can go (ticks).\\n    pub window: u64,\\n    pub enabled: bool,\\n}\\n\\nimpl Default for History {\\n    fn default() -> Self {\\n        Self { snapshots: VecDeque::new(), inputs: VecDeque::new(), interval: 20, window: 60 * 30, enabled: true }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nimpl History {\\n    pub fn clear(&mut self) {\\n        self.snapshots.clear();\\n        self.inputs.clear();\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Call before stepping from `state.tick` with `input`.\\n    pub fn record(&mut self, state: &SimState, input: &InputFrame) {\\n        if !self.enabled {\\n            return;\\n        }\\n        if self.snapshots.back().is_none_or(|s| state.tick >= s.tick + self.interval) {\\n            self.snapshots.push_back(state.clone());\\n        }\\n        self.inputs.push_back((state.tick, *input));\\n        while self.snapshots.len() > 1 && self.snapshots[1].tick + self.window <= state.tick {\\n            self.snapshots.pop_front();\\n        }\\n        let oldest = self.snapshots.front().map(|s| s.tick).unwrap_or(0);\\n        while self.inputs.front().is_some_and(|(t, _)| *t < oldest) {\\n            self.inputs.pop_front();\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    pub fn oldest_tick(&self) -> Option<u64> {\\n        self.snapshots.front().map(|s| s.tick)\\n    }\\n\\n    pub fn newest_tick(&self) -> Option<u64> {\\n        self.inputs.back().map(|(t, _)| t + 1)\\n    }\\n\\n    pub fn input_at(&self, tick: u64) -> Option<InputFrame> {\\n        let front = self.inputs.front()?.0;\\n        let i = tick.checked_sub(front)? as usize;\\n        self.inputs.get(i).filter(|(t, _)| *t == tick).map(|(_, f)| *f)\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Forgets everything after `tick` (acting after a rewind starts a new timeline).\\n    pub fn truncate_after(&mut self, tick: u64) {\\n        self.snapshots.retain(|s| s.tick <= tick);\\n        while self.inputs.back().is_some_and(|(t, _)| *t >= tick) {\\n            self.inputs.pop_back();\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Rough memory use in bytes.\\n    pub fn approx_bytes(&self) -> usize {\\n        self.snapshots.iter().map(|s| s.approx_bytes()).sum::<usize>()\\n            + self.inputs.len() * std::mem::size_of::<(u64, InputFrame)>()\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n\",\"total_lines\":109}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-b86e2476-a26f-4aba-8db1-6669f9359564-32","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"820→                if b.is_dynamic() {\\n                    let m = b.mass();\\n                    b.apply_impulse(dir * push * fall * m, true);\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n        self.state.physics.wake_near(pos, reach + 4.0);\\n        events.push(SimEvent::Explosion { pos, radius });\\n    }\\n\\n830→    /// Runs `n` ticks with the same input.\\n    pub fn run(&mut self, n: u64, input: &InputFrame) {\\n        for _ in 0..n {\\n            self.step(input);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Takes the events produced since the last call.\\n    pub fn drain_events(&mut self) -> Vec<SimEvent> {\\n        std::mem::take(&mut self.events)\\n840→    }\\n\\n    // ------------------------------------------------------------------ time travel\\n\\n    /// Shows the world as it was at `target` (restore + re-simulate). The recorded future is\\n    /// kept until `commit_rewind`, so scrubbing forward again works.\\n    pub fn rewind_to(&mut self, target: u64) -> bool {\\n        let Some(snap) = self.history.snapshots.iter().rev().find(|s| s.tick <= target).cloned() else { return false };\\n        self.restore(snap);\\n        self.replaying = true;\\n850→        while self.state.tick < target {\\n            let Some(input) = self.history.input_at(self.state.tick) else { break };\\n            self.step_inner(&input);\\n        }\\n        self.replaying = false;\\n        true\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Starts a new timeline from the current tick (drops the recorded future).\\n    pub fn commit_rewind(&mut self) {\\n860→        let t = self.state.tick;\\n        self.history.truncate_after(t);\\n        // The replay recording restarts from scratch semantics: keep inputs up to `t`.\\n        let mut r = Replay { scene: self.recording.scene.clone(), seed: self.recording.seed, ..Default::default() };\\n        for (i, f) in self.recording.iter().enumerate() {\\n            if i as u64 >= t {\\n                break;\\n            }\\n            r.push(f);\\n        }\",\"content_concise\":\"820→                if b.is_dynamic() {\\n                    let m = b.mass();\\n                    b.apply_impulse(dir * push * fall * m, true);\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n        self.state.physics.wake_near(pos, reach + 4.0);\\n        events.push(SimEvent::Explosion { pos, radius });\\n    }\\n\\n830→    /// Runs `n` ticks with the same input.\\n    pub fn run(&mut self, n: u64, input: &InputFrame) {\\n        for _ in 0..n {\\n            self.step(input);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Takes the events produced since the last call.\\n    pub fn drain_events(&mut self) -> Vec<SimEvent> {\\n        std::mem::take(&mut self.events)\\n840→    }\\n\\n    // ------------------------------------------------------------------ time travel\\n\\n    /// Shows the world as it was at `target` (restore + re-simulate). The recorded future is\\n    /// kept until `commit_rewind`, so scrubbing forward again works.\\n    pub fn rewind_to(&mut self, target: u64) -> bool {\\n        let Some(snap) = self.history.snapshots.iter().rev().find(|s| s.tick <= target).cloned() else { return false };\\n        self.restore(snap);\\n        self.replaying = true;\\n850→        while self.state.tick < target {\\n            let Some(input) = self.history.input_at(self.state.tick) else { break };\\n            self.step_inner(&input);\\n        }\\n        self.replaying = false;\\n        true\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Starts a new timeline from the current tick (drops the recorded future).\\n    pub fn commit_rewind(&mut self) {\\n860→        let t = self.state.tick;\\n        self.history.truncate_after(t);\\n        // The replay recording restarts from scratch semantics: keep inputs up to `t`.\\n        let mut r = Replay { scene: self.recording.scene.clone(), seed: self.recording.seed, ..Default::default() };\\n        for (i, f) in self.recording.iter().enumerate() {\\n            if i as u64 >= t {\\n                break;\\n            }\\n            r.push(f);\\n        }\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/sim.rs\",\"offset\":820,\"limit\":50,\"raw_output\":\"                if b.is_dynamic() {\\n                    let m = b.mass();\\n                    b.apply_impulse(dir * push * fall * m, true);\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n        self.state.physics.wake_near(pos, reach + 4.0);\\n        events.push(SimEvent::Explosion { pos, radius });\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Runs `n` ticks with the same input.\\n    pub fn run(&mut self, n: u64, input: &InputFrame) {\\n        for _ in 0..n {\\n            self.step(input);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Takes the events produced since the last call.\\n    pub fn drain_events(&mut self) -> Vec<SimEvent> {\\n        std::mem::take(&mut self.events)\\n    }\\n\\n    // ------------------------------------------------------------------ time travel\\n\\n    /// Shows the world as it was at `target` (restore + re-simulate). The recorded future is\\n    /// kept until `commit_rewind`, so scrubbing forward again works.\\n    pub fn rewind_to(&mut self, target: u64) -> bool {\\n        let Some(snap) = self.history.snapshots.iter().rev().find(|s| s.tick <= target).cloned() else { return false };\\n        self.restore(snap);\\n        self.replaying = true;\\n        while self.state.tick < target {\\n            let Some(input) = self.history.input_at(self.state.tick) else { break };\\n            self.step_inner(&input);\\n        }\\n        self.replaying = false;\\n        true\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Starts a new timeline from the current tick (drops the recorded future).\\n    pub fn commit_rewind(&mut self) {\\n        let t = self.state.tick;\\n        self.history.truncate_after(t);\\n        // The replay recording restarts from scratch semantics: keep inputs up to `t`.\\n        let mut r = Replay { scene: self.recording.scene.clone(), seed: self.recording.seed, ..Default::default() };\\n        for (i, f) in self.recording.iter().enumerate() {\\n            if i as u64 >= t {\\n                break;\\n            }\\n            r.push(f);\\n        }\\n\",\"total_lines\":1187}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"7b3a91ca-5802-47f4-b955-bf66ea83d474"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_51","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"SimState will be cloned every 20 ticks to maintain history.\n\nSurgeryFrame will be defined in frame.rs and filled by mod.rs. RenderFrame will hold an optional Arc to SurgeryFrame. The take_frame function will live in games/surgery/mod.rs.\n\nSurgeryFrame will be public. It will hold mode, vitals, BPM, phase, flat, hazards, cursors, echo cursor, banner, callout, story line, camera params, gifts, fever, code, rank, score, and timer. sim.rs will call `crate::games::surgery::take_frame` to retrieve it.\n\nCameraShot will hold tilt, yaw, distance, FOV, target, follow lag, and height offset. Default values are set for the opening camera. Surgeon will derive Clone, Serialize, and Deserialize.\n\nSurgeon fields include position, tool, score, chain, best chain, mistakes, timer, meter, gift arm charge, input flags, cooldowns, name index, bot flag, focus target, and suture side. Checkpoint will store only scores and related data, not full surgeon states.\n\nLoading a case resets surgeons but preserves tune, difficulty, campaign flag, and accumulated coverage. Coverage accumulates across the entire campaign and only clears on a new campaign or a fresh single load from command. Campaign transitions keep coverage and unlocked gifts such as Hush persisting between cases.\n\nSkill persists across cases. Surgeon scores and mistakes reset each operation, so rank is per case; campaign score sums separately. last_rank and last_score will be stored for the results screen. Campaign tick flow moves through TITLE, STORY, CHART, and OP modes.\n\nCampaign results mode returns to story after confirmation. Single case results end immediately with the win outcome. Rank is computed before the outcome changes so the bot loop can detect it correctly. The bot holds confirm during story to advance lines automatically, and skip commands process all remaining lines in one tick.\n\nStory will use a `story_at: u16` line index to track progress. The full narrative spans roughly 200 lines across about 16 blocks. Clinical callouts are mapped to eight indices, covering routine checks, IV warnings, and code alerts. Surgeon names are fixed at Mara Quill, Pet Nwosu, and Bashir Haddad.\n\nSurgeon names now include Teddy Lark. Colors are #e8443a, #3b82f6, #22c55e, and #f5b700. Patient and operation name arrays are planned.\n\nThe eye phase ends with forceps rather than gel. A separate inflam will spawn for the gel stage after eye surgery.\n\nTool mappings are set: bone uses forceps rotate then suture; eye uses scalpel then forceps; ember uses cryo then scalpel then forceps; tumor uses scalpel circle then forceps then gel.\n\nA potential bug in apply() match ordering was spotted.\n\nThe suspected match-order bug is resolved. Rust or-pattern guards apply to the entire pattern, so `K_FOREIGN | K_TENDRIL | K_TUMOR if h.tool == FORCEPS` correctly matches all three kinds with forceps. Tumor stage 2 uses gel and matches paint; stage 0 uses scalpel and stage 1 uses forceps. Nodule and ember stage 0 tools are also confirmed correct.\n\napply() ordering is confirmed correct. The bot falls back to center for bone stage zero, gel paint, and laser targets.\n\nThe bot will derive suture cursor side from the tick count. Every five ticks the target flips to the opposite side. The suture tool detects the actual crossing of that side sign.\n\nA bug was found in the suture crossing detection. When the cursor crosses zero in a single tick, `prev.abs()` becomes zero and fails the `> 0.06` threshold, so the flip is missed. The check needs to handle the transition through zero.\n\nThe suture crossing bug is fixed by storing the last non-zero cursor side. The bot will only update its stored sign when the absolute side value exceeds 0.06, preventing a zero sample from breaking the detection.\n\nIncision quality stays good when the cursor sits exactly on the line. Tumor circle tracking leads the aim point by 0.28 units, and the target advances by 0.04 per tick as work increases.\n\nThe 0.28 rad lead on the tumor circle converts to roughly 0.18 units, which is reachable because work only advances when the cursor stays within 0.48 of the moving point.\n\nForceps targets the center while ungrabbed and work is below 0.05, then switches to the pull point once grabbed. The match condition uses `h.grabbed || (h.tool == FORCEPS && h.kind != K_TUMOR)` with correct operator precedence.\n\nThe forceps arm correctly targets the pull point once grabbed or work exceeds 0.05, and falls back to center otherwise. Tumor stage 1 and nodule stage 1 also route to the correct aim points before grab. Eye stage 2 will be checked next. The bot will decay its stored cursor side by hazard ID instead of adding an idle field.\n\nfinish() for intermediate stages sets hit.done = true even when the hazard is not fully cleared, though scoring per stage remains correct. advance_stage for nodule stage 1 requires 0.7 work for the forceps pull. Story text is embedded in Rust, so no std::fs calls are needed in the hot path. Strain drift will use a deterministic sine of tick instead of RNG to keep all seeds identical; slight position jitter will still come from sim.rng.\n\nBot code will not use RNG. Position jitter is applied only at spawn inside pre/tick so it becomes part of state evolution and replays stay identical. Checkpoints will store the jittered hazard positions. A field clamp will be added when spawning. The implementation will be pure Rust with no Python.\n\nDrafting mod.rs with serde defaults for the Surgery struct. All fields implement Serialize. The bot reads state from self rather than sim. A deterministic hash01 function is being added for noise.\n\nThe deterministic noise hash01 uses a 64-bit multiply-xor-shift with tick, slot, and salt to produce a 0..1 float. Skill 0 bot retargets tools every tick via tick % 11, so they never hold a laser and frequently pick the wrong tool. Cursor integration in pre is being drafted.\n\nBot cursor logic is now drafted. Surgeons are activated or disabled based on input flags, and their tool is set to the requested value. The bot computes an absolute aim point from the next stepped target, adds deterministic noise scaled by skill, and moves the cursor toward it at the tuned speed.\n\nThe bot step is capped at 3 to 15 times dt, matching the game's 0.25-unit slew rate so the cursor reaches the aim point exactly. A question remains about whether tool positions should use the true cursor position or the tremor-adjusted one.\n\nTremor offsets the tool position rather than accumulating into the cursor, so the cursor does not drift away. The bot will automatically hold PRECISION whenever skill exceeds 0.5, ensuring perfect aim at skill 1 and preventing missed sutures. The hush cursor slew also uses full dt instead of rules_dt.\n\nCursor movement uses full dt, while hazard and heart updates use rules_dt scaled by hush. This lets the cursor track in real time while the wound and laser fill degrade more slowly. The bot still releases at 0.90 aux, so the slower fill simply extends the operation. Hush lasts six beats of slowed heart.\n\nHush countdown starts at six beats scaled by gift duration. Echo lasts ten seconds of real time, not rules time. Gift activation is independent of tool use and does not release the current tool. The bot will only trigger gift when no gift is active, the meter is full, the unlock flag is set, and it is not in a code phase; it avoids firing during the first second.\n\nThe bot will track which gifts it has already used with a `gift_used` bitmask. It selects the highest unlocked gift that has not yet been triggered, so hush fires first in case 3 and echo takes over in case 4 once it unlocks.\n\nGift meters will be set to 100 immediately upon unlock so the bot can trigger them right away. Hush unlocks at case 3 split phase entry, echo at case 4 wave 2, and duet at case 7 soloist entry.\n\nDuet lasts eight seconds. The partner cursor is drawn on the same hazard and any completion counts double. Coverage marks duet on activation. Tendril pull requires both cursors near or the partner to assist.\n\nA simple partner bot will be added inside pre for case 7, joining slot 1 when it is inactive to help on a second hazard. This makes the case faster without softlocking if the gift fails.\n\nThe code defibrillator gesture is being drafted: while code_t is active, tools cannot work on hazards, and holding DEFIB or using it while USE charges defib_charge.\n\nA bug was found in the heart beat detection: during flatline, `since_r` keeps growing forever because it never resets, so `on_beat` becomes permanently false. This breaks the code defibrillator gesture that relies on ghost beats. A separate `ghost_on_beat` check using the raw ghost phase will be added instead.\n\nThe `ghost_on_beat` helper is being added to `heart.rs`. It detects R waves by checking whether the ghost phase sits near zero or wraps around. The defibrillator logic now requires a charge between 1.1 and 2.2 ticks, plus a ghost beat. The bot will hold the defib button until the charge reaches 1.2, then release on the next ghost beat.\n\nThe defibrillator charge rate is one tick per frame. At flatline the ghost period is roughly 0.4 seconds, so a 1.2-second charge takes about 72 ticks. The bot holds DEFIB while charging or when the ghost beat is missed, then releases once the charge reaches 1.2 and the beat aligns. If the charge hits 1.2 mid-beat, it keeps holding until the next ghost beat; the charge can then rise to 1.6 before release, which still satisfies the 2.2 cap.\n\nSkill below 0.5 causes the bot to release the defib charge immediately at 1.2, missing the ghost beat and failing the code. The `flatline` function now sets vitals to zero, marks the heart flat, starts the scripted code window, and records code coverage.\n\nThe scripted code path now decrements `codes_left` and re-enters flatline immediately if more shocks are required. A successful defibrillator charge restores vitals to 30, clears the flat state, and advances the shock counter. If the charge fails, the code window counts down until death. The `die()` function either revives the player with full vitals when immortal is set, or transitions into the DEAD mode.\n\nThe game outcome is set to LOST. A dev immortal flag prevents vitals from staying at zero. The code window pauses the timer so the challenge is purely skill-based. Checkpoints are saved only at phase starts and not during codes. Retry restores the last checkpoint, returns the mode to OP, and clears the flat state; dying before any checkpoint restarts the case.\n\nThe final score equals the sum of all surgeon scores plus a bonus: 20 points per vital, 5 points per second, minus 100 points per mistake. Par targets are set low so skill 1 can still reach ENCORE.\n\nPar targets will stay low so skill 1 can still reach ENCORE. \"Winning\" means the outcome is Won, not a specific rank. Two automated tests are planned: one that plays skill 1 and verifies the chain reaches at least five actions within eight seconds, and another that drives the simulation to confirm beat window detection works.\n\nThe beat-window test will use a short operation to record whether a completion triggers the on_beat bonus. A pool takes about one beat, so it may or may not land on the beat. The test asserts meter logic by completing during a known window. Poking public fields on Surgery is acceptable because it uses the shipped simulation state.\n\nThe beat-window test will step the shipped simulation and read the `on_beat` flag from the status JSON, rather than setting heart state directly. A rank test will also be added, either by calling `rank_of` on a computed score or by checking the final rank in the case status.\n\nThe rank test will use both a direct `rank_of` unit check and a campaign assertion that the final status rank is S or ENCORE. The bot will play real cases rather than using one-hit shortcuts, though tests can be optimized if they become too slow. Physics simulation still runs even in an empty scene, so benchmarking after compilation is planned. The focus point is set to (0, 0, 0.2), and the scene name is handled by `Sim::new`.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-97210713-0ca2-4e8e-b3c8-a1bec0ef0603-33","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/Cargo.toml","limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-97210713-0ca2-4e8e-b3c8-a1bec0ef0603-34","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"#!\\[deny|#!\\[warn","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/lib.rs"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7216,"output_tokens":7201,"cache_read_input_tokens":82176,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"e71b172f-7976-4725-b800-50b98e2033c9"}
