{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_60","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Writing content.rs, bot.rs, and mod.rs.\n\nFocusing on reliability and avoiding bugs like applying inactive tools or suture gestures failing during bot oscillation.\n\nCampaigns must finish quickly. The 240-second code blue limit will be kept, but the first priority is getting one case to win. The bot will use a spiral fallback if it gets stuck. I am also working out how to borrow state correctly in mod.rs and where to place the ops functions.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-10d2aa9f-6040-4a6e-b5ce-be9a69d7cd90-48","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/surgery/kinds.rs","offset":230,"limit":30}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":424,"output_tokens":586,"cache_read_input_tokens":121600,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"63df0a1a-1d74-4d2c-a063-db115f927e04"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-10d2aa9f-6040-4a6e-b5ce-be9a69d7cd90-48","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"230→pub const CUE_BACK: u16 = 26;\\npub const CUE_BANNER: u16 = 27;\\npub const CUE_FLAT: u16 = 28;\\npub const CUE_WRONG: u16 = 29;\\npub const CUE_FROST: u16 = 30;\\npub const CUE_VOICE: u16 = 31;\\npub const CUE_COUNT: u16 = 32;\\n\\npub const PLATES: [&str; 10] =\\n    [\\\"forearm\\\", \\\"abdomen\\\", \\\"bowel\\\", \\\"chest\\\", \\\"lung\\\", \\\"liver\\\", \\\"femur\\\", \\\"pericardium\\\", \\\"heart\\\", \\\"torso\\\"];\\n240→\\npub fn plate_index(name: &str) -> u8 {\\n    PLATES.iter().position(|s| *s == name).unwrap_or(0) as u8\\n}\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"230→pub const CUE_BACK: u16 = 26;\\npub const CUE_BANNER: u16 = 27;\\npub const CUE_FLAT: u16 = 28;\\npub const CUE_WRONG: u16 = 29;\\npub const CUE_FROST: u16 = 30;\\npub const CUE_VOICE: u16 = 31;\\npub const CUE_COUNT: u16 = 32;\\n\\npub const PLATES: [&str; 10] =\\n    [\\\"forearm\\\", \\\"abdomen\\\", \\\"bowel\\\", \\\"chest\\\", \\\"lung\\\", \\\"liver\\\", \\\"femur\\\", \\\"pericardium\\\", \\\"heart\\\", \\\"torso\\\"];\\n240→\\npub fn plate_index(name: &str) -> u8 {\\n    PLATES.iter().position(|s| *s == name).unwrap_or(0) as u8\\n}\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/surgery/kinds.rs\",\"offset\":230,\"limit\":30,\"raw_output\":\"pub const CUE_BACK: u16 = 26;\\npub const CUE_BANNER: u16 = 27;\\npub const CUE_FLAT: u16 = 28;\\npub const CUE_WRONG: u16 = 29;\\npub const CUE_FROST: u16 = 30;\\npub const CUE_VOICE: u16 = 31;\\npub const CUE_COUNT: u16 = 32;\\n\\npub const PLATES: [&str; 10] =\\n    [\\\"forearm\\\", \\\"abdomen\\\", \\\"bowel\\\", \\\"chest\\\", \\\"lung\\\", \\\"liver\\\", \\\"femur\\\", \\\"pericardium\\\", \\\"heart\\\", \\\"torso\\\"];\\n\\npub fn plate_index(name: &str) -> u8 {\\n    PLATES.iter().position(|s| *s == name).unwrap_or(0) as u8\\n}\\n\",\"total_lines\":243}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"9577ab0e-4b76-49ce-bfce-4cc84a8645c2"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_61","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I will write content.rs, bot.rs, and the simulation.\n\nI will write the Rust files directly rather than using a generator. content.rs will be comprehensive and long. Then I will write bot.rs, mod.rs, and ops.rs. The critical path for ops.rs covers cursor integration, tool application, draining, heart logic, phases, code, gifts, and win/lose conditions.\n\nI rejected the idea of assisted completion for the bot. The simulation must remain fair and skill-based. I will make suture slightly more forgiving and ensure the bot's zigzag motion is correct. Alternatively, I could program the bot to compute exact crossing points and hold on each side.\n\nThe bot will use a stateful suture routine. It moves to the current side, waits until the cursor is within 0.15 distance, then moves to the opposite side. The game updates the side flag when the cursor arrives. This replaces the earlier zigzag approach.\n\nThe game will flip the suture side flag when the cursor is within 0.12 of the aim point. The bot then targets the opposite side on the next tick. This stored gesture phase is invisible to the human player, so it does not constitute cheating.\n\nThe suture side will flip every 8 ticks while the tool is active and near the hazard. This gives the bot roughly two units of travel to reach each side before switching.\n\nThe suture timing checks out: the cursor travels one unit in four ticks, dwell for four more, cross over in four, then dwell again. The bot will toggle suture side with a simple `(tick / 8) % 2` expression instead of extra state.\n\nFor the bone tool, the bot will rotate until the auxiliary angle drops below twelve, then hold. For the laser, it will hold until the auxiliary value reaches the release threshold, release for one tick, and avoid re-holding immediately.\n\nThe laser release logic is verified: the auxiliary value resets to zero after a successful hit, so the bot will not immediately re-hold. Stage advancement also zeroes auxiliary values, which is correct for tool transitions. The IV tool follows the same pattern. Gel will use a circular motion.\n\nThe bot will special-case the scanner tool. It holds at the center while a hidden hazard is being revealed.\n\nFor multiple hazards, the bot selects the highest drain among alive non-heat targets. If a hidden hazard must be revealed first, it uses the scanner before treating other tools. A visible bleeder takes priority over a hidden foreign hazard.\n\nThe bot will break ties by choosing the hazard with the lower ID. The partner will avoid that same target and select the next best option.\n\nIn code mode, the bot ignores all hazards and focuses on defibrillation when the heart is flat.\n\nGifts are only attempted when the meter is full, an unused gift is unlocked, the heart is not flat, and the skill level exceeds 0.5.\n\nChart and story modes are handled in the bot. Attract mode mirrors the operator logic. Title and dead states require no input; the run stops at death because the outcome is lost. Results simply confirm.\n\nA single case will immediately set the outcome to Won and enter results mode so the bot can confirm without waiting. Campaign mode keeps the outcome as playing, confirms the results, shows the story, then advances to the next case. The finish_op function computes the score, sets the rank, switches to results mode, and marks victory for non-campaign runs.\n\nCampaign logic is being refined. After the final case's after-story, the next confirmation ends the campaign. Otherwise the bot advances to the next case and enters its before-story.\n\nThe campaign flow is being split into two phases. `prepare_case` will reset operation parameters without changing the mode, and `begin_case_now` will set the mode to story. This prevents the after-story from accidentally switching back to operation mode.\n\nCampaign mode initializes case zero and enters story mode. Story blocks advance one line per tick on confirmation, with a skip command to jump ahead. Chart mode returns to operation mode when confirmed.\n\nEmpty story blocks will advance immediately to avoid getting stuck. Code blue is a separate mode, not part of the campaign; the campaign covers cases zero through seven and includes code coverage in cases two and seven.\n\nI estimated the simulation workload. Ten seeds per case across fourteen thousand ticks each would total roughly one to two minutes at a 0.2 millisecond step, or ten to twenty minutes at two milliseconds. I will optimize physics later if needed. I also need to initialize history_seconds to zero in the build function and verify whether the step function panics when no player is present.\n\nI verified that game_pre handles a missing game gracefully. I am now writing the files and will keep the operation logic correct. I will add the RenderFrame field before compiling so all sites are accounted for. Surgeon colors are defined as four RGB triplets. The field bounds are clamped to X ±9.0 and Z ±4.2. SurgeonInput defaults to tool zero.\n\nThe integration loop now handles absolute aim and stick input. It clamps the tool index to ten, snaps the cursor position when the snap flag is set, and applies slew smoothing for relative aiming.\n\nBot movement is capped by a step size that keeps the cursor within the game's slew range. Tremor is applied only to the working point, not the bot's aim target, which it uses directly. The bot holds PRECISION when skill reaches 0.45 or higher, disabling tremor.\n\nTremor is applied only to the local lead in slot 0, since the Pet assistant braces those hands. The cursor uses full delta time while hazards use a scaled rules delta. During Hush, heart ticks and R-wave events decrement the hush counter; the counter starts at six times the gift duration rounded up to one.\n\nGift state fields are being defined: hush_beats, echo_left, duet_left, echo_x, echo_z, echo_cursor_on, last_tool, last_rating, last_id, and echo_cool. Echo and duet timers decrement by real delta time each tick. When echo is active and its cooldown has expired, the bot will auto-clear a matching non-heat hazard that is not the player's current grab target, while still counting that coverage.\n\nThe bot will initialize last_rating to IMMACULATE after its first successful action so echo can replicate a good rating. Echo may clear hazards before the player finishes using the tool, but gesture coverage is tracked per tool use and still increments correctly.\n\nEcho clearing a hazard counts as the tool being exercised, so the campaign requirement is satisfied. The bot still performs the real gestures at least once before echo unlocks. A hazard cleared instantly by echo still counts toward phase completion.\n\nHush scales the heart tick interval by the gift duration, so six beats last about fourteen seconds in wall time. The bot still completes the phase, just more slowly.\n\nHush also slows tool charging and case timers, so the patient survives. Drain uses the scaled delta time too. Strain speed and toughness are baked into new hazard needs at spawn; mid-phase slider changes will not affect existing hazards. A successful one-hit application kills the hazard instantly with an immaculate rating.\n\nThe revealed timer decrements by scaled rules delta and does not re-hide a hazard. Sac swelling will be handled in the orchestrator so it still occurs when no surgeon targets the sac.\n\nI will remove the sac swell logic from `apply` in `hazard.rs` so it only happens once in the orchestrator. The orchestrator will also handle heat sweep and nodule wander, tagging wander-phase spawns with tag 1.\n\nThe `enter_phase` function clears all existing hazards before spawning the new phase's set. It applies per-phase drain and axis overrides, sets the hidden flag, and scales each hazard's need by the strain-toughness multiplier.\n\nSpawn positions receive a small random jitter and are clamped to the arena bounds. Each hazard is assigned a unique ID. When a phase has zero strain, its tag is set to one so it wanders. The orchestrator saves a checkpoint if the phase is marked checkpoint or if it is the first phase. It also increments the corresponding strain coverage counter.\n\nThe phase entry logic now sets the active strain, plate, and heat values. It unlocks gifts and fills the lead meter so they can be fired immediately. Script codes trigger shock sequences. The phase is marked ready and a two-second callout banner is scheduled.\n\nA pending script triggers code mode once the heart is stable. Phase completion requires no flat heart, all required shocks to be delivered, and every non-heat hazard to be cleared.\n\nScript-only phases will not finish immediately because the shock counter must still reach zero. Entering a phase that starts a script on the same tick leaves the heart flat, so phase completion is blocked until the shocks finish and the heart recovers. Empty phases are avoided by requiring a shock count in every script phase. When the phase index exceeds the case length, `finish_op` is called to end the operation. Code blue is handled as a separate mode with its own tick loop and no phase system.\n\nCode blue ticks advance a 24-second wave timer and spawn bleeders with high drain to pressure the idle patient. When the case timer drops to thirty seconds, a small finale spawns two nodules. The operation ends once the timer expires, vitals remain above zero, and the heart is not flat.\n\nThe operation timer decrements by scaled rules delta divided by the timer tune. It pauses while the heart is flat, so a code-blue case can survive past its target four minutes if the patient remains alive. The hub expects a four-minute runtime.\n\nThe operation timer will no longer pause during code blue. When vitals reach zero, the system checks for an immortal flag first; if absent, it triggers a code sequence. A non-scripted phase enters code mode with a full vitals reset and a flat heart. Scripted phases enter code mode with zero vitals and a flat heart. Each code attempt consumes one of the limited code windows.\n\nScripted code mode sets the vitals to zero, flattens the heart, and starts a five-second real-time window. The code timer decrements by wall delta time so hush cannot slow it. If the window expires, the operation dies.\n\nDefibrillation restores thirty vitals and clears the flat state. It also resets the charge meter. When the defib succeeds during a scripted phase, the system increments the shock counter and immediately re-enters code mode if more shocks are still required.\n\nThe checkpoint is cloned before restoration to avoid borrow issues. Retrying resets the mode to OP, sets the outcome to Playing, clears the flat heart state, and removes any active code timer. When the immortal flag is active, dying restores forty vitals, clears the flat state, and removes the code timer.\n\nDie sets the mode to DEAD, outcome to LOST, and flattens the heart. The command handler applies Retry, Skip, SetTune, ResetTune, LoadCase, LoadCampaign, and LoadCode.\n\nThe command handler also processes SetDifficulty, Dev, and SpawnHazard. Difficulty is clamped to two and resets the available code windows. Dev toggles flags like immortal or one-hit, freezes the timer, unlocks all gifts, forces a code, kills the patient, wins the operation, or jumps phases; extra is ignored for toggles except jump. SpawnHazard places a hazard at the arena center plus a spiral offset. A rating test can spawn a polyp and exercise it.\n\nCoverage persists across campaign cases. Scores reset at the start of each operation. Elapsed time will be tracked. Total score sums all surgeon scores and adds a bonus based on vitals, remaining timer, and mistakes.\n\nChain is tracked per surgeon, but the global best chain is the maximum across all surgeons. Rank is computed from total score against a par target. Par is set to 1200 so the bonus alone guarantees at least an S rating; a skill 1 case with thin vitals and time still clears this threshold.\n\nPar is set to 700 so every case clears at least an ENCORE. Mistakes are counted for wrong-tool usage and overheat. A healthy-tissue bonus requires holding a scalpel with no hazard within 1.2 units while not engaged. The bot releases the scalpel before moving between cuts to avoid one-frame wrong-tool penalties.\n\nThe bot will set the correct tool before holding use and approach without using. A global IV cooldown is added to each surgeon. Idle decay subtracts 60 percent of work when idle exceeds 0.3 seconds, but it skips bone angle, sac swell, and heat. Suture samples reset after one second of idle time. Laser auxiliary decay requires the apply function to run.\n\nHeat tool auxiliary decay will run only in the orchestrator for unengaged hazards, avoiding double decay with the apply function. Surgeons pick targets and call apply when using or releasing tools. Unengaged hazards then decay outside the apply loop. Forceps work resets on release inside apply.\n\nSurgeons apply tools sequentially. When releasing a tool, the system targets the hazard with the matching focus ID if it is alive; otherwise it selects the nearest valid hazard. Wrong-tool detection scans for any nearby hazard within radius. The nearest-hazard helper filters out dead and heat hazards, then picks the closest alive target by distance.\n\nSurgeons will only call apply when the target is within radius plus 0.45 units, or if the tool is IV, or if they are releasing. The scanner will also scan within radius plus 0.35 units. When a hit finishes, the system increments the tool and rating counters in coverage, then updates scores, chain, meter, and hazard stats.\n\nStage advancement only counts a hazard once it is dead. Overheat and pool hits mark the hazard dead and count it with a sloppy rating. IV hits mark the hazard dead unless the rating is sloppy, then count it. Echo clear also marks the hazard dead.\n\nCoverage counters for tools, ratings, and hazards are updated after each hit. Heat sweep end increments the heat hazard count once without spawning a new hazard to avoid double counting. Gift activation maps HUSH, ECHO, and DUET to coverage indices 0, 1, and 2.\n\nGift activation increments coverage for the activated gift, marks it used and unlocked, sets its duration from the tune multiplier, and resets the first surgeon's meter. A helper function will select which gift triggers next.\n\nThe gift selector prioritizes DUET, then ECHO, then HUSH. The bot will trigger a gift only if the next gift is available, its meter is full, skill is at least 0.6, and the heart is not flat.\n\nThe gift selector prioritizes DUET over ECHO, so if echo never triggered in case 4, case 7 would skip it entirely. The bot can also fill its meter before the phase that unlocks a gift, but next_gift returns zero until that gift is actually available.\n\nA `recent_unlock` field will track the most recently unlocked gift. The bot will prefer that gift when it is unused, rather than always selecting the lowest-numbered one. This prevents case 7 from skipping duet if echo was never triggered.\n\nThe gift selector now returns the most recently unlocked gift if it is unused. In case 7, the partner bot runs in slot 1 and avoids focusing on the lead surgeon's target to prevent stealing the tendril during diastole. Local joins are processed for any active surgeon slot, and the player count used for code-blue scaling is captured at wave spawn time.\n\nDifficulty scales the timer and available code windows. The timer shrinks by 15 percent on medium and 30 percent on hard. Code windows drop to one on medium and zero on hard. Drain is multiplied by a difficulty factor. Fever is set to 1.0 during heat phases for the view. Otherwise it equals 30 percent of the vitals deficit.\n\nPlate blending advances by one-eighth of a second per frame and locks to the next plate once complete. Callout and banner timers decay each frame. Vitals below forty trigger a one-time IV push warning. Popups store rating, life, and position data and fade out over time. A status JSON endpoint will expose all operation state for tests. The HUD displays the patient name, operation number, vitals, and heart rate.\n\nThe HUD now shows score, rank name, and elapsed time. A clock helper formats the timer as MM:SS. Mode names and a story line will be included in the status output for the shell later. A public `take_frame` function will build a `SurgeryFrame` snapshot for mod.rs. Checkpoint and Surgery are made serializable via serde, with popups stored as f32 arrays and banner as an index; strings are excluded. The `was_use` flag is updated at the end of each surgeon's processing.\n\nGift arm timing is now implemented. The bot holds the gift button for 0.12 seconds during the heart beat window, then releases it to trigger the next available gift if the meter is full and the heart is not flat. The bot sets the gift input when it wants to fire and the heart is off beat, or when it just fired; otherwise it releases. The initial `was_gift` state is false.\n\nDefib charging now requires holding both DEFIB and USE. During a flat heart, the bot charges for 1.12 to 2.5 seconds before releasing to resuscitate. The bot releases at 1.2 seconds if its skill is below 0.35, otherwise it waits for the heart beat.\n\nThe bot holds the defib button until the ghost heart beats, then releases to resuscitate. A skill-zero bot releases at 1.2 seconds and fails. The `was_defib` flag is visible to the bot. Hazards are not processed while the heart is flat. Resuscitation cues a thump. Code start cues flat and alarm. Every R-wave cues lub.\n\nRating cues fire when a hit finishes. Chain cues trigger at 5, 10, and 20 hits. Banner cues fire on phase start. Voice cues advance the story line with pitch from the speaker. Random jitter is only used during enter_phase inside pre, so replays stay deterministic. Checkpoints save after spawn so retries include hazards; restore skips respawning.\n\nPhase advancement only triggers when phase_ready is false. On restore, that flag stays true so enter_phase is skipped. The first operation tick enters the phase and saves a checkpoint; an immediate death lets the retry restore from that saved state. A dev command can jump phases by clearing hazards, incrementing phase_i, and resetting phase_ready. Another dev command finishes the operation with a win outcome. The bot will spiral around its target when focus_t exceeds 2.0 by adding a perpendicular offset to its aim.\n\nThe bot spiral applies a cosine and sine offset scaled by 0.4 to the aim point when focus_t exceeds 2.0 seconds, which unsticks the scanner but may score sloppy. Gel quadrants are visited by circling with angle tick * 0.35 and radius 0.45 times the hazard radius, completing the pattern within roughly one second. Tumor tracking uses aim_point, and focus_t resets whenever work increases during tracking.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-f764815b-9a8a-4ac3-bd83-7ad329f89363-49","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"tick_rate|enum Tick|hz\\(","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src","glob":"*.rs","head_limit":30}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":824,"output_tokens":10759,"cache_read_input_tokens":121984,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"15da189c-174d-48d6-af8e-9afe1a39e44f"}
