{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_80","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The test finished in 0.\n\nThe bot died after 51 seconds. It used the defibrillator twice and ran out of codes. No hazards were completed.\n\nThe defibrillator works. The bot never engaged any hazard. Tool gestures are broken.\n\nThe bot's movement and tool selection logic looks correct. The cursor starts at 0,0, so it may be failing to reach the hazard.\n\nThe nearest hazard is about 1.4 units away, so the bot should reach it. The zero ratings confirm the suture gesture never finished. The death timing matches a 48-second drain with no damage reduction, meaning the bot never actually engaged the hazard.\n\nA focus_t spiral may be preventing suture completion. If the suture gesture never increases its work counter, focus_t grows past 2.2 seconds and triggers a spiral that disrupts the stitching path. This would explain why the bot holds the tool but never finishes the hazard.\n\nA temporary debug test will step 180 ticks and print the surgeon's position, active tool, and hazard work or samples. This should reveal whether the bot is actually near the hazard or if the suture gesture is not registering any progress.\n\nThe debug test will read the Surgery struct directly from the simulation state. It will print the surgeon's position, active tool, and every hazard's work, samples, and alive status. The bot's aim step may conflict with the game's cursor slew.\n\nThe cursor slews at the same speed as the bot's aim step, so they should meet. The suture aim point is calculated from the laceration's axis and side vector. The bot starts at 0,0.\n\nThe suture gesture logic checks out. The bot sets the correct tool and holds use once it is near the aim point, so the hold condition should be satisfied. The game selects the nearest hazard to the cursor rather than the aim target.\n\nThe suture gesture may be stuck in an infinite loop. If the zigzag path never crosses the laceration's midline near the needle, the sample counter stays at zero and the hazard never finishes. The focus_t spiral would then prevent any completion after two seconds. I will run a quick debug test to verify the bot's exact position.\n\nThe bot prioritizes the laceration because its drain rate is higher than the pool's. This explains why both hazards remain alive. The suture gesture may be too fast for the game to register crossings. I am now simulating the exact zigzag path to verify whether the cursor dwell and side-flip timing actually triggers sample increments.\n\nThe suture zigzag math checks out. The cursor should cross the laceration midline and trigger a sample increment. Skill 1 correctly enables PRECISION, so tremor is not blocking the gesture.\n\nThe movement step logic is correct. The bot's decision path from run_bot through bot_input to Surgery::bot is intact. I am now checking whether the bot's tool selection is broken, possibly defaulting to the scalpel instead of suture.\n\nThe bot sees no hazards on tick 0 because the phase spawns them after the bot runs. It should see them from tick 1 onward. I will dump the simulation state after three seconds to verify what the bot actually sees. I also suspect the bot might be reading a different copy of the game state through serde or the game box.\n\nThe bot reads the same game instance as the simulation, so stale state is not the issue. A new hypothesis is that the bot computes its hold condition using the raw aim target, but the game later step-limits that aim point toward the surgeon. This would make the distance check fail and prevent the tool from being held.\n\nI traced the `decide()` function and found that `aim_toward` takes the target coordinates by value, so it cannot modify the caller's local variables. The bot therefore computes its hold condition using the raw ideal aim point rather than the game's step-limited position.\n\nThe hold condition is confirmed to use the raw ideal aim point, not the game's step-limited cursor position. The bot therefore cannot hold the suture tool because `h.grabbed` is false and the active tool is not IV.\n\nThe hold logic survives the side-flip jump because `was_use` keeps the tool held when distance is below 1.55. I will add debug prints to verify whether `apply` actually receives `use_held`. I also spotted a potential bug where `hands()` reads the tool from the surgeon state after it was set from the input struct.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-85b3f148-adae-47b6-a5c2-6458d63e9e94-78","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/surgery/hazard.rs","offset":510,"limit":70}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1321,"output_tokens":3231,"cache_read_input_tokens":198144,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"88f29b0e-fdc5-4831-876d-4c70a7c18e2c"}
