{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_101","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Cases 0, 1, 2, 3, 7, and Code Blue WIN at ENCORE.\n\nCase 4 Lacrimosa failed at phase 3. All 18 sacs were cleared, but the eye and inflam phase died with 2 alive, 46 mistakes, and a \"Wrong instrument\" error.\n\nThe eye phase required two scalpel stages and one forceps stage. Only two scalpel tools were used, and no forceps was ever applied. The failure likely occurred during the forceps pull.\n\nThe bot repeatedly selected the wrong tool, causing the 46 mistakes. The forceps pull logic for the eye phase was examined: it targets eye, tendril, nodule, and ember nodes with forceps, grabs when near, and pulls once work exceeds 0.05.\n\nThe pull mechanics were traced. After two scalpel stages, the eye need is 1.4 and the pull distance is 1.55. The bot should hold USE while moving toward the grab point at 0.25 per tick. Forceps work equals the cursor's projection onto the axis. The pull should finish once the cursor reaches the target. The suspicion is that something resets the grab before completion.\n\nThe bot may be targeting the inflam node instead of the eye due to focus spiral. The 46 wrong instrument mistakes likely stem from holding scalpel on the eye or forceps near the inflam gel. The grab radius is 0.5 with cursor tremor disabled.\n\nThe wrong-tool penalty only triggers when the bot is holding a tool and aiming at a node that does not accept it. If the bot grabs the eye with forceps, no penalty applies. The 46 mistakes likely come from the cursor passing near the inflam gel while holding forceps, since the inflam rejects that tool.\n\nThe 46 mistakes average about one per second, matching the 0.8-second wrong-tool cooldown. The bot likely switches selection to the inflam node while pulling forceps on the eye, since apply only acts on the selected hazard. This causes repeated wrong-tool penalties and prevents the eye from being cleared.\n\nThe bot always selects the eye node because its drain is higher than the inflam's. If the eye is grabbed, USE stays held and the pull continues. The suspicion shifts to whether the grab actually succeeds. If the grab fails, the bot aims at the center but cannot reach the working point, so it never begins pulling.\n\nThe eye's pull axis is 0.0, so the cursor moves along +X. The bot may briefly target the inflam gel while pulling on the eye, but the eye only receives updates when selected.\n\nThe eye pull target sits at roughly (1.55, 0.15). The inflam node is at (1.75, -0.35). The cursor path passes within the inflam's radius while pulling the eye. The bot selects the inflam because it is closer to the cursor. Forceps on the inflam triggers a wrong-tool penalty every 0.8 seconds. The eye phase stalls because the bot cannot operate it while the inflam is selected.\n\nThe inflam node remains closer to the cursor even at the eye's pull target. The bot therefore never returns to the eye, so the forceps pull never completes and the wrong-tool penalty continues until the patient dies.\n\nThe fix is to make selection prefer the grabbed hazard while USE is held. This prevents the cursor from switching to a closer node that rejects the current tool, which would otherwise stall the forceps pull. The same logic should apply to suture so the focus stays locked during the gesture.\n\nThe fix will extend the existing `just_release` rule. When USE is held and the cursor is within a generous working distance of the focused hazard, selection should stay locked to that hazard instead of snapping to the nearest node. This prevents the bot from latching onto a neighbor like the inflam gel while pulling forceps on the eye.\n\nThe implementation will add a `use_held` parameter to `select`. When USE is held, the function will prefer the focused hazard if it is alive, grabbed, or accepts the current tool, and only switch to a closer node if that hazard is more than 2.2 units away. This prevents the cursor from snapping to a neighbor like the inflam gel while pulling forceps on the eye, and it also keeps suture locked during gestures.\n\nCase 6 failed at phase 0. The bot cleared the laceration with suture and started one stage of the tumor with scalpel, but the tumor never died because its hazard count dropped to zero. Two hazards remained alive and the patient died. The run logged 43 mistakes and a \"Wrong instrument\" error.\n\nCase 6 cleared the laceration first because its drain is highest. The bot then circled the tumor, but the pool hazard count was zero so it never cleared. The 43 wrong-tool mistakes likely stem from the same sticky-focus bug.\n\nThe suture zigzag is unlikely to hit the tumor. After clearing the laceration, the bot selected the tumor because its drain is higher than the pool's. The first scalpel stage completed successfully. The second forceps stage pulls along the +X axis from the tumor center toward the pool at (1.65, 0.95). The pull distance is only about 0.95 units, so the cursor may never reach the pool.\n\nThe pool is roughly 1.1 units from the tumor pull endpoint, so it likely does not block the cursor path. The 43 wrong-tool mistakes in Case 6 probably stem from the bot failing to switch to forceps after the scalpel stage completes, rather than from a neighbor node. The tool count shows only one scalpel and one suture, confirming forceps was never applied to the tumor.\n\nThe forceps pull logic for the tumor is identical to the eye. It targets the center, grabs, then pulls toward the endpoint. The pool at (1.65, 0.95) is roughly 1.1 units away from the pull target at (0.95, 0.1), so it should not block the cursor path.\n\nThe focus spiral is the real culprit. Tumor stage 0's circular work loop resets focus_t when work nears TAU, so the bot loses its grip on the hazard before forceps can finish stage 1. Stage 1 work resets to zero on advance, but the focus_t spiral still interrupts the pull.\n\nThe focus spiral is not the culprit. The tool updates on the hazard immediately, so the bot reads the new forceps state on the next tick. The real issue may be the match arm order in the apply logic, which could prevent the forceps pull from starting after the scalpel stage completes.\n\nThe match arm order is not the problem; forceps correctly targets stage 1 of the tumor. The 43 wrong-tool mistakes likely come from a sync bug where the bot still holds scalpel after the stage advances, causing it to use the wrong instrument on the pool or the next hazard.\n\nThe wrong-tool penalty only triggers when the held tool mismatches the target hazard and the cursor is close. If forceps is already held at the tumor center, grab succeeds and engagement blocks penalties. After grabbing, pulling also stays engaged while USE is held. The suspicion shifts to whether the surgeon's tool actually updates from scalpel to forceps after the stage advances. The bot sets inp.tool to h.tool every tick, so it should switch immediately.\n\nA frame-delay theory is being considered: the bot may briefly hold scalpel while the tumor hazard already expects forceps. To break the cycle of guesswork, debug probes will be added for cases 4, 5, and 6 to log cursor position, held tool, focus state, and every hazard's kind, stage, tool, work, grabbed status, alive flag, and mistakes every 30 ticks. Case 5 Dies Irae shows five embers, nine finished heat sweeps, zero cleared embers, two immaculate ratings, five alive hazards, and ten mistakes.\n\nCase 5 is actually a heat-scoring failure. The \"Lift off!\" callout means the bot is being scorched by heat, not that it is making wrong-tool mistakes. One ember reached its first two stages but stalled on forceps while the other four remain untouched. The ten mistakes likely come from heat damage rather than instrument errors.\n\nThe bot died from heat and drain damage, not wrong-tool mistakes. Heat hits deal -8 vitals each while USE is held inside the beam, so releasing before the beam strike prevents damage. The ten mistakes are heat damage, not instrument errors.\n\nA one-tick delay in the heat system is now suspected. The bot evaluates its position and tool choice using the previous heat angle, but the angle advances before the hands check runs. This means the bot may decide to hold USE while the beam is still 0.22 radians away, then release after the angle update, causing a missed hit and extra damage.\n\nThe heat sweep delay is confirmed. The beam covers roughly five ticks, so the bot releases one tick late and takes one -8 damage hit per sweep. Ten sweeps total about 80 damage, plus drain and resuscitations, which matches the final vitals.\n\nCase 5 is stuck, not merely scorched. The bot releases cryo before the beam strike, which resets the charge to zero and interrupts the ember stage. Because the heat sweep repeats every three seconds but cryo takes only half a second, the charge should finish between sweeps unless the bot is not staying on the ember.\n\nThe \"Lift off\" callout means the bot is holding USE inside the heat beam. The heat danger and hit angle formulas are identical, so a mismatch is unlikely. The bot probably releases cryo before the beam strikes because it is sitting on an ember that lies within the sweep radius.\n\nThe focus spiral is the real culprit for charge tools. During laser or cryo charging, work stays flat while auxiliary energy grows. This pushes focus_t higher and drags the cursor off the ember before the charge completes. The bot never holds long enough to reach 0.90 auxiliary energy.\n\nThe focus spiral is not the culprit. The aim offset stays within the 0.5 radius, so the cursor never leaves the ember. Only one cryo stage completed in 34 seconds. The real cause is likely the bot releasing during stage 0 heat.\n\nThe cryo charge math shows it should finish in about 0.85 seconds, well inside the 3-second heat sweep window, so repeated interruptions are unlikely. Only one ember stage completed in 34 seconds, which points to a deeper bug. Stage 1 scalpel hold lasts roughly one second.\n\nThe bot is stuck on the first ember's forceps stage. It selects the highest drain hazard, which is the first ember, then attempts forceps while the others keep draining. Nearby embers may be triggering selection switches that prevent the pull from applying.\n\nThe first ember's forceps pull endpoint sits roughly 0.7 units from the second ember, inside the 0.5 radius, so the cursor may graze a neighbor and break the grab. Another possibility is that heat interrupts the pull by releasing USE, which drops the grab and resets work.\n\nThe bot will be modified to keep the grab active across brief heat releases and sticky-select neighbors. A debug dump is planned to inspect the stuck state. Case 4 is now being examined, where Phase 3 is the eye.\n\nSacs completed successfully with 18 laser hits, confirming the auxiliary fix worked. The eye phase is now the failure point: two scalpel completions but zero forceps, leaving the bot stuck on eye forceps while inflammation triggers wrong-tool spam. Case 6 shows one scalpel done and one suture laceration done, yet the tumor forceps and pool remain unfinished; the 43 wrong-tool mistakes likely stem from selection stealing during the pull or gel-versus-forceps confusion.\n\nSticky select will be extended to the focused hazard while USE is held, keeping the cursor on the target within a leash. A short debug probe on cases 5 and 6 will run before further changes.\n\nA debug test will log state whenever mistakes increase or every 60 ticks for the first 400 ticks. The focus is now on case 6: the circle stage finished, so the failure must occur during forceps. The suspicion is that the circle's finish condition might fire repeatedly and skip stage advancement, or that advance_stage is misfiring after the circle completes.\n\nThe circle stage finishes once and advances to forceps, so the stage jump is not the bug. The suspicion now shifts to whether the hazard's tool field is actually set to FORCEPS when the bot arrives, since the aim logic falls back to the center point if it does not match. A focused debug test will verify this.\n\nThe grab radius is 0.55, so the center point is well inside the range. The pool is not being selected by mistake because the bot always picks the highest-drain hazard. The real issue in case 6 may be that after suturing the laceration, the suture zigzag leaves the cursor far away from the tumor, which then breaks the grab or forceps pull.\n\nThe forceps pull endpoint is 1.10 units from the pool, safely outside the 0.75-unit radius. The path does not graze the pool, so wrong-tool penalties from the pool are ruled out.\n\nThe pool is ruled out as the cause. The forceps pull endpoint sits 0.85 units from the pool, safely outside its radius, so the cursor stays on the tumor and should not trigger wrong-tool penalties. The bot is stuck on the tumor forceps stage for over forty seconds, which points to a deeper bug in the forceps match guard or stage logic.\n\nThe forceps match guard is confirmed correct. The suspicion now shifts to `advance_stage`: a bug in its ordering might leave the hazard's tool field as GEL instead of FORCEPS when stage 1 begins, which would cause the forceps function to abort immediately.\n\nadvance_stage is confirmed correct: it sets the tool to FORCEPS before stage 1 begins. Tumor hold logic also looks sound. The real cause of the stall remains unknown, so a targeted probe is needed. A new suspicion is that cursor spiral behavior combined with suture-like movement might be disrupting the grab.\n\nWork decay only runs when idle exceeds 0.4 seconds. Forceps keeps idle at zero while pulling, so work should not decay. If the bot releases USE or switches selection, idle grows and work drops toward zero. This could explain why case 6 stalls on forceps.\n\nCase 4's eye forceps pull overlaps the inflammation hazard, confirming sticky select will fix it. Case 6 is suspected to be a different bug. A temporary debug test will print tool, stage, and mistake changes for case 6, plus ember stages and auxiliary energy for case 5. The aim logic for grabbed tumors is also being checked to see if the center-point fallback is interfering with the grab.\n\nThe grabbed-tumor aim logic is confirmed correct: the grab guard fires and the pull target is set properly. The probe will write a compact trace to stdout. Once forceps works, gel paint for the tumor and eye phases still need review.\n\nCase 5's cryo tool is confirmed correct, so the bot holds CRYO and completes one stage. The next step is to fix the heat reset: if the heat beam strikes during the four-tick forceps pull, it resets work and breaks the grab.\n\nThe heat beam is too brief to explain a 34-second stall, so the bot likely never grabs the ember at all. Nearby embers do not overlap enough to steal selection. A new possibility is that heat_tool overheat kills the ember by setting alive=false, which would decrement the alive count.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a55edfc5-3f0f-49a5-8471-625f86defad8-39","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/tests/surgery.rs","old_string":"#[test]\nfn survey_skill1() {","new_string":"#[test]\nfn probe_stuck() {\n    for case in [4u8, 5, 6] {\n        let mut sim = Sim::new(&format!(\"surgery/case/{case}\"), 1).unwrap();\n        let mut last_m = 0u32;\n        let mut last_phase = 99u8;\n        eprintln!(\"--- case {case}\");\n        for _ in 0..60 * 80 {\n            if sim.outcome() != pav_core::games::Outcome::Playing {\n                break;\n            }\n            let f = sim.bot_input();\n            sim.step(&f);\n            let g = sim.play().unwrap().get::<pav_core::games::surgery::Surgery>().unwrap();\n            let interesting = g.mistakes != last_m || g.phase_i != last_phase || sim.state.tick % 120 == 0;\n            if interesting && (g.phase_i >= 2 || case != 4) {\n                last_m = g.mistakes;\n                last_phase = g.phase_i;\n                let s = &g.surgeons[0];\n                eprint!(\n                    \"t={} ph={} tool={} cur=({:.2},{:.2}) use={} mis={} vit={:.0} heat={} ang={:.2} \",\n                    sim.state.tick, g.phase_i, s.tool, s.x, s.z, s.was_use, g.mistakes, g.heart.vitals, g.heat_on, g.heat_angle\n                );\n                for h in &g.hazards {\n                    if h.alive && h.kind != 13 {\n                        eprint!(\n                            \"[k{} st{} tl{} w={:.2}/{:.2} ax={:.2} g={} aux={:.2} ({:.2},{:.2})] \",\n                            h.kind, h.stage, h.tool, h.work, h.need, h.aux, h.grabbed, h.aux2, h.x, h.z\n                        );\n                    }\n                }\n                eprintln!();\n            }\n        }\n        eprintln!(\"end {}\", status(&sim));\n    }\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn survey_skill1() {"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":9361,"output_tokens":8865,"cache_read_input_tokens":92800,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"47b70622-0b2e-4d47-9b61-c9b34f21382e"}
