\\\"vitals\\\":0.0,\\\"voices\\\":0.699999988079071}\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"1bd883d8-c13a-4cf9-80df-7d96a0adaa7d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_108","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The requirements are now clear. Case 4 eye is next.\n\nThe forceps `along` calculation uses the grab axis. With axis 0, it equals dx. The eye is at -0.04 and the cursor at 1.51, so dx is 1.55. The work peak is only 0.75. This mismatch needs fixing.\n\nThe grab likely occurred at x = 0.46, not the eye center. The pull target would then be 2.01, but the cursor stopped at 1.51. Work peaked at 0.75 and decayed while the grab state remained true.\n\nThe forceps `apply` function is likely not running on the eye. The select command may have chosen the inflamed target instead, so the eye never receives the `along` update. This leaves the grab state active while idle decay drains work to zero.\n\nThe decay rate of 0.45 per second matches idle decay exactly. This confirms the forceps apply loop is not running on the eye. The inflamed target at (1.78, -0.37) is only 0.63 units away from the cursor, so it is the likely selection target.\n\nThe release code only runs inside forceps(), so the grab state never clears. The bot keeps aiming at the pull target and idle-decays work while stuck on the wrong neighbor. Sticky select will keep forceps applied to the grabbed hazard even when a closer neighbor is selected.\n\nCase 5 repeats the same failure. The ember at (-1.64, 0.45) is stage 2 and grabbed. The cursor sits at the pull point (-0.81, 0.62). Work remains zero. The grab axis is 0.2 radians from spawn. The direction vector is roughly (0.98, 0.20). The pull target is offset by that direction times 0.85.\n\nThe pull target (-0.81, 0.62) matches the cursor position exactly if the grab was at the first ember center. The second ember at (-0.43, 1.17) is only 0.67 units away from that point, while the first is 0.85 units away. A closer neighbor could therefore be selected and forceps would not apply to the grabbed hazard.\n\nCase 6 repeats the same failure. The tumor is stage 1 and grabbed, but the pool at (1.65, 0.90) is closer than the cursor at (1.33, 0.20). Select steals the pool, suction is not forceps, and the grabbed tumor's work decays.\n\nThe root cause is that select does not know which surgeon holds a grab. The hazard only has a single `grabbed` flag, while focus ids are per surgeon. Two surgeons can grab different things in local play.\n\nThe fix is a sticky focus rule in the select function. When a hazard is already focused and grabbed, or when use is held and the focus accepts the tool within a leash, select should return that hazard instead of the nearest neighbor.\n\nThe fix adds a `use_held` parameter to the `select` function. When a hazard is already grabbed or within a 2.4-unit leash of the tool, it is selected even if a closer neighbor exists. This keeps the eye focused while the inflamed target is closer, so forceps can apply and `along` reaches the need threshold.\n\nThe 2.4-unit leash is sufficient. The pull target sits roughly 2.05 units from the hazard center, so the 2.9-unit radius-plus-leash threshold easily covers it. The grabbed-hazard path bypasses distance checks entirely. The remaining edge case is a far-away surgeon approaching a hazard that has not yet been grabbed.\n\nThe far-away edge case is acceptable. Sticky focus only activates once the surgeon is close enough to engage.\n\nA finished hazard dies and its focus id becomes invalid, so select falls through to the nearest neighbor automatically.\n\nSuture zigzag and laser charge both keep the cursor within the 2.4-unit leash, so they remain safe.\n\nOne concern remains: could sticky focus trap the bot on a hazard it cannot complete?\n\nSticky focus is safe. It only applies when the tool matches and the hazard is grabbed or within the leash. A scalpel near a suture wound will not stick because accepts(tool) is false. If the cursor sits inside the leash but the gesture is stalled, the bot keeps aiming at the right point. When heat releases USE, use_held becomes false and sticky focus drops; select then falls back to nearest, and the grab state clears once forceps() sees !use_held.\n\nBrief heat releases will not lose the grab if select misses the ember during that tick. If select does hit the hazard, the grab clears and work resets by one tick; this is acceptable. The eye pull distance of 1.55 already exceeds its need of 1.4, so no change to the eye requirement is needed.\n\nTumor work peaked at 0.50 before decay because select stole the focus before arrival; sticky focus will let it finish. Ember work stayed zero because apply never ran; sticky focus will keep the pull target active once the grab is set. Nodule and tendril pulls in case 7 were unaffected. The next step is updating the select function and its call site.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The pull reaches the right point, then a nearer hazard steals the tool and the grab stalls. I'll keep the held gesture locked to the hazard it already has."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-69039e88-839d-4f95-ae02-31123780de25-46","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/surgery/ops.rs","old_string":"        let focus = g.surgeons[i].focus;\n        let idx = select(&g.hazards, x, z, tool, focus, just_release);","new_string":"        let focus = g.surgeons[i].focus;\n        let idx = select(&g.hazards, x, z, tool, focus, just_release, use_held);"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-69039e88-839d-4f95-ae02-31123780de25-47","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/surgery/ops.rs","old_string":"fn select(hazards: &[Hazard], x: f32, z: f32, tool: u8, focus: Option<u32>, just_release: bool) -> Option<usize> {\n    if just_release {\n        if let Some(id) = focus {\n            if let Some(i) = hazards.iter().position(|h| h.id == id && h.alive) {\n                return Some(i);\n            }\n        }\n    }","new_string":"fn select(\n    hazards: &[Hazard],\n    x: f32,\n    z: f32,\n    tool: u8,\n    focus: Option<u32>,\n    just_release: bool,\n    use_held: bool,\n) -> Option<usize> {\n    if just_release || use_held {\n        if let Some(id) = focus {\n            if let Some(i) = hazards.iter().position(|h| h.id == id && h.alive && h.kind != K_HEAT) {\n                let h = &hazards[i];\n                // A pull travels away from the wound. Keep the grabbed hazard, or the\n                // one this tool is already working, so a neighbor does not steal it.\n                if h.grabbed || (use_held && h.accepts(tool) && h.dist(x, z) < h.radius + 2.6) || just_release {\n                    return Some(i);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":22772,"output_tokens":3298,"cache_read_input_tokens":113792,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"9600dcb8-21a8-4a81-b5b7-0e09a2353df9"}
