{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_292","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The first task is fixing the mouse ownership latch.\n\nNext, I'll remove the unused native `install_hook` and `drain_hook` stubs. They are only defined under `not-wasm` but never called because the callers are cfg-gated.\n\nThe mouse ownership latch will be added to Harbor as `mouse_owns` and `mouse_aim`. In compose, mouse ownership is set when the cursor moves and cleared when WASD, arrows, or analog input is held. The ownership flag is passed to hand, and the last aim is stored while the mouse owns input.\n\nThe last ground point does not need to be stored. The aim is recomputed every frame from the current mouse position via ground_point, so only the ownership flag must remain true while the mouse owns input. WASD and arrow keys will release ownership during operation. If the cursor has not moved yet, it stays in place.\n\nShift precision is already correctly excluded for mouse ownership. The device stays locked to KeyboardMouse after a single mouse movement frame, which suits prompts and shells. I will also remove the unused `press` function outside tests and begin compiling.\n\nThe ownership logic now prioritizes analog input and keyboard keys over mouse movement. If both a stick or key and the mouse move this frame, the stick wins and clears ownership. The mouse flag is only set when no other device acts. Passing aim to hand while mouse owns is safe even if ground_point returns None.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-1655384b-b7ab-4318-95d2-48d06374af27-9","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/engine-link/crates/pav_app/Cargo.toml"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-1655384b-b7ab-4318-95d2-48d06374af27-10","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/