       next_id: 1,\"},{\"line_number\":450,\"content\":\"            next_id: self.next_id,\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-c67d88a0-4e75-4611-9708-4de25ab2a64a-177","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"648→fn select(\\n    hazards: &[Hazard],\\n650→    x: f32,\\n    z: f32,\\n    tool: u8,\\n    focus: Option<u32>,\\n    just_release: bool,\\n    use_held: bool,\\n) -> Option<usize> {\\n    if just_release || use_held {\\n        if let Some(id) = focus {\\n            if let Some(i) = hazards.iter().position(|h| h.id == id && h.alive && h.kind != K_HEAT) {\\n660→                let h = &hazards[i];\\n                // A pull travels away from the wound. Keep the grabbed hazard, or the\\n                // one this tool is already working, so a neighbor does not steal it.\\n                if h.grabbed || (use_held && h.accepts(tool) && h.dist(x, z) < h.radius + 2.6) || just_release {\\n                    return Some(i);\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n    if tool == IV {\\n670→        if let Some(i) = hazards.iter().position(|h| h.alive && h.kind == K_IV) {\\n            return Some(i);\\n        }\",\"content_concise\":\"648→fn select(\\n    hazards: &[Hazard],\\n650→    x: f32,\\n    z: f32,\\n    tool: u8,\\n    focus: Option<u32>,\\n    just_release: bool,\\n    use_held: bool,\\n) -> Option<usize> {\\n    if just_release || use_held {\\n        if let Some(id) = focus {\\n            if let Some(i) = hazards.iter().position(|h| h.id == id && h.alive && h.kind != K_HEAT) {\\n660→                let h = &hazards[i];\\n                // A pull travels away from the wound. Keep the grabbed hazard, or the\\n                // one this tool is already working, so a neighbor does not steal it.\\n                if h.grabbed || (use_held && h.accepts(tool) && h.dist(x, z) < h.radius + 2.6) || just_release {\\n                    return Some(i);\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n    if tool == IV {\\n670→        if let Some(i) = hazards.iter().position(|h| h.alive && h.kind == K_IV) {\\n            return Some(i);\\n        }\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/surgery/ops.rs\",\"offset\":648,\"limit\":25,\"raw_output\":\"fn select(\\n    hazards: &[Hazard],\\n    x: f32,\\n    z: f32,\\n    tool: u8,\\n    focus: Option<u32>,\\n    just_release: bool,\\n    use_held: bool,\\n) -> Option<usize> {\\n    if just_release || use_held {\\n        if let Some(id) = focus {\\n            if let Some(i) = hazards.iter().position(|h| h.id == id && h.alive && h.kind != K_HEAT) {\\n                let h = &hazards[i];\\n                // A pull travels away from the wound. Keep the grabbed hazard, or the\\n                // one this tool is already working, so a neighbor does not steal it.\\n                if h.grabbed || (use_held && h.accepts(tool) && h.dist(x, z) < h.radius + 2.6) || just_release {\\n                    return Some(i);\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n    if tool == IV {\\n        if let Some(i) = hazards.iter().position(|h| h.alive && h.kind == K_IV) {\\n            return Some(i);\\n        }\\n\",\"total_lines\":787}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"654c5cc8-5b99-4842-8ae2-1df427fa2fc9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_376","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"next_id starts at 1. Case hazards use IDs 1 and 2.\n\nSpawn IDs 3 and 4 are assigned next. The focus is case hazard 2, the original pool at (1.5, -0.8), which remains alive. Sticky selection keeps this case pool because its distance to the cursor is within the combined radius. The new pool at ID 4 never receives work since the case pool blocks it.\n\nThe first spawned pool at (1.2, 0) was closer to the cursor than the sticky case pool. It was suctioned and died, so focus switched to its ID. After release, the dead focus failed sticky selection. The second pool at (0.75, 0.63) became the nearest target.\n\nFocus should have stayed on the second pool. Sticky selection should have kept it. The failure shows focus switched to the case pool instead. The spawn tick had no use command. just_release was true because the previous suction hold was still active.\n\nThe hold phase should keep the second pool alive via sticky selection. Yet focus still switches to the case pool. The select function only updates focus when it returns a valid index. This suggests sticky selection may be returning the case pool's ID instead of the second pool's.\n\nThe spawn tick itself set focus to the case pool. The second pool is closer to the cursor, so the case pool must have been jittered closer during entry.\n\nThe `just_release` branch in the selection logic may be forcing focus back to the old target if it is still alive. This would explain why the second pool at ID 4 never becomes the active focus.\n\nThe `just_release` branch returns the focused hazard if it is alive, even when it does not accept the tool and regardless of distance. On the spawn tick, focus points to the dead first pool, so this branch is bypassed. The suspicion is now that the first pool might actually be the case pool at ID 2, which was cleared during suction.\n\nThe reverse search finds the newest pool first. The first spawn at ID 3 was cleared correctly. The second spawn at ID 4 was aimed at, but focus still ended as 2. Focus switched to the case pool during the hold. The just_release behavior on the first hold tick is now being examined.\n\nThe spawn tick had no use command, so was_use was false and just_release was true. Focus updates on that tick. The first hold tick then has was_use false and use true, meaning sticky selection should follow the current focus. If the spawn tick set focus to the case pool at ID 2, the hold would stick to it. The cursor is still at the first spawn position (1.2, 0), so the nearest target should be the second pool.\n\nThe second spawn is closer than the case pool, so nearest selection should pick it. The dead first pool cannot be the focus because just_release would not return it. The reverse search correctly targets the newest pool. Next, I will check the case pool's accepts() method.\n\nThe case pool accepts suction. To prevent sticky selection from fighting over targets, the test should be restructured so pools spawn far enough apart that the 2.6-unit sticky radius cannot bridge them.\n\nSpawned pools are placed in a tight spiral near the origin, all within about 1.2 units of center. The case pool at (1.5, -0.8) is therefore within the sticky radius of every spawned pool. Once focus locks onto the case pool, the sticky radius covers all nearby pools, so no newly spawned pool can ever be selected while holding use. This explains why the second pool never became the active target. The first pool was cleared because it was the first to spawn and had not yet been targeted.\n\nFocus should have started unset or on nothing. The first pool was nearest at distance zero, so it was selected and cleared. Focus then became the dead pool. On the next spawn tick, just_release would not return that dead focus, so nearest selection should set focus to the new pool without a use command. Sticky should then keep the new pool during the hold. The suspicion is now that just_release may be incorrectly returning the case pool as the focused hazard instead of the dead pool.\n\nRechecking the spawn math shows the second pool at (0.75, 0.63) is closer to the cursor than the case pool at roughly 0.85 units. The nearest target should win and focus should stick to the new pool during the hold. The earlier suspicion that the case pool was winning is incorrect.\n\nThe spawn tick itself set focus to the case pool at ID 2. This means the nearest target at the cursor was the case pool, not the newly spawned pool. The second spawn must have been farther from the cursor than expected.\n\nThe case pool at its closest jittered point is about 0.79 units away from the cursor, slightly farther than the new pool at 0.77 units. The nearest target should therefore win. The suspicion now shifts to whether the cursor was actually at (1.2, 0) during the spawn tick, or whether the first cleared pool was not at the expected position.\n\nThe spawn tick likely set focus to the case pool because the new pool was not nearest from the old cursor position. When the cursor then moved to the new pool while use remained held, sticky selection kept the case pool alive because it stayed within the 2.6-unit radius.\n\nThe sticky radius of 2.6 units is confirmed to be the culprit: it keeps the case pool alive while the new pool is ignored, which matches the bug exactly. The remaining puzzle is why the case pool was nearest at spawn time if the first cleared pool was indeed at (1.2, 0). The reverse search order and push sequence were re-examined, but they still point to the new pool as the first target.\n\nThe spawn position at 0.75, 0.63 is confirmed to be exactly 0.74593 units from the cursor, not the previously estimated 0.77. This brings the gap between the new pool and the case pool down to roughly 0.08 units. With only a small margin left, floating-point jitter or an imprecise distance calculation could easily tip the nearest target toward the case pool.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-af7cb617-3465-4ae8-95f3-15b4324d74ac-178","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"spawn_n","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/engine-link/crates/pav_core/src/games/surgery","glob":"**/*.rs"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1047,"output_tokens":3805,"cache_read_input_tokens":172672,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"2ffca015-7b5c-435b-a0c3-9b7534b1e37e"}
