LTS: u8 = 4;\\npub const M_DEAD: u8 = 5;\\npub const M_ATTRACT: u8 = 6;\\npub const M_CARD: u8 = 7;\\npub const M_MIRROR: u8 = 8;\\n\\npub const X_MIN: f32 = -9.0;\\npub const X_MAX: f32 = 9.0;\\npub const Z_MIN: f32 = -4.0;\\npub const Z_MAX: f32 = 4.2;\\n\\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\\npub struct Surgeon {\\n    pub active: bool,\\n    pub x: f32,\\n    pub z: f32,\\n    pub tool: u8,\\n    pub score: i32,\\n    pub chain: u32,\\n    pub best_chain: u32,\\n    pub mistakes: u32,\\n    pub since_chain: f32,\\n    pub meter: f32,\\n    pub gift_arm: f32,\\n    pub was_use: bool,\\n    pub was_gift: bool,\\n    pub was_defib: bool,\\n    pub defib: f32,\\n    pub iv_cd: f32,\\n    pub wrong_cd: f32,\\n    pub name_i: u8,\\n    pub focus: Option<u32>,\\n    pub focus_t: f32,\\n}\\n\\nimpl Surgeon {\\n    fn new(i: u8) -> Self {\\n        Self {\\n            active: i == 0,\\n            x: 0.0,\\n            z: 0.0,\\n            tool: SCALPEL,\\n            score: 0,\\n            chain: 0,\\n            best_chain: 0,\\n            mistakes: 0,\\n            since_chain: 30.0,\\n            meter: 0.0,\\n            gift_arm: 0.0,\\n            was_use: false,\\n            was_gift: false,\\n            was_defib: false,\\n            defib: 0.0,\\n            iv_cd: 0.0,\\n            wrong_cd: 0.0,\\n            name_i: i,\\n            focus: None,\\n            focus_t: 0.0,\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\\npub struct Coverage {\\n    pub tools: [u32; N_TOOLS],\\n    pub hazards: [u32; N_KINDS],\\n    pub strains: [u32; 18],\\n    pub gifts: [u32; 3],\\n    pub code: u32,\\n    pub ratings: [u32; 4],\\n}\\n\\nimpl Default for Coverage {\\n    fn default() -> Self {\\n        Self { tools: [0; N_TOOLS], hazards: [0; N_KINDS], strains: [0; 18], gifts: [0; 3], code: 0, ratings: [0; 4] }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\\nstruct Checkpoint {\\n    phase_i: u16,\\n    phase_ready: bool,\\n    hazards: Vec<Hazard>,\\n    next_id: u32,\\n    vitals: f32,\\n    heart_phase: f32,\\n    since_r: f32,\\n    beats: u32,\\n    timer: f32,\\n    elapsed: f32,\\n    scores: [i32; 4],\\n    chains: [u32; 4],\\n    best: [u32; 4],\\n    meters: [f32; 4],\\n    mistakes: u32,\\n    codes_left: u8,\\n    strain_phase: u8,\\n    gifts_unlocked: u8,\\n    gifts_used: u8,\\n    recent_unlock: u8,\\n\",\"total_lines\":1032}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"cd13f1ac-24af-4f45-98b9-3a4363a8e3e9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_402","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"A critical insight about server mode and message routing has emerged.\n\nThe server is the last key in the player list. It receives messages from players and sends snapshots back. Player IDs are 0 through n-1, while the server ID equals n. In server mode, the host acts as the server. `stats.sent` increments on `sendGame`.\n\nBidirectional traffic requires both sending and receiving. For a single player without a server, traffic is not required, but the round must still end with a valid result.\n\nWhen the host leaves, the game should not finish on its own. The harness removes the host first, then waits before declaring a timeout.\n\nThe host's iframe is removed immediately after coordinator.leave marks the host out. This prevents the old host from calling finish and racing with settle(). Other clients receive 'leave' and 'end' messages without error. The shim's time-up handler does not fire.\n\nThe start message carries a countdown in milliseconds. The client sets its goAt to the current time plus that delay.\n\nCard events emit as 'card' with a detail object. The shim pushes these as `['card', d]`.\n\nA card is shown only when the player owns it, has sent ready, and the round is not yet ended.\n\nThe card is only sent after `ready()` is called. The harness sends it every 250 milliseconds until the flag is set, with a two-second timeout. The shim loads before WebAssembly and can handle keydown events immediately. The startup sequence is: shim connects first, then wasm unpacks, then Rust calls `ready()`, and finally the harness delivers the card.\n\nThe card sequence is confirmed: `ready()` triggers the first tick, which sets `cardSent` within the 250 ms window, allowing the 2000 ms wait to pass before the Enter key is poked.\n\n`ready()` must run after the first Rust frame so the game is actually running before the round begins. The 20-second timeout may be tight for a heavier engine, since packed wasm can be large and unpacking slow.\n\nI will call `ready()` from the first Rust frame once graphics are initialized, rather than waiting for a full frame. A JavaScript fallback will trigger it when `loadGame` finishes if the first frame is too late. The 20-second conformance window already includes page load, so a slow WebAssembly compile on this machine should still fit.\n\nThe server must still run its frame loop to pump the hub, step the simulation, and send snapshots. It can skip drawing, audio, and egui.\n\nHosts send snapshots once the countdown reaches zero.\n\nClients do not run an authoritative simulation. They apply snapshots to a mirror Surgery so their view draws matching vitals and cursors.\n\nLocal cursor prediction will overwrite the own cursor position before drawing. The two-page test will verify that a client's aim change updates the host's cursor and vitals. It will use a harness with two players, low latency, and no auto mode. The test will move the client aim and read the host status cursors and vitals.\n\nSynthetic mouse events may not reach winit on wasm. The harness pokes keyboard events for WASD and arrow keys during conformance, but winit likely listens on the window or canvas rather than document.body. Untrusted synthetic events might be ignored.\n\nThe PLAN explicitly requires JavaScript to listen for synthetic Enter events, not only winit. Gameplay arrows likely cannot reach winit either.\n\nBidirectional traffic is satisfied by message frequency alone. The host sends snapshots at 15 Hz and clients send input at 15 Hz during play, so the harness does not need arrow keys to perform any action.\n\nFor the two-page cursor test, client input must actually move the host cursor. I am weighing options to make that happen.\n\nA JavaScript hook will expose `window.__game.aim(slot, x, z, tool, held)` for tests to drive the host cursor. The shim will also listen for keyboard events and forward arrow and WASD input to Rust. This satisfies the two-page verification that client aim changes update the host surgeon's cursor and vitals.\n\nThe shim will track keyboard aim from keydown and keyup events, plus mouse movement. This aim state will be exposed as `H.aim` for Rust to read each frame during a session. Synthetic keyboard events from the harness can then move the cursor even if winit ignores them. The change is safe because it adds fields without breaking the required API.\n\nThe shim will preserve the required API and add DOM event listeners. It will track key presses in `H.keys` and release them on keyup, plus expose a `H.stick()` helper for WASD and arrow input. A `H.mouse` object will capture mouse movement.\n\nThe two-page test will dispatch an ArrowRight keydown on the client iframe and verify the host cursor moves. Rust will read aim state from `H.stick()` or `H.keys`. Both keyboard and mouse input paths will be supported for client aim.\n\nSnapshots must stay under 1200 bytes to meet the 20 message-per-second cap. At 15 Hz this is easily achievable. Code blue hazards may reach roughly twenty bleeders, so the snapshot array will be compact.\n\nThe snapshot format is now concrete. Vitals are scaled by ten, hazard positions are quantized to tenths, and only alive hazards are kept. A twelve-hazard array fits within the 1200-byte limit. Cursor data stores slot, position, tool, and active state. The cue queue keeps the last eight IDs. Client input stays around eighty bytes. The host will store the latest SurgeonInput per remote slot.\n\nEach tick the host merges its local hand into the InputFrame and overwrites remote slots with the latest SurgeonInput from messages. The server has no local slot, so all surgeons come from messages and inactive slots stay idle. The client mirror must avoid stepping the world with local simulation rules that diverge from the authoritative state.\n\nThe client mirror will use a `M_MIRROR` mode. In `ops::tick`, this mode returns early and only updates the local predicted cursor. `apply_mirror` then overwrites vitals, hazards, other cursors, and the timer from the snapshot. The local cursor is drawn last, so it always wins. Host mode remains `M_OP`.\n\nThe start event will set host and server to `M_OP` with a seeded RNG and activate known surgeons, while clients enter `M_MIRROR` without simulating hazards. The simulation RNG must be public so the seed can be injected. The game timer is four minutes, but the harness triggers `timeUp` after a few seconds of play to respect the six-minute protocol cap.\n\nThe game timer does not need to finish on its own during the harness. The shim's time-up handler will call finish, and Rust must publish standings via H.publish so H.last is populated. The first publish should happen immediately when play starts, including every present player in the standings array. Whether a removed player should still appear in the final standings is still undecided.\n\nThe conformance check requires every present player to appear in the standings array. A removed player may be omitted, but including one who is not present is a failure. The safest approach is to use `AIHub.players` at time-up as the present set, since the hub filters out leaving players. The shim fallback of `[AIHub.players.map(p => p.id)]` already forms a single tie group for all present players.\n\nThe time-up handler may fire before Rust updates H.last. The fallback standings array can safely include every original player, even those who later leave, because checkStandings allows removed players to be placed. The only hard requirement is that all present players must appear in the final standings.\n\nRust will not set H.last with standings that can become stale. The shim fallback will handle the final standings array instead.\n\nThe fallback standings array is safe because the hub already filters out leaving players before time-up. A false won flag is acceptable since the conformance check only requires status to be played. The shim may publish standings on its own interval rather than relying solely on Rust.\n\nRust will build standings from player IDs stored at welcome and updated on leave events. Scores are optional. The server is the host, so its own ID is excluded from the standings array.\n\nRust must not call finish directly; only the shim's time-up handler should trigger it. Console errors and uncaught exceptions will fail the page-error check, so those must be avoided. The packed page uses WebGPU.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-62a45ffe-678c-4232-8db5-dc61caaadcbe-44","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"request_redraw|fn frame|surgery_frame","glob":"**/pav_app/src/**/*.rs"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-62a45ffe-678c-4232-8db5-dc61caaadcbe-45","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"pub rng|state\\.rng","glob":"**/pav_core/src/**/*.rs","head_limit":30}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-62a45ffe-678c-4232-8db5-dc61caaadcbe-46","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/engine-link/web/index.html","limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7337,"output_tokens":3965,"cache_read_input_tokens":108160,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"f775b659-b2d6-4eef-99c8-f0b31953b333"}
