, {'t': 1579, 'me': 1, 'host': False, 'last': False, 'sentN': 0, 'pubN': 0, 'sentLen': 0, 'sendErr': None, 'mode': 8, 'screen': 'op', 'vitals': 70, 'timer': 240, 'x0': 0, 'x1': 2}]\\n--- 38 ---\\n[{'t': 2087, 'me': 0, 'host': True, 'last': True, 'sentN': 0, 'pubN': 1, 'sentLen': 0, 'sendErr': None, 'mode': 3, 'screen': 'op', 'vitals': 69.306640625, 'timer': 240, 'x0': -2, 'x1': 0}, {'t': 2089, 'me': 1, 'host': False, 'last': False, 'sentN': 0, 'pubN': 0, 'sentLen': 0, 'sendErr': None, 'mode': 8, 'screen': 'op', 'vitals': 70, 'timer': 240, 'x0': 0, 'x1': 2}]\\n--- 39 ---\\n[{'t': 2607, 'me': 0, 'host': True, 'last': True, 'sentN': 0, 'pubN': 1, 'sentLen': 0, 'sendErr': None, 'mode': 3, 'screen': 'op', 'vitals': 69.306640625, 'timer': 240, 'x0': -2, 'x1': 0}, {'t': 2609, 'me': 1, 'host': False, 'last': False, 'sentN': 0, 'pubN': 0, 'sentLen': 0, 'sendErr': None, 'mode': 8, 'screen': 'op', 'vitals': 70, 'timer': 240, 'x0': 0, 'x1': 2}]\\nnot complete    0.33  round 1: 2 players, seed 987827038, limit 20 s, mode shared-world\\n   0.43  p0 → hub: hello\\n   0.47  hub → p0: welcome\\n   0.58  p1 → hub: hello\\n   0.74  hub → p1: welcome\\n   2.46  hub → p0: card\\n   2.47  p0 → hub: ready\\n   3.36  hub → p0: card\\n   4.36  hub → p0: card\\n   5.36  hub → p0: card\\n   6.36  hub → p0: card\\n   7.36  hub → p0: card\\n   8.36  hub → p0: card\\n   9.36  hub → p0: card\\n  10.36  hub → p0: card\\n  15.28  p1 → hub: ready\\n  15.36  hub → p1: card\\n  15.37  p0 answers ready on the game's card\\n  15.37  hub → p0: card\\n  15.37  hub → p1: card\\n  15.37  p1 answers ready on the game's card\\n  15.37  hub → p0: card\\n  15.37  hub → p1: card\\n  15.42  hub → p0: card\\n  15.42  hub → p1: card\\n  15.42  splash over: the round starts once every game has loaded\\n  15.44  hub → p0: start in 2980 ms\\n  15.44  hub → p1: start in 2978 ms\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"command\":\"export TMPDIR=/tmp/hbr-pw PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64\\nxvfb-run -a python3 /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer/hub_probe.py\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Count shim sends during a two-player round\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a%2Fwork/01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2/terminal/call-cf95ad84-377e-4d2d-94f4-0096e810280e-110.log\",\"total_bytes\":4486}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"e3fda81b-1fd1-47f3-978c-fd8b5346fcf9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_512","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"A critical issue was found: `pubN` is 1 while `sentN` remains 0 for over a second during gameplay.\n\n`send_snap` runs before `publish` in the same block. Since `sentN` is zero, `H.send` was never invoked. The focus is now on why `send_snap` returns early.\n\nThe render loop appears almost dead. Only one frame published after play began. The game advanced 1.5 seconds of AIHub.time but produced just a single Rust frame. This explains why `pubN` remained at 1.\n\nThe first frame's `send_snap` only accumulated 0.016 of its 0.066 threshold, so it returned early and never sent. The suspicion is that the first op frame panics or deadlocks after `publish`, which sits just before the egui draw step.\n\nA silent hang in `gfx.draw` on the first op frame would freeze the loop without a JS exception. That matches the single publish and zero send exactly.\n\nThe vitals reading of 69.3 suggests only a few simulation ticks occurred before the freeze. The client x1 value of 2 may reflect local arrow-key movement before the loop also froze.\n\nThe client also shows zero sentN. Its x1 of 2 likely reflects a single large frame delta from stick input, not many ticks. The host x0 of -2 similarly suggests one slow frame rather than several. This points to the loop running extremely slowly—perhaps one frame every few seconds—rather than freezing completely.\n\nThe probe ended at sample 39, well before the round's time-up. Only about five seconds of gameplay were captured, and during that brief window pubN remained at one. This confirms the game is effectively frozen once the operation begins.\n\nThe likely cause is the plate mesh being rebuilt every frame on SwiftShader with two contexts, which is extremely slow. A deadlock or a GPU process hang from the first draw are also possible.\n\nThe 1/60 second accumulator is the root cause. When frames take over two seconds, the accumulator never reaches four frames and sends never fire.\n\nThe fix is to send the first snapshot immediately and switch to a wall-clock accumulator using real `dt`. Poll can accumulate elapsed time and send every 1/15 of real time, or simply every frame while play is active. This removes the false 60 FPS assumption.\n\nThe concrete fix is to pass `dt` into `send_snap` and accumulate real elapsed time. The first snapshot should always go out immediately. After that, only send when the accumulator reaches 1/15 of a second, or every frame if the interval is larger.\n\nThe same wall-clock accumulator bug exists in `send_input`, so the client will also be fixed. The first snapshot must always go out immediately, even if the frame delta is tiny.\n\nA single frozen frame would still register one sent snapshot and match the vitals reading at that instant. Cursor movement requires many frames, so the loop must stay alive to pass the two-page test.\n\nThe extreme slowness likely stems from SwiftShader compiling shaders on the first frame for a 420x240 cel plate with outlines. The observed two-second window may have captured only that initial shader compilation.\n\nThe first frame's `publish` ran before the draw call, which is why `pubN` reached one while the second frame never completed. The probe ended before the second frame could finish, so the stall likely lasted longer than the two-second observation window.\n\nA slow `gfx.draw` stall is the likely culprit. If shader compilation takes several seconds, the play window ends before the second frame can start.\n\nThe first fix is to send the snapshot immediately on the first play frame, before the draw begins. This guarantees at least one sent packet even if only one frame finishes before time-up.\n\nThe second fix is to make the initial draw faster so more frames can run. One option is a quiet plate on all hub copies. This would hide the operation in the hub but keep traffic flowing. Cursor movement happens in the simulation before drawing, so the two-page test would still pass.\n\nSending the first snapshot immediately would pass traffic, but a single simulation tick with a capped delta of 0.1 might only move the cursor by 1.5 units. That is too flaky for the two-page test.\n\nThe real cost is likely the heavy plate mesh. It rebuilds every frame inside small hub iframes and may be the bottleneck. The better fix is to skip the expensive plate drawing in hub iframes while keeping it visible to clients and the host.\n\nThe plate mesh rebuild costs only 50–200 ms, not the full eight seconds. Shader compilation is the real bottleneck. The host rendered the title card for fifteen seconds before play began, so shaders should already be warm. The operation plate likely uses different pipelines that compile on its first frame.\n\nThe title card rendered for many seconds, so shaders should already be warm. The operation frame should therefore be fast, yet only one frame appeared in two seconds. This points to a stuck draw call or a deadlock in the simulation loop after boot. The frozen status and single publish confirm that `surgery_frame` never re-entered.\n\nThe card frames ran fine, so the loop was alive before the operation began. The freeze happens specifically on the first operation frame, when instruments and hazards are drawn for the first time. SwiftShader pipeline compilation for those new meshes can block for many seconds. Prewarming those pipelines during the title card would eliminate the stall.\n\nThe harness can burst simulation ticks via a `__game.step(n)` hook. The hook increments a counter that the drain hook later drains as `host.burst(n)`. If the frame loop is merely slow but alive, this could advance the simulation and move the cursor enough for the two-page test.\n\nThe `step()` hook is a poor substitute for real frames. A one-second key hold could fall between two-second frames and fail the test.\n\nThe better fix is to make hub draws cheaper so the loop stays interactive. One option is to set `hub_quiet` on every hub copy, not just the server, which would skip the expensive plate rebuild entirely. The canvas would then show mostly clear color.\n\nThe two-page test checks cursors via status JSON, not pixels, so the hub harness does not need to render the plate. Setting quiet on all hub copies would skip the expensive mesh rebuild entirely while keeping the simulation alive. Audio would still run for the host.\n\nThe plate draw is not the hang. Card mode draws the same plate and runs fine, so the stall is specific to the operation frame compiling new hazard and instrument shaders. The first op frame blocks for a long time while those pipelines warm up, which explains why only one frame appears before the play window ends.\n\nThe client must also send its first input snapshot before its draw call. Both sides will post traffic before rendering, so the round still counts as having sent data even if the draw hangs.\n\nOne snapshot from each side is enough to satisfy the traffic check, because the bridge delivers the message and both sides register sent and received. The cursor test still needs more frames after input, so either the operation draw must not hang or the test must wait for a second frame. I will inspect what the operation view draws that is new.\n\nThe chosen fix is to set `hub_quiet` to true for every hub copy, including clients. This skips the expensive plate and instrument mesh rebuilds entirely, so frames stay fast like during the title card. The host already uses this flag.\n\nA blank hub plate would hide the operation from the user, so that fix is rejected. The simulation pump returned successfully because publish ran before the stall, which means the hang occurs after publishing in audio, scene building, or draw. Skipping instruments and hazards on the first frames was considered too messy.\n\nThe plan is to rate-limit sends using delta time while still allowing the first snapshot immediately, and to keep hub clients and host drawing to investigate the stall. The cursor test will wait up to fifteen seconds after play starts for a second frame. Next, I will inspect the view emit code to see if operation mode spawns an explosion of particles or meshes compared to card mode.\n\nA single simulation tick drops vitals by 0.7 while the timer barely moves, confirming the freeze is limited to one frame. The emit code is being checked for operation-specific mesh or particle explosions. The browser is also being checked for a post-test hang.\n\nA 25-second probe will run after the operation mode starts to check if frames resume. It will poll the publish counter and print its value. This avoids changing Rust code before confirming whether the simulation loop recovers.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-32f254d8-5119-4225-93cb-c9108c498c1b-111","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer/hub_probe.py","old_string":"        for i in range(40):","new_string":"        for i in range(70):"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2362,"output_tokens":4175,"cache_read_input_tokens":185856,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"fb2a8273-48e6-4c26-81cc-c00a9da4a161"}
