a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer\\\"\\nxvfb-run -a python3 \\\"$SCRATCH/hub_raf.py\\\" > \\\"$SCRATCH/hub-raf.txt\\\" 2>&1\\necho EXIT:$?\\ntail -c 8000 \\\"$SCRATCH/hub-raf.txt\\\"\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Probe iframe rAF counts and errors during hub play\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a%2Fwork/01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2/terminal/call-736b4e7c-2762-44df-9174-f255df584cdc-36.log\",\"total_bytes\":8007}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"bcd9c8aa-e8b8-4bed-afd9-b017b8711e09"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_536","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"A critical performance issue was found: requestAnimationFrame is firing roughly 20,000 times per second.\n\nThe wrapper increments a counter on every requestAnimationFrame call, not on every callback execution. This explains the inflated count. The real issue may be winit's ControlFlow::Poll on wasm calling it in a tight loop.\n\nThe browser normally coalesces requestAnimationFrame into a single callback per frame. The inflated count suggests the event loop is scheduling a new rAF on every about_to_wait cycle, and ControlFlow::Poll may be spinning in a tight loop.\n\nThe simulation state froze after one tick. The timer and vitals stopped updating for over five seconds. This confirms the event loop is stuck in a tight loop.\n\nThe frozen timer proves surgery_frame is not executing on those requestAnimationFrame callbacks. Either the callbacks are cancelled or the wrong function runs. The status snapshot is also stale, since it should update at the end of every frame.\n\nA zero delta-time hypothesis explains the frozen state. If dt is zero after the first frame, the timer stops, vitals stay flat, and accumulated data never advances. The host sent exactly two packets, matching two frames with non-zero dt. This suggests the simulation loop stalled right after the initial tick.\n\nThe timer value of 239.8667 equals exactly eight ticks of 1/60, or one step of roughly 0.133 seconds. This suggests the simulation advanced by a single non-zero delta-time frame before freezing.\n\nThe simulation likely advanced by roughly 0.133 seconds through eight fixed 1/60 substeps before freezing. The 20,000 requestAnimationFrame callbacks may be spinning without ever invoking surgery_frame, or the function returns early before updating status. The function begins with an early return when the graphics or host is missing.\n\nThe delta-time calculation caps each frame at 0.1 seconds. Even with twenty thousand scheduled callbacks per second, the total accumulated time should still exceed five seconds and advance the timer. The frozen state proves the function is not actually executing.\n\nThe counter likely sits on the schedule side, not the callback side. requestAnimationFrame does not execute its callback immediately, so a tight JavaScript loop cannot produce twenty thousand actual invocations per second. The inflated count instead reflects rapid scheduling calls that the browser coalesces into a single frame.\n\nThe next frame might actually execute all 20,000 queued callbacks at once. That would freeze the page for a single vsync and explain the frozen status. The real game loop may not be the requestAnimationFrame callbacks at all. Winit 0.30 on wasm might use setTimeout or queueMicrotask instead. The symptoms remain unchanged: one simulation update, extreme rAF scheduling, no errors, and only two messages sent.\n\nThe sustained rAF count over five seconds suggests the scheduling loop is yielding rather than blocking for an entire frame. A recursive winit poll pattern that schedules a new callback after each frame may be responsible.\n\nA simple recursive winit poll pattern would only produce sixty callbacks per second, not twenty thousand. A tight busy-loop that repeatedly schedules requestAnimationFrame could explain the inflated count. The wrapper itself is not recursive; a callback that calls requestAnimationFrame once per frame is normal.\n\nA synchronous JavaScript spin is required to explain twenty thousand requestAnimationFrame calls per second. The next step is to inspect winit's wasm ControlFlow::Poll implementation in the cargo registry.\n\nThe wrapper might be re-entering winit's animation frame loop. The timer is frozen at 239.87 while the mode is still op, so the simulation only advanced once. Either surgery_frame never runs, returns before publishing status, or delta-time is zero and the simulation never steps again.\n\nThe browser may be deferring all scheduled callbacks until a single frame, or dropping them entirely. If they do execute with zero delta-time after the first frame, status remains frozen. The counter still grows because the spin loop yields enough for Playwright to evaluate between samples. Even so, the sum of delta-time should still advance over five hundred milliseconds if Instant is moving forward.\n\nA single synchronous burst of twelve thousand callbacks could all fire within one millisecond. Each would have zero delta-time because Instant resolution is one millisecond, so the timer would not advance during that burst. The browser would then idle until the next sample. This fits the observed timeline and explains why the counter grew while the simulation remained frozen.\n\nThe zero-delta-time theory is weakening. If the callbacks fired continuously, the timer should still advance by roughly half a second. The real cause may be a bug where frame_dt and simulation dt are mismatched, or surgery_frame may not actually be running. I will inspect host.pump() to see how delta-time is applied.\n\nThe simulation may be using its own internal wall-clock timestep inside host.pump() rather than the delta-time passed to link.poll and compose. The freeze could also stem from a paused state: note_mode might have set the shell paused flag incorrectly, or hub.play could be true while the simulation control speed is zero.\n\nThe initial tick likely occurs during boot rather than inside host.pump(). After the first pump advances eight substeps, later pumps stop stepping entirely. The simulation clock may have been set to a future time, causing it to think it is already caught up. Another possibility is that RedrawRequested is not actually driving the execution path.\n\nThe rAF callbacks are likely a separate spinner that never invokes surgery_frame. I will add a counter inside the Rust function to verify how many times it actually executes.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-553a8712-1178-4be9-88b3-9c3c7524856a-37","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"fn pump|paused|accum","glob":"**/pav_app/src/**/*.rs","head_limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-553a8712-1178-4be9-88b3-9c3c7524856a-38","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"fn pump","glob":"**/*.rs"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6154,"output_tokens":3084,"cache_read_input_tokens":72320,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"686d8d77-865d-4d99-836b-20cd61ce5c81"}
