{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_626","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Checking whether gilrs on WASM uses the Gamepad API.\n\nGilrs likely uses web-sys Gamepad on WASM. Stubbing navigator.getGamepads to return a DualSense with standard mapping might let gilrs detect it. If the pad is present but idle, pad_nav evaluates to false. The device would remain keyboard until mouse movement occurs.\n\nTo simulate a pad press, set `window.__pad.buttons[0].pressed = true` for one frame. The game must poll `getGamepads` during that frame and then release the button. Standard mapping uses index 0 for South (A/Cross), 1 for East (B/Circle), 2 for West (X/Square), and 3 for North (Y/Triangle). DPad buttons map to indices 12 through 15. Gilrs maps Button::South to Cross.\n\nChecking whether gilrs WASM actually calls navigator.getGamepads. A mouse click might be more reliable than a simulated pad press, since the game activates on input.confirm or input.click, and click is triggered by left mouse press.\n\nThe boot script clicks the canvas before reading status, but the title screen persists. The click likely arrived too early and was lost or cleared before the game processed it. After waiting 1200 ms, the status remained unchanged.\n\nThe click might not reach winit because the canvas is not the event target, or because Playwright does not generate the exact event winit listens for. Egui ignores consumed mouse events, so it should not block the click. The timing looks correct: frames are already running when the click happens, and the position falls inside the canvas.\n\nWinit on WASM listens to canvas pointer events, so the click should reach the game. The real issue may be frame timing: the 1200 ms wait might not cover a full frame, meaning the status is read from before the click was processed. Pressing Enter also queues an event. The status stays unchanged because no frame has run yet. Later, the shoot() wait_screen polls for ten seconds and eventually processes the queued Enter, but that happens in a different browser instance.\n\nThe shoot script clicks the canvas and immediately grabs the title status. It then presses keys. The about sequence returned \"menu\" rather than \"story\". This suggests only one event landed, either the click or the Enter. If frames took over ten seconds, wait_screen would have timed out on the title screen. It did not. It returned menu immediately.\n\nA frame clearly ran within ten seconds because the menu appeared. The remaining keys were lost because they were pressed during a long frame gap. Winit on WASM queues DOM keyboard events and wakes the loop on input. Slow GPU work during redraw may be causing the delay.\n\nA keydown event arrives and winit queues it. The next frame runs `compose()`, which consumes the key and clears the queue. That frame may take several seconds of GPU work. Playwright sends keydown and keyup in rapid succession, but if the browser is blocked by a long synchronous Rust frame, the JavaScript event loop cannot process those events.\n\nA blocked frame queues key events in the browser. They all arrive at once after the frame returns. One redraw consumes them in a batch. ate() handles Enter and one ArrowDown. just.clear() drops the rest. The game processes both a down and an enter in the same step() call. Focus increments before activate runs.\n\nThe `ate` function removes every matching key from the queue, not just one. If Enter and five ArrowDowns arrive in a single frame, the first Enter consumes all of them at once. Only one confirm event fires, so the menu only moves down once.\n\nThe observed result was \"menu\", not \"difficulty\". If five ArrowDowns arrived in one frame, they would consume all of them and activate the second menu item. The fact that only the title-to-menu transition occurred suggests the extra keys were not processed in that same frame. I am now tracing the exact timeline of shoot() to see when those key events actually landed.\n\nThe exact shoot() sequence is now clear: click, wait, press Enter, press ArrowDown five times with 180 ms gaps, press Enter again. If the first frame after these keys is blocked by slow GPU work, every key queues and arrives together in one batch. ate() consumes one Enter and one ArrowDown, leaving the focus at zero and activating the title-to-menu transition. This matches the observed \"menu\" result.\n\nThe story sequence also lands both Enter presses in one frame, producing the same title-to-menu transition. The dev sequence from that menu state yields another single confirm, returning to menu and matching the observed \"SHOT dev menu\" result.\n\nThe fix is to poll status after each key press until it changes or a new frame appears. A frame counter in the status would require a rebuild, so that option is avoided.\n\nA frame counter would require a WASM rebuild, which is too slow. Instead, the bot should wait long enough between key presses for a frame to definitely complete. The title screen status appears static: BPM and timer remain unchanged across snapshots, so the game likely does not advance its internal clock while on the title screen.\n\nMouse movement does not reveal whether a frame was consumed. The bot should hold each key down until the status changes, then release it. This prevents keys from being sent while a frame is in progress or before the next redraw. If the JavaScript event loop is not blocked and Wait wakes on every key, the batching problem may not occur.\n\nThe bot must wait until the status reflects a key press before sending the next one. For keys that change the screen or focus, it should poll until the status changes. The launch test uses one Enter and waits for the screen to become \"menu\", which proves the input altered state. Solo title is not quiet, so it runs the full plate and likely takes longer than the hub op.\n\nA 20-second timeout for each key press should be enough, since the ten-second wait already reached the menu. The about sequence needs five ArrowDowns and one Enter; the story sequence needs two Enter presses; and the op case files need one Enter.\n\nThe op sequence moves focus through files and op screens. The dev sequence pauses, moves down four times to reach Dev Mode, and enters. No keyboard cheat was found. The plan requires saving screenshots of results, but the evidence item says these do not override a green suite, so the results may be missing. Winning a case would take four minutes of real time if the bot does not play. Dev mode has a \"Bot plays\" option that could help. I will read bot_plays next.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-763b578c-1ac2-42ad-8327-27e10629c875-19","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"bot_plays|Cmd::Dev|what: 6|what == 6","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/engine-link/crates","glob":"*.rs"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-763b578c-1ac2-42ad-8327-27e10629c875-20","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer/balance.txt","limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-763b578c-1ac2-42ad-8327-27e10629c875-21","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer/hub-p4.txt","limit":30}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-763b578c-1ac2-42ad-8327-27e10629c875-22","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer/hub-cursor2.txt","limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2003,"output_tokens":3210,"cache_read_input_tokens":52608,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"56d2143c-1f8c-475b-888c-9eddbff151f2"}
