e = app.egui_state.as_mut().unwrap();\\n    let raw = state.take(&gfx.window);\\n    let shell = &app.harbor.shell;\\n    let op_ref = op.as_deref();\\n    let out = app.egui_ctx.run_ui(raw, |ui| {\\n        pav_ui::surgery::draw(ui, shell, op_ref);\\n    });\\n    state.platform_output(&gfx.window, out.platform_output);\\n    let ppp = out.pixels_per_point;\\n    let prims = app.egui_ctx.tessellate(out.shapes, ppp);\\n    let mut textures = out.textures_delta;\\n    gfx.draw(&scene, Some((&prims, &mut textures, ppp)));\\n    let _ = &mut scene;\\n\\n    let status = status_json(&app.harbor.shell, op.as_deref());\\n    app.harbor.status = status.clone();\\n    publish_status(&status);\\n    #[cfg(target_arch = \\\"wasm32\\\")]\\n    install_hook(app);\\n}\\n\\nfn aim_rig(app: &mut App) {\\n    app.rig.params = CameraParams {\\n        tilt: 73.0,\\n        yaw: 0.0,\\n        distance: 19.9,\\n        fov: 36.0,\\n        ortho: false,\\n        follow_lag: 0.0,\\n        height_offset: 0.0,\\n    };\\n    app.rig.snap(Vec3::new(0.0, 0.0, 0.2));\\n}\\n\",\"total_lines\":886}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"b4329d69-e75a-4b91-b6d5-fe4da0b6751e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_652","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The draw step precedes publish_status and uses the updated shell.\n\nThe screenshot showing Code Blue reflects an older presented frame. WebGPU present is asynchronous, so the canvas may briefly display the previous swapchain image. The sequence for Frame N shows the down press processing, shell focus updating to 3, drawing Code Blue, and publishing status.\n\nThe script immediately presses down again after seeing focus 3. Frame about draws and publishes its status. If frame time is two seconds, the GPU may finish presenting before the script polls for about. The about frame could already be submitted when the script checks.\n\nThe black title shots are the smoking gun. The old script waited 1200 ms plus a click before capturing, while the new script captures as soon as status reads title. If the first frames render as clear color before the mesh is ready, 600 ms may be too short for the initial present.\n\nLater shots are painted, confirming the mesh works. The title shot was black because it captured before the mesh appeared. The about.png image shows Code Blue instead of About, which is several navigation steps behind the actual focus. This suggests the image description may be hallucinating the highlight.\n\nThe dev.png image shows Controls highlighted, but the final state should have been focus 4 (Dev Mode). This confirms the screenshots are two to three steps behind the actual focus. The vision model may be misreading the images. I considered computing a hash or inspecting pixels directly, but PIL is unavailable.\n\nI considered using ImageMagick or ffmpeg to inspect the actual pixel data in the screenshots. I also realized the Read tool already returned the image itself, so the vision description might be accurate after all. I will trust the grab descriptions and proceed to fix the capture timing.\n\nA three-second pause after the status matches should let the correct frame present. The lag of several menu steps suggests ffmpeg is grabbing a cached frame from earlier, not just one frame behind. If the display is frozen, three seconds may still be too short. The X window might be showing a different screen than the one being updated.\n\nThe DISPLAY environment is correctly inherited from xvfb-run, so the grab target is not the issue. The suspicion now falls on Swiftshader and WebGPU presenting to an offscreen surface while the X window buffer stays frozen. This would explain why the title shot was black before any blit occurred, and why later shots captured only an intermediate frame that did get blitted.\n\nThe story.png image shows the title screen instead of the story page. This confirms the grab is capturing a frame from before the menu transition finished. The X window is updating, but it is consistently behind the latest frame by several transitions.\n\nChrome's compositor may update the X buffer on a slower timer than the script polls status. A fixed 600 ms pause is too short when the tab is busy. Reading canvas pixels via page.evaluate is unreliable for WebGPU canvases. The revised strategy is to wait until two more consecutive status polls confirm the same screen, with a minimum wall time of two seconds.\n\nThe story.png description explicitly cites title screen text like \"Press Enter\" and \"Mercy Harbor General · Night Ward,\" not story dialogue. This confirms the grab is capturing a stale frame rather than a misread image.\n\nThe story.png description explicitly cites title screen text like \"Press Enter\" and \"Mercy Harbor General · Night Ward,\" not story dialogue. This confirms the grab is capturing a stale frame rather than a misread image.\n\nI will compare file timestamps and sample pixels to verify whether story.png and title.png differ. The black title shots still show the browser tab chrome, so the window is visible but the canvas itself is blank at that moment. This rules out a missed window and confirms the canvas is black before the game content appears.\n\nThe title shot was captured before the first real present. The story shot showing the title screen confirms the entire navigation is lagged. The shoot took roughly one hundred seconds for three boots and many key presses, so each frame likely takes half a second to one second. A sixty-hundred millisecond wait after the story status should have been enough to see the story page if present were within that window.\n\nI considered whether status updates without a present, but publish_status follows gfx.draw so the timing should be fine. The real issue may be that Swiftshader on WebGPU defers X compositing until the next rAF, and the script's frame waits for that same rAF. A sixty-hundred millisecond pause after the story status should have allowed that next composite to finish if frames were under six hundred milliseconds.\n\nHeavy scenes may delay the story frame's composite until GPU work finishes. I will compare file sizes and pixel means to verify whether the screenshots are stale. A three-second pause after the screen settles should let several frames render the same state.\n\nThe capture timing will be fixed by extending the pause to 2500 ms in shot() and adding a 2000 ms wait before the title screenshot so the mesh appears. The blank launch-1 and launch-2 title PNGs must be retaken, since they fail the \"substantially painted\" requirement; the status.json is already correct, so only the screenshots need replacement.\n\nA full rerun with longer waits is the simplest fix. The script will be re-run with three-second pauses after each screen settle, plus extra waits before the title shots so the mesh appears. This should take about four minutes. I will also add a pixel-mean check via ffmpeg to reject black grabs.\n\nI will compute mean luma with ffmpeg and reject black grabs by waiting and reshooting up to three times. Luma cannot distinguish a stale menu from the correct screen, so the real fix is to wait until two consecutive status polls confirm the same state before capturing.\n\nThe settle function will hold until the same screen has been present for four seconds. This is safer than two and a half seconds, adding roughly twenty-four seconds of extra wait time across six shots. The story screenshot showing the title screen was likely caused by an immediate grab of a frozen X image from page load; four seconds of continued animation should update the display buffer.\n\nI considered moving the mouse during the settle wait to force a composite, but that would alter the before-status snapshot in the launch test. I will skip the mouse move.\n\nI will add a 2500 ms wait inside shot() and a luma check with retries. I will also dump luma values on the existing images to confirm which ones are black before rerunning. A results capture is still pending if time allows.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-ba3e3985-a17d-48f7-b480-115d5bd7789d-81","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"python3 - << 'PY'\nimport subprocess\nfrom pathlib import Path\nroot = Path(\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer\")\nfiles = [\n    root/\"launch-1\"/\"title.png\",\n    root/\"launch-2\"/\"title.png\",\n    root/\"shots\"/\"title.png\",\n    root/\"shots\"/\"about.png\",\n    root/\"shots\"/\"story.png\",\n    root/\"shots\"/\"op.png\",\n    root/\"shots\"/\"dev.png\",\n]\nfor f in files:\n    proc = subprocess.run(\n        [\"ffmpeg\",\"-v\",\"error\",\"-i\",str(f),\"-f\",\"rawvideo\",\"-pix_fmt\",\"gray\",\"-\"],\n        capture_output=True)\n    data = proc.stdout\n    n = len(data)\n    if not n:\n        print(f.name, \"NO PIXELS\", proc.stderr[-200:])\n        continue\n    s = sum(data)\n    # center 200x200\n    # assume 1920x1080\n    w,h=1920,1080\n    if n != w*h:\n        print(f.name, \"size\", n, \"mean\", s/n)\n        continue\n    c=0; cs=0\n    for y in range(440,640):\n        row=y*w\n        for x in range(860,1060):\n            cs += data[row+x]; c+=1\n    print(f\"{f.parent.name}/{f.name} mean={s/n:.1f} center={cs/c:.1f} bytes={f.stat().st_size}\")\nPY","description":"Measure screenshot brightness to spot blank frames"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2794,"output_tokens":3447,"cache_read_input_tokens":118656,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"84231bb2-47ca-46e0-95a2-f57ea3bf67fa"}
