{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_683","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Everything needed for the final reply is ready.\n\nThe reply will lead with the outcome. It will report the four packed about.json sections verbatim, including the testing arrays mapped to their questions. Evidence bullets will follow.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion is packed, and the checks that can run on this machine are done.\n\nThe work folder has the dev game, `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json`, and `Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015.html`. The page’s about-data matches the exit interview. Usage is `added on import`. `session.js` is the first script. Cargo’s target directories are removed. The hub server on port 39337 is stopped.\n\nThe suite printed `13 passed, 0 failed`. At skill 1.0 the bot wins all eight cases and Code Blue. At skill 0 it loses all of them.\n\nTwo fresh `file://` launches, each with a stubbed DualSense, showed the title Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion, music `0.10`, a 1920×1080 canvas, and no page errors. Cross opened the menu the same way both times (`title` focus 0 to `menu` focus 0, device `gamepad`). The title, story, operation, dev, and about shots are painted. The results screen opens only after an operation is won, so that shot is not in the set.\n\nHub harness, one browser at a time: the four-player lossy round passed (`p0 ↑38 ↓77`, `p1 ↑38 ↓78`, `p2 ↑38 ↓76`); host-leave voided the round and did not finish (`p1 ↑7 ↓18`, `p2 ↑7 ↓17`, `p3 ↑7 ↓17`); the server round passed (`p0 ↑54 ↓106`, `p1 ↑54 ↓106`, `server ↑54 ↓106`); the one-player round passed (`p0 ↑15 ↓0`). A client’s aim moved the host’s seat-1 cursor from 0 to 3.42. Vitals were 61.68 on the host and 62.40 on the client, one snapshot apart.\n\n## Source Information\n\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:40:15\n- Source: Trauma Center: Second Opinion\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre: Simulation\n- Perspective: Fixed overhead view of the operative field\n- Tech: Pavilion engine (Shardfall commit 49ef2ba), Rust 1.98.1, wgpu/WebGPU, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129, egui 0.36.2\n- Art direction: Cel-shaded overhead operative field painted per vertex, with layered paper-theatre cards for story\n- Animation: Simulation-driven tissue, instruments, heartbeat, and paper-card motion\n- Audio: Synthesized music, effects, and short gibberish voices. No audio files. Music bus defaults to 0.10\n- Online: Optional AI Hub shared-world Code Blue for 1-4 surgeons\n- Testing: Deterministic sim suite, headless shell, Chromium file:// launches, hub harness\n- Research: Engine seams and the hub protocol, recorded in PLAN.md\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\n- Usage: added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch? Testing caught five issues: a Rust or-pattern guard skipped stage-0 sutures, nearest-hazard selection stole forceps pulls, a sac's swell shared the laser charge meter, hub snapshots waited on a 60 Hz accumulator that never fired in an iframe, and ControlFlow::Poll cancelled the pending animation frame so an operation froze after one tick.\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking? 5\n- What was the most helpful test you ran? The most helpful checks were a skill-1.0 bot on every case from a fresh scene, and the hub harness traffic counters once the frame loop actually ticked.\n- What was the least helpful test you ran? The least helpful test was a temporary print probe that only dumped stuck hazards and was removed once the selection bug was fixed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine? The Game hook, recorded input, RenderFrame, headless egui, and the single-file packer.\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong? Yes. A colon in the project path makes rustc fail joining LD_LIBRARY_PATH, and the demo frame path captures the camera, pause menu, and touch layer unless a game returns first.\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself? The operative rules, the plate mesh, the ten-foot shell, the cue and music buses, and the hub shim.\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine? Keeping a surgery scene off the demo boot panel, stats, guide, toasts, and the engine pause menu.\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? Added the surgery frame, surgeon input, and cue event; a view emit and camera shot; a product frame branch; audio buses and formants; session.js injection in the packer; and js-sys. On the web the surgery loop waits for animation frames, because Poll cancelled the pending frame. The hook alone cannot draw, play cues, or join a hub.\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be? A first-class per-game frame path, so a new game does not have to outrun the demo loop.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about this prompt? The tested camera, light, and palette numbers, and a hub shim that the harness had already accepted.\n- What was confusing? The deliverable folder name must contain a colon, which Cargo cannot use as its working directory.\n- What was the hardest thing to do? Shipping one packed page that is the full game, a hub client, and a file:// WebGPU build.\n- What would you change in this prompt? Say at the start that the Cargo target directory cannot contain a colon."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2907,"output_tokens":1469,"cache_read_input_tokens":158720,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"9cb6b77f-a5b4-4692-a8e3-ea327a820fb8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_684","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I will read STAGE.md and proceed with the tasks outlined in PLAN.md and TASK.md.\n\nThe evaluator says the goal is incomplete. I need to drive a skill-1.0 bot through a finished operation, save a 1920×1080 screenshot, write balance.txt for skills 1.0, 0.7, 0.4, and 0, and re-run the suite so its log shows N passed and 0 failed. I will check what already exists.\n\nThe product does not auto-run the bot during a normal operation. I need to find a way to manually trigger the skill-1.0 bot and capture the results screenshot.\n\nThe native sim has a `run_bot` function, but the target was deleted so rebuilding is impossible. The existing dev mode \"Bot plays\" does not actually drive the simulation. I am now examining how attract mode runs the bot as a possible workaround.\n\nAttract mode reboots instead of showing results. The packed HTML also skips bot execution during normal operations. I need to find a way to drive the shipped code and capture the screenshot.\n\nI am now weighing a headless render path or enabling the bot directly in the product to capture the screenshot. The native test cannot render at 1920×1080 without a GPU.\n\nThe `bot_plays` flag is currently non-functional. Wiring it to actually run the bot would require a WASM rebuild. The native app also needs xvfb for headed rendering. The burst function steps with manual input rather than bot input. I am now weighing these options to find the cleanest path that drives the shipped code.\n\nI will wire the Dev Mode \"Bot plays\" toggle to feed `game.bot()` input during operations. This requires a WASM rebuild, which conflicts with the prior no-retune decision. I need to read the code to find the cleanest path.\n\nI will check whether suite.txt and balance.txt already exist. The deleted .harbor-target forces a rebuild, so I will run the native suite first to generate balance output, then pack only if code changes are needed for the screenshot.\n\nHeadless egui rendering could produce a 1920×1080 PNG, but the evaluator requires an actual skill-1.0 bot to finish the operation first. I will confirm that `bot_plays` is currently unwired.\n\nI will wire the Dev Mode \"Bot plays\" toggle to call the skill-1.0 bot during operations, rebuild WASM, and launch the product to capture a screenshot after the bot finishes. A 2.3-second operation at skill 1.0 needs roughly 23 frames because each frame is capped at 0.1 seconds.\n\nI will read the sim's `run_bot` and the product frame loop to see how the bot is invoked. I also need to verify whether the native window path requires the same code change, since the native suite does not exercise the browser. I will examine the code carefully before making any edits.\n\nHeadless wgpu rendering is too complex. I will use the existing packed page instead.\n\nI need to read the relevant code and check for existing balance.txt and suite.txt files.\n\nThe todo list must keep at least one task in_progress.\n\nAny change to bot wiring requires a targeted test that confirms the bot finishes and the results screen appears.\n\nThe screenshot script itself validates the results UI, while the native suite covers the simulation. I will re-run the suite and regenerate balance.txt to ensure fresh logs. The plan is to read the bot integration points, wire the Dev Mode toggle if it is unwired, and confirm whether existing tests already cover bot wins.\n\nI will change the code, rebuild the packed HTML, capture the results screenshot, and then run the native suite. The suite does not need the WASM build.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The results shot is still missing, and the suite and balance logs need a fresh run. I’ll drive the skill-1.0 bot through a finished operation and capture that screen."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-9f143bcb-9f6c-4be7-a835-8feafa61a61e-136","name":"todo_write","input":{"todos":[{