style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:40:15\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/TASK.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"SOURCE: Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall/blob/main/START_HERE.md, making a new game on its engine. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\nScope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:40:15\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\nTesting Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"total_lines\":151}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-541226c7-4dfe-4412-a81d-14f60f6b8fa1-3","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# Plan: Build the original surgical-action drama in workspace PLAN.md and hand in the TASK.md package\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. The work folder contains `PROGRESS.md`, a dev subfolder, and beside it one self-contained HTML file whose basename is `{GameTitle}-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015` (the same name as the dev subfolder). `{GameTitle}` is one playful riff on the source name, chosen with a real RNG and stored so the title screen, page title, hub card, and About screen match. `exit-interview.json` in that same root parses as one object in TASK.md’s exact shape: `usage` is exactly `added on import`; feedback arrays follow the question order; `aiHub` is filled. The page embeds those answers in `<script type=\\\"application/json\\\" id=\\\"about-data\\\">` and the About screen shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback. Shipped game content has no TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features, and does not use the source’s world (Hope Hospital, Caduceus, Stiles, GUILT, Healing Touch, COOL/XS, or its other proper names).\\n2. From each case’s own start, a deterministic sim (no GPU) plays surgeon slots on a fixed overhead field: eight-way tool select with no menu; Scalpel, Forceps, Suture, Suction, Laser, Gel, Scanner, IV Push, plus contextual Cryoprobe, Dressing, and Defibrillator, each with its own gesture, rating, and misuse; the hazards, vitals 0–100, one heartbeat clock (BPM rises as vitals fall), on-beat window, Code with timed defibrillation, chains, and ranks C/B/A/S/ENCORE; gifts the Hush, the Echo, and the Duet. Cases 0–7 (The First Movement, original cast and R.E.Q.U.I.E.M. strains Introit, Lacrimosa, Dies Irae, Libera Me) and Code Blue each finish under the bot at skill 1.0 and are lost by an idle or random-tool player. A campaign run from its start exercises every tool, hazard, strain phase, gift, and Code. Retry after death restores the last checkpoint. Rewind and replay hashes match.\\n3. The same screens serve a standard-mapping pad and mouse/keyboard (PLAN control table): prompts switch immediately to PlayStation glyphs, Xbox glyphs when that setting is on, or keys/mouse, matching the last device. Title and main menu (Continue, New Game, Case Files, Code Blue, Settings, About) go to attract after 10 seconds without input; any key, mouse move/click, pad button/stick, or tap returns to the title. Touch-only shows “Controller or keyboard required” and a Settings control, with no touch gameplay, and attract still runs. Pause opens Dev Mode with the live balance sliders, defaults marked, and Reset All. Music, effects, and voices are synthesized in code with no audio files; voices are short gibberish syllables; the music bus and Music setting default to 10% (gain 0.10). Story text is original, earnest medical melodrama between operations and short clinical lines during them.\\n10→4. With no hub, the title and campaign run normally and Code Blue plus cases accept 1–4 local surgeons. In a hub session the only round is shared-world Code Blue, seats 1–4, 4 minutes, with the required `hub:session*` tags, unmodified `session.js` as the first script, a pre-op card, host-authoritative snapshots, and a client mirror. The harness passes `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`, the same with `scenario=host-leave`, `players=2&scenario=server`, and `players=1` (auto). A second page’s input moves that surgeon’s cursor on the host, and both pages report the same vitals. `AIHub.server` runs the world without a local slot or drawing.\\n5. The packed HTML starts from `file://` in Chromium (WebGPU software flags). Two consecutive launches each show no page errors, the chosen title rather than Shardfall overlays, a render surface whose drawing size equals the requested viewport and is substantially painted, and a driven pad or mouse input that changes reported game state the same way both times. The single test command prints only failures and one `N passed, M failed` line, and that run is green, including the sim, shell, audio, browser, and hub checks above.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: From the dev folder, run the one suite command (expected `tests/run_all.py` without `python -I`) after it includes real tests that construct each case, tool, hazard, gift, Code, rank, chain, beat window, and checkpoint from the shipped sim’s start state — not a reimplementation or a mid-scene skip. Capture stdout/stderr to `{SCRATCH}/suite.txt`. Must show exit 0, no failure lines, and `N passed, 0 failed`. Must include: bot wins every case and the campaign on 10 seeds at skill 1.0; idle and random-tool play lose every case; snapshot, rewind, and replay hashes match; coverage counters fire for every tool, hazard, strain phase, gift, and Code; content lint parses with no empty line and no TODO; music default gain 0.10; every cue, voice, and track renders non-silent offline.\\n2. gating: The same suite, or a headless UI driver it calls, sends pad, keyboard, and mouse through every screen from the title’s initial state. Observations that must pass: hover moves focus; confirm activates; prompts follow the last device and the Xbox setting; attract starts at 10 seconds of no input and exits on key, pad, and mouse; touch-only shows the required sentence and Settings and no touch controls; dev sliders change live values and Reset All restores defaults; About contains every TASK section and `Usage: added on import`.\\n3. gating: Launch the root HTML from `file://` twice, new browser each time, Chromium with `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, viewport 1920×1080. Stub a standard-mapping DualSense before load. Capture console, `window.__game` status, and a screenshot to `{SCRATCH}/launch-1/` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2/`. Both runs: zero page errors; title is the chosen game name; no engine boot, stats, guide, hint, toast, or touch overlay; canvas drawing size equals 1920×1080 and the painted area covers most of the surface; status reports music 0.10; a stick/button or mouse input changes focus, screen, or cursor in status the same way both times. A blank or partial buffer from a successful readback fails. If the browser cannot start or is killed by the environment, save that log under `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.txt` and then the green sim/shell suite is the bar — do not invent a screenshot.\\n4. gating: Serve the packed game next to the hub harness on `127.0.0.1` and an OS-picked port (`python3 -u -m http.server`). Confirm the body is this game, then require `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and `window.__hubCheck.pass` for the four queries in criterion 4. Then two pages: client aim changes the host cursor for that seat; vitals match. Then one page outside a session: the title is shown and play does not depend on the hub. Write the log to `{SCRATCH}/hub.txt`. Stop only that server PID.\\n5. gating: Parse `exit-interview.json` and the page’s `about-data` block; they must match each other and criterion 1. The HTML basename and the dev subfolder must match the pattern. A search of shipped story, UI strings, and ranks must not find the banned source names in criterion 1.\\n6. evidence: Save 1920×1080 screenshots of title, operation, story, results, dev, and about under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Balance output (wins, time, vitals, suggested par at skills 1.0, 0.7, 0.4, and 0) goes to `{SCRATCH}/balance.txt`. These do not override a green suite.\\n20→\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, optimization, or keeping `engine/target` after the final pack (delete it when the HTML is done).\\n- Copying Trauma Center’s hospital, cast, GUILT strains, Healing Touch star, organisations, UI, music, or rank words. The source name appears only in the filename, Source Information, and the title riff.\\n- A longer campaign than cases 0–7 plus Code Blue. Fewer sets or shorter strain waves are allowed only if every tool, gift, screen, Case 3, and Case 7 remain.\\n- Touch gameplay, audio files, a physical DualSense, real iOS Safari, a second human, a TV, or a listening test. Stand-ins in TASK.md are enough.\\n- Hub modes other than one shared-world Code Blue round, chat, or pause inside a session.\\n- Shardfall’s own pause menu, touch layer, demo overlays, and starter scene as this game’s UI.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n30→Work root only. New dev subfolder; clone `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall` at or after `49ef2ba` inside it; ai-hub `lib/hub` (`session.js` unmodified, plus harness, bridge, round). Engine crates and `scripts/build-web.sh` / `pack_single_html.py` may change. Embed the OFL Barlow Condensed file from the URL in PLAN.md. Playwright 1.63 (Python), Rust 1.98.1, wasm32 target, and wasm-bindgen 0.2.129 are already installed. Research already done for this plan: Second Opinion is pointer surgery, nunchuk/stick tool wheel (~8 tools), vitals and a timer as fail states, gesture ratings and letter ranks up to XS, chapter story around operations, a limited Healing Touch, and defibrillation. PLAN.md’s original remake of those mechanics is the contract, not a remake of the licensed game.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nKeep rules in one deterministic sim the headless tools can tick: surgeon slots, heartbeat, tools, strains, story phases, and the bot live there, with no wall clock, threads, or files. Drive the cursor in real time during the Hush via scaled sim dt, not the engine speed control. Let rendering, egui shell, and audio only read a single frame snapshot plus cue ids, so a browser client can decode the same snapshot. Keep the shell’s input abstract so pad, mouse, and tests share it, including while the sim is paused. Put cases, lines, plates, and rigs in text with file-and-line errors. Expose status, step, and shell commands on `window.__game` for the browser. Pack one HTML that works from `file://`. These are structure hints, not a required layout.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [x] Clone the engine in the dev subfolder, confirm the commit, read its start and make-a-game docs, pick the title with a real RNG, and start `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- [x] Open the sim/input/camera/product seams and ship one overhead operation (field, vitals, heartbeat, one tool, bot win) that also boots in the packed page with the session client in place.\\n- [x] Add every instrument, hazard, rating, chain, Code, death/retry, and cases 0–2 as data; teach the bot; add the cue table and coverage tests.\\n- [x] Add the 10-foot shell: menus, pause, settings, save, glyphs, attract, touch-only, and dev sliders with reset.\\n40→- [x] Add paper-theatre story, music buses at music gain 0.10, and gibberish voices through case 7’s script.\\n- [x] Add the four strains, Hush, Echo, tremor, Cryoprobe, Duet, cases 3–7, local 1–4 play, and Code Blue.\\n- [x] Add the hub shim, card, mirror scene, and host snapshots; pass the harness and the two-page cursor/vitals check.\\n- [x] Balance, write `exit-interview.json` and the about block, pack the single HTML to the work root, delete `engine/target`, and save the green suite plus two launch captures under `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md allows cutting sets and strain length if time runs out, but forbids cutting any tool, gift, screen, Case 3, or Case 7. A build that drops a forbidden piece is not done.\\n- The HTML basename must contain the source string `Trauma Center: Second Opinion`, including the colon. Do not sanitize it away.\\n- The title is chosen at implementation time, so this plan cannot name the file. Match the pattern.\\n- Headless WebGPU pixels need the swiftshader flags; a successful blank capture is a game failure. If earlyoom or a missing display kills the browser, keep the log and use the sim/shell bar only in that case.\\n50→- Other agents share this machine. Do not `pkill`, `killall`, or kill a port you did not bind; stop only PIDs this work started. Keep files inside the work folder.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# Plan: Build the original surgical-action drama in workspace PLAN.md and hand in the TASK.md package\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. The work folder contains `PROGRESS.md`, a dev subfolder, and beside it one self-contained HTML file whose basename is `{GameTitle}-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015` (the same name as the dev subfolder). `{GameTitle}` is one playful riff on the source name, chosen with a real RNG and stored so the title screen, page title, hub card, and About screen match. `exit-interview.json` in that same root parses as one object in TASK.md’s exact shape: `usage` is exactly `added on import`; feedback arrays follow the question order; `aiHub` is filled. The page embeds those answers in `<script type=\\\"application/json\\\" id=\\\"about-data\\\">` and the About screen shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback. Shipped game content has no TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features, and does not use the source’s world (Hope Hospital, Caduceus, Stiles, GUILT, Healing Touch, COOL/XS, or its other proper names).\\n2. From each case’s own start, a deterministic sim (no GPU) plays surgeon slots on a fixed overhead field: eight-way tool select with no menu; Scalpel, Forceps, Suture, Suction, Laser, Gel, Scanner, IV Push, plus contextual Cryoprobe, Dressing, and Defibrillator, each with its own gesture, rating, and misuse; the hazards, vitals 0–100, one heartbeat clock (BPM rises as vitals fall), on-beat window, Code with timed defibrillation, chains, and ranks C/B/A/S/ENCORE; gifts the Hush, the Echo, and the Duet. Cases 0–7 (The First Movement, original cast and R.E.Q.U.I.E.M. strains Introit, Lacrimosa, Dies Irae, Libera Me) and Code Blue each finish under the bot at skill 1.0 and are lost by an idle or random-tool player. A campaign run from its start exercises every tool, hazard, strain phase, gift, and Code. Retry after death restores the last checkpoint. Rewind and replay hashes match.\\n3. The same screens serve a standard-mapping pad and mouse/keyboard (PLAN control table): prompts switch immediately to PlayStation glyphs, Xbox glyphs when that setting is on, or keys/mouse, matching the last device. Title and main menu (Continue, New Game, Case Files, Code Blue, Settings, About) go to attract after 10 seconds without input; any key, mouse move/click, pad button/stick, or tap returns to the title. Touch-only shows “Controller or keyboard required” and a Settings control, with no touch gameplay, and attract still runs. Pause opens Dev Mode with the live balance sliders, defaults marked, and Reset All. Music, effects, and voices are synthesized in code with no audio files; voices are short gibberish syllables; the music bus and Music setting default to 10% (gain 0.10). Story text is original, earnest medical melodrama between operations and short clinical lines during them.\\n10→4. With no hub, the title and campaign run normally and Code Blue plus cases accept 1–4 local surgeons. In a hub session the only round is shared-world Code Blue, seats 1–4, 4 minutes, with the required `hub:session*` tags, unmodified `session.js` as the first script, a pre-op card, host-authoritative snapshots, and a client mirror. The harness passes `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`, the same with `scenario=host-leave`, `players=2&scenario=server`, and `players=1` (auto). A second page’s input moves that surgeon’s cursor on the host, and both pages report the same vitals. `AIHub.server` runs the world without a local slot or drawing.\\n5. The packed HTML starts from `file://` in Chromium (WebGPU software flags). Two consecutive launches each show no page errors, the chosen title rather than Shardfall overlays, a render surface whose drawing size equals the requested viewport and is substantially painted, and a driven pad or mouse input that changes reported game state the same way both times. The single test command prints only failures and one `N passed, M failed` line, and that run is green, including the sim, shell, audio, browser, and hub checks above.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: From the dev folder, run the one suite command (expected `tests/run_all.py` without `python -I`) after it includes real tests that construct each case, tool, hazard, gift, Code, rank, chain, beat window, and checkpoint from the shipped sim’s start state — not a reimplementation or a mid-scene skip. Capture stdout/stderr to `{SCRATCH}/suite.txt`. Must show exit 0, no failure lines, and `N passed, 0 failed`. Must include: bot wins every case and the campaign on 10 seeds at skill 1.0; idle and random-tool play lose every case; snapshot, rewind, and replay hashes match; coverage counters fire for every tool, hazard, strain phase, gift, and Code; content lint parses with no empty line and no TODO; music default gain 0.10; every cue, voice, and track renders non-silent offline.\\n2. gating: The same suite, or a headless UI driver it calls, sends pad, keyboard, and mouse through every screen from the title’s initial state. Observations that must pass: hover moves focus; confirm activates; prompts follow the last device and the Xbox setting; attract starts at 10 seconds of no input and exits on key, pad, and mouse; touch-only shows the required sentence and Settings and no touch controls; dev sliders change live values and Reset All restores defaults; About contains every TASK section and `Usage: added on import`.\\n3. gating: Launch the root HTML from `file://` twice, new browser each time, Chromium with `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, viewport 1920×1080. Stub a standard-mapping DualSense before load. Capture console, `window.__game` status, and a screenshot to `{SCRATCH}/launch-1/` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2/`. Both runs: zero page errors; title is the chosen game name; no engine boot, stats, guide, hint, toast, or touch overlay; canvas drawing size equals 1920×1080 and the painted area covers most of the surface; status reports music 0.10; a stick/button or mouse input changes focus, screen, or cursor in status the same way both times. A blank or partial buffer from a successful readback fails. If the browser cannot start or is killed by the environment, save that log under `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.txt` and then the green sim/shell suite is the bar — do not invent a screenshot.\\n4. gating: Serve the packed game next to the hub harness on `127.0.0.1` and an OS-picked port (`python3 -u -m http.server`). Confirm the body is this game, then require `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and `window.__hubCheck.pass` for the four queries in criterion 4. Then two pages: client aim changes the host cursor for that seat; vitals match. Then one page outside a session: the title is shown and play does not depend on the hub. Write the log to `{SCRATCH}/hub.txt`. Stop only that server PID.\\n5. gating: Parse `exit-interview.json` and the page’s `about-data` block; they must match each other and criterion 1. The HTML basename and the dev subfolder must match the pattern. A search of shipped story, UI strings, and ranks must not find the banned source names in criterion 1.\\n6. evidence: Save 1920×1080 screenshots of title, operation, story, results, dev, and about under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Balance output (wins, time, vitals, suggested par at skills 1.0, 0.7, 0.4, and 0) goes to `{SCRATCH}/balance.txt`. These do not override a green suite.\\n20→\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, optimization, or keeping `engine/target` after the final pack (delete it when the HTML is done).\\n- Copying Trauma Center’s hospital, cast, GUILT strains, Healing Touch star, organisations, UI, music, or rank words. The source name appears only in the filename, Source Information, and the title riff.\\n- A longer campaign than cases 0–7 plus Code Blue. Fewer sets or shorter strain waves are allowed only if every tool, gift, screen, Case 3, and Case 7 remain.\\n- Touch gameplay, audio files, a physical DualSense, real iOS Safari, a second human, a TV, or a listening test. Stand-ins in TASK.md are enough.\\n- Hub modes other than one shared-world Code Blue round, chat, or pause inside a session.\\n- Shardfall’s own pause menu, touch layer, demo overlays, and starter scene as this game’s UI.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n30→Work root only. New dev subfolder; clone `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall` at or after `49ef2ba` inside it; ai-hub `lib/hub` (`session.js` unmodified, plus harness, bridge, round). Engine crates and `scripts/build-web.sh` / `pack_single_html.py` may change. Embed the OFL Barlow Condensed file from the URL in PLAN.md. Playwright 1.63 (Python), Rust 1.98.1, wasm32 target, and wasm-bindgen 0.2.129 are already installed. Research already done for this plan: Second Opinion is pointer surgery, nunchuk/stick tool wheel (~8 tools), vitals and a timer as fail states, gesture ratings and letter ranks up to XS, chapter story around operations, a limited Healing Touch, and defibrillation. PLAN.md’s original remake of those mechanics is the contract, not a remake of the licensed game.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nKeep rules in one deterministic sim the headless tools can tick: surgeon slots, heartbeat, tools,