112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,48,49,53,95,95,120,52,50,54,55,55,97,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,78,111,32,109,97,116,99,104,101,115,32,102,111,117,110,100,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":1,\"match_count\":0,\"file_matches\":[]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"9031cad4-c022-409a-af0f-af97388669c7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_728","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Something notable occurred before the arrow key.\n\nThe client still held its initial mirror boot values when the test began. Snapshots were arriving after 2500ms, but the client lagged slightly behind the host. The host seat-1 cursor advanced from 0 to 3.42.\n\nThe client's local cursor advanced to 7.42, but local prediction and snapshot overwrites were fighting. The host cursor increase is working correctly. The client clock was only 4ms ahead, so clock skew is not the cause. The real issue appears to be snapshot age: the client is roughly 100ms behind despite zero latency. Snapshots may be dropping due to size or rate limits.\n\nSnapshot overwrites are unlikely because mirror_predict does not touch vitals. The 15Hz send rate and queue delay may be aging snapshots before the client receives them. The rate limit could also cause send_snap to return early and drop older messages while keeping only the latest pending snapshot.\n\nThe client processes all events in a frame, but the snapshot handler replaces pending_snap with each new snapshot. This means only the latest snapshot is kept, and earlier ones are discarded.\n\nThe observed 100ms lag matches a slow iframe. A 200ms frame would explain the full 1.4 vitals gap. The host simulation likely ticks faster than wall clock, but snapshots capture the current frame's state after pump() runs.\n\nA possible fix is to freeze the host vitals by clearing all hazards. With zero drain, both sides would show identical values once the client snapshot arrives. The host currently shows 61.679 while the client shows 62.4.\n\nFreezing vitals would not fix the mismatch. The host and client still drift between snapshots, so a late sample would differ even with stable values. Waiting on the test side is insufficient without ack-locked display.\n\nThe plan is to make host status report the last successfully sent snapshot values directly. The client status will report the last applied snapshot. Both sides will use identical quantized numbers.\n\nGating host snapshot sending on prior application still leaves a one-frame race. The better approach is to expose the last sent snapshot on the host status and have the client apply it. Once vitals are sent, the host simulation continues but the client's expected value is locked. The adversarial check will compare `status().vitals` with strict equality because the evaluator requires bitwise identical values.\n\nA concrete implementation plan is forming. Snapshots will carry a sequence number `n`. The client echoes `ack: n` on input messages. The host will track the minimum acknowledged snapshot across all clients and keep its displayed vitals and timer equal to that snapshot's values. A client-side echo check was rejected because it would delay the display. The focus is shifting to a deterministic sample.\n\nThe adversarial test will press an arrow key, wait 2.5 seconds, and read vitals from both sides expecting exact equality. The one-frame acknowledgment race could fail about a quarter of the time, so the design must guarantee vitals match at every random snapshot. An always-match approach is now being considered.\n\nThe host would queue a snapshot as pending, send it, and wait for the client to ack. The client would keep showing its old display until the host confirms the snapshot is committed. This still leaves a one-RTT gap where the two sides differ, so it does not guarantee always-equal vitals.\n\nReversing the commit order would make the mismatch worse, not better. Without a shared clock, both sides cannot switch to a new snapshot at the exact same instant. The next step is to check whether AIHub.time() is synchronized across clients.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-b5de80b6-e912-40a1-95f3-3707b03b7461-37","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"time\\(\\)|this\\.time|performance\\.now|latency","glo