d>, [SurgeonInput; 4], bool) {\\n        let op = self.shell.screen_name() == \\\"op\\\";\\n        let stick = self.pads[0].present && self.pads[0].right.length() > 0.12;\\n        let keys_aim = self.key(KeyCode::KeyW)\\n            || self.key(KeyCode::KeyA)\\n            || self.key(KeyCode::KeyS)\\n            || self.key(KeyCode::KeyD)\\n            || self.key(KeyCode::ArrowUp)\\n            || self.key(KeyCode::ArrowDown)\\n            || self.key(KeyCode::ArrowLeft)\\n            || self.key(KeyCode::ArrowRight);\\n        if stick || keys_aim {\\n            self.mouse_owns = false;\\n        } else if self.mouse_moved {\\n            self.mouse_owns = true;\\n        }\\n        let pad0 = self.pads[0];\\n        let pad_nav = pad0.present && (pad0.up || pad0.down || pad0.south || pad0.east || pad0.start || pad0.left.length() > 0.2 || pad0.right.length() > 0.2);\\n        let mouse_fine = self.mouse_moved || self.mouse_click || self.just.iter().any(|k| !matches!(k, KeyCode::F1 | KeyCode::F2 | KeyCode::F3));\\n        let device = if pad_nav && !mouse_fine { Some(DeviceKind::Gamepad) } else if mouse_fine || !self.just.is_empty() { Some(DeviceKind::KeyboardMouse) } else { None };\\n        let was = self.was;\\n        self.was = pad0;\\n        let up = self.ate(KeyCode::ArrowUp) || (pad0.up && !was.up);\\n        let down = self.ate(KeyCode::ArrowDown) || (pad0.down && !was.down);\\n        let left = self.ate(KeyCode::ArrowLeft) || (pad0.left_b && !was.left_b);\\n        let right = self.ate(KeyCode::ArrowRight) || (pad0.right_b && !was.right_b);\\n        let confirm = self.ate(KeyCode::Enter) || self.ate(KeyCode::NumpadEnter) || (!op && self.ate(KeyCode::Space)) || (!op && pad0.south && !was.south);\\n        let back = !block_back && (self.ate(KeyCode::Escape) || (!op && pad0.east && !was.east) || (pad0.start && !was.start));\\n        let any = device.is_some() || self.mouse_moved || self.touch_click || self.mouse_click || back || confirm || up || down;\\n        let pointer = if op { None } else { Some([self.mouse.x, self.mouse.y]) };\\n        let click = !op && (self.mouse_click || self.touch_click);\\n        let tab_l = self.ate(KeyCode::PageUp) || self.ate(KeyCode::BracketLeft);\\n        let tab_r = self.ate(KeyCode::PageDown) || self.ate(KeyCode::BracketRight);\\n        let cmds = self.shell.step(ShellIn {\\n            up: !op && up,\\n            down: !op && down,\\n            left: !op && left,\\n            right: !op && right,\\n            confirm: !op && confirm,\\n            back,\\n            tab_l,\\n            tab_r,\\n            pointer,\\n            click,\\n            any,\\n            dt,\\n            device,\\n        });\\n        let confirm_sim = cmds.iter().any(|c| matches!(c, Cmd::ConfirmSim));\\n        self.tools_from_hands(op, local);\\n        let mut surgeons = [SurgeonInput { tool: TOOL_KEEP, ..SurgeonInput::default() }; 4];\\n        if op {\\n\",\"total_lines\":886}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"a54020fb-18c0-4a92-bac2-4a874b659168"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_733","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"A cleaner solution will make vitals identical at a comparison point and for most of the time.\n\nHost reports live unquantized vitals while the client reports a quantized snapshot one delivery behind. The fix latches both sides to 0.1 quantized values on 500ms hub time buckets. The client ignores older buckets. The test polls until host and client vitals match and the cursor moves. Hub time is also needed on the View.\n\nThe host will not advance its latch until the snapshot is sent. The client applies the latched value from each snapshot as it arrives. The only remaining mismatch window is delivery delay, which the test simply waits through.\n\nThe snapshot will carry a sequence number `n`. The host embeds `snap: n` in its status, and the client embeds `snap: applied_n`. The test waits until both snapshots match and vitals match. The host increments `n` only when the 500ms latch bucket changes, not every send.\n\nThe test will poll for up to three seconds after the arrow is held for one second. The client status snap ID will be stored in SurgeryFrame so both sides can read it from the frame. The host will not write latched vitals directly into the simulation state.\n\nThe `status_json` function will accept optional `vitals_override` and `timer_override` parameters. The host passes its latched value through this override, while the client passes `None` so it uses the snapshot vitals set by `apply_mirror`. On the client, applying the snapshot happens before the pump, ensuring the status reflects the latched values. The host updates its latch after the pump but before sending the snapshot.\n\nThe host status must use the latched value rather than live vitals. Both sides will derive their 500ms buckets from AIHub.time() so they stay synchronized. The client does not need its own clock; it simply displays the snapshot it receives and discards stale packets by ignoring lower bucket IDs.\n\nThe first snapshot will latch immediately because the bucket counter starts at i32::MIN. Before any send, the host reports live vitals while the client may still show its default value; the test waits until both sides are in play and vitals match. The View struct will gain a time_ms field, and both its idle and wasm constructors will be updated to supply it.\n\nThe Harbor struct will store `net_vitals`, `net_timer`, and `net_bucket` fields. The client does not use them; only the host status reads them. The `snap_json` signature must change to accept vitals and timer overrides. I need to check the call site and how SurgeryFrame stores vitals.\n\nThe snapshot JSON will include a `bucket` field so the client can discard stale packets. The client will store an `applied_bucket` counter on its Harbor and only apply a snapshot if its bucket is at least as new as the last applied one. This prevents cursor updates from being skipped when a stale snapshot arrives.\n\nA `tenth` helper will quantize host vitals to 0.1 before latching. The client will re-apply snapshots from the same bucket even when vitals are unchanged, so cursor updates still flow through. The host will only update its latch while hub time is non-negative, skipping latches during card animations.\n\nThe host will compute its 500ms bucket from `hub.time_ms / 500.0` and floor it to `i32`. It will skip advancing the bucket when time is negative or non-finite, and instead send the previous latch or the current tenth-quantized value.\n\nThe `t` variable from the poll loop will be stored as `hub_ms` in View, clamped to -1.0 if non-finite. Native idle construction will pass -1.0, and wasm construction will pass `self.hub_ms`. The `status_json` call site will supply the host’s latched vitals and timer overrides from `app.harbor.net_vitals` and `net_timer`, while the client passes None.\n\nThe host will update its 500ms bucket latch immediately before sending the snapshot, so the status read can pull from the freshly latched harbor fields.\n\nThe host only updates its 500ms latch when the bucket changes or the first snapshot is sent. If the initial `net_vitals` is `None`, `snap_json` falls back to tenth-quantized live vitals for both the snapshot and status. The client will skip applying a snapshot if its bucket ID is older than the last accepted one, but this risks stalling cursor updates.\n\nThe initial client vitals of 70 will align with the host latched value after the first snapshot. The extra integer in JSON is negligible, and the packet stays under 1400 bytes. Native tests do not exercise the wasm hub, so no native unit test is needed; the browser two-page check serves as the real-path validation.\n\nThe browser test is the only validation path. I will rebuild the wasm target after packing HTML, which takes roughly eighty seconds. The hub.txt file will be overwritten once the test passes. The HTTP server is still stopped and must be started on 127.0.0.1 port zero. Playwright scratch files will go to /tmp/hbr-pw because of socket path length limits.\n\nThe cursor script will poll until host seat 1 vitals are exactly equal and its x coordinate increases. It will print the values and fail on timeout. The host seat-1 x coordinate should move from zero toward 3.42. Both sides must be in play before polling begins.\n\nThe host will also latch the timer so both vitals and timer can be compared in the log. If the timer fails to match, only vitals must align.\n\nA potential floating-point serialization bug is being traced: the host quantizes vitals to f32, writes them to JSON, and the client reads them back from the snapshot. The exact round-trip precision needs verification.\n\nThe f32 round-trip through JSON is safe. serde_json serializes f32 as f64, and parsing back to f32 preserves the original bit pattern. The client will not see a different vitals value from the host.\n\nThe host will pass the already-quantized f32 directly into snap_json and status_json, skipping a redundant tenth() re-quantization. This guarantees identical JSON text across both sides so JavaScript `===` comparisons work reliably. bpm and phase remain live fields and are not part of the check.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-2159ffaa-719c-41da-b906-81b3822894fb-45","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"View \\{|View::idle","glob":"**/hub.rs"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-2159ffaa-719c-41da-b906-81b3822894fb-46","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_app/src/product.rs","offset":1,"limit":62}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2223,"output_tokens":3320,"cache_read_input_tokens":115328,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"e71ab7cb-2bb3-4a21-866c-b61e19767918"}
