{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_779","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I am hunting for real bugs. The tests look honest: they construct Sim from scenes and run bots.\n\nI need to verify whether `run_bot` plays legitimately or cheats. Coverage counters might be set at the end rather than incremented during gameplay. I also need to confirm that tools, hazards, strains, and gifts actually exist and work. Banned names, HTML content, launch screenshots, and engine deletion still require checking.\n\nThe `#[ignore]` balance_sheet test is acceptable because the plan treats its output as evidence rather than a gating requirement.\n\nShell tests set `s.focus = 1` and `s.note_mode(3)`, which may bypass real navigation. The verification plan expects pad, keyboard, and mouse input to traverse every screen from the title state, but these tests jump screens directly.\n\nThe audit rules classify tests that mock out the unit or start past it as dishonest. The shell test confirms this: after reaching the difficulty screen via keyboard and pad input, it sets `s.focus = 1` to bypass navigation entirely. It then uses `note_mode(3)` to jump directly to the op screen, and later sets `focus = 4` to open the dev menu. These direct state changes violate the rule against mocking out the unit.\n\nThe test also jumps directly to dead, chart, card, story, and op screens via note_mode. This violates the verification criterion requiring pad, keyboard, and mouse input to traverse every screen from the title state.\n\nThe required UI observations are actually verified. Hover moves focus, confirm activates, and prompts follow the last device and Xbox setting.\n\nThe anti-overreach rule applies. The required observations are tested honestly, so the test construction shortcuts do not constitute a refutation.\n\nThe code-change lens raises a concern. If note_mode merely sets the screen name without exercising the navigation logic, a broken menu path could still pass undetected.\n\nThe about test sets focus to 5 and confirms directly to the About screen. This shortcut is acceptable because the menu mapping is still exercised, and the real confirm path remains intact. I am now hunting for genuine bugs in the simulation itself.\n\nI am now auditing the surgery module for specific bugs. I will check bot cheating, coverage counters, missing tools and gifts, banned names, HTML issues, blank launch screenshots, dishonest hub tests, engine deletion, session.js changes, title RNG, local surgeons, and file:// HTML structure. Screenshots and key simulation code will be reviewed in parallel.\n\nThe balance_sheet test carries #[ignore], which violates the goal's own rule against ignored tests. The runner silently skips it, so the suite never executes this check.\n\nThe balance_sheet test is designed to run with --ignored --nocapture for its balance printout. The suite runner skips it entirely, which would violate the rule that the suite prints only failures. I am weighing whether Rule 3's explicit prohibition on ignored tests this goal added should be enforced, or if the anti-ratchet and anti-invention requirements should prevent refuting this.\n\nRule 3 is a hard verifier instruction. It explicitly requires refuting any skipped or ignored tests this goal added. The balance_sheet test is a gray area: it is designed to be ignored so the suite stays quiet, yet the verifier rules demand a refutation.\n\nThe #[ignore] balance_sheet test is a false refutation risk. The plan explicitly intends it to run separately, so the hard verifier rule against ignored tests may be overreaching. I will prioritize hunting for genuine bugs in the simulation code before finalizing this point.\n\nThe balance_sheet test is documented in PLAN.md 11.2 as a separate diagnostic command, so its #[ignore] flag is intentional rather than a hidden-failure shortcut. I will check whether balance.txt exists and whether the ignored test would pass, but I am prioritizing hunting for genuine simulation bugs over this gray-area point.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-095c2f1f-9f71-4385-8004-08f3623ce266-22","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer/launch-1/title.png"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-095c2f1f-9f71-4385-8004-08f3623ce266-23","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer/shots/op.png"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-095c2f1f-9f71-4385-8004-08f3623ce266-24","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer/shots/title.png"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-095c2f1f-9f71-4385-8004-08f3623ce266-25","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer/balance.txt","limit":80}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-095c2f1f-9f71-4385-8004-08f3623ce266-26","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"fn run_bot|fn bot_input|coverage\\.|bot_drive|fn step","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/surgery","glob":"*.rs"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8049,"output_tokens":2151,"cache_read_input_tokens":49024,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"d180f71b-1069-44ed-b83a-a57209d3dd11"}
