on-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/src/frame.rs\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":310,\"content\":\"    pub surgery: Option<std::sync::Arc<crate::games::surgery::SurgeryFrame>>,\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"7694ba42-b27c-402c-8ef4-5d9379162115"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_838","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Cloning the Arc repository. Checking product.rs for other octant usages.\n\nOnly tools_from_hands uses octant. tray_tool is only in surgery_frame. shape_stick is dead code. HazardView ToolSite fields are public. Slices work for references. product.rs and ops.rs pass slices correctly. Checking echo_copy next.\n\nEcho_copy repeats the same tool. This is not our bug. Do not touch sac or suture.\n\nWas_use and contextual are fine.\n\nCRYO early return has a flaw. If the shell sends tool 8 after swapping to CRYO, hands returns early even if the player moved off the ember.\n\nThe one-frame CRYO delay is acceptable. A real bug remains: if the shell sends CRYO while the player stands on a nodule that needs laser, the early return keeps CRYO and blocks laser. Case 7 nodules and embers are separate phases. Code blue waves can spawn both together.\n\nThe shell can send CRYO based on last frame's ember position while the cursor has moved to a bleeder. The sim keeps CRYO for one frame and suture fails. The worse case is the same-frame swap: the shell replaces the ring with CRYO using last frame's position, but the sim already sees the new cursor target. The sim should re-evaluate regardless of the incoming tool, yet the shell already swapped the ring.\n\nThe shell replaces the ring with CRYO or DRESSING before sending, so the sim never sees the original laser tool. The fix is to stop early-returning for CRYO and DRESSING from the input. This lets the sim always resolve the true cursor target.\n\nThe shell should keep its internal tool as the raw ring value (0-7). The emitted SurgeonInput.tool can be contextual_tool(ring, position, hazards), which may be 8 or 9. The sim hands() must always run contextual_tool on the incoming tool. If the incoming tool is already 8, falling back to LASER is wrong when the player used a scanner.\n\nA concrete sim-side fix is to fold an already-resolved CRYO or DRESSING back to its original ring value. This lets a later aim point undo the swap. The author notes that a bot explicitly sending CRYO at an ember would still work.\n\nMapping CRYO back to LASER is safe for bots. A bot sending CRYO while slightly outside the ember radius still resolves to CRYO once it enters range, because contextual_tool re-evaluates the true cursor position.\n\nA bot at distance 0.55 while the ember radius is 0.5 still resolves to CRYO because the contextual reach is 0.65. Between 0.58 and 0.65 the tool stays CRYO but heat nearness is 0.58. If the bot sends CRYO while far from an ember the tool becomes LASER for those ticks. This has no effect because the charge aux decays when the bot is not near. The same applies to DRESSING while walking.\n\nMapping DRESSING back to its ring is safe. A bot can send DRESSING while walking and still paint at the dressing station once it enters range. DEFIB should remain untouched. Skill 0 tool selection can produce CRYO.\n\nFolding CRYO back to LASER is acceptable. Skill 0 random cryo becomes laser unless near an ember, which slightly reduces accidental cryo use but remains a loss. Pressing digit 9 sets tool 8 in the shell, yet contextual folding reverts it to laser away from an ember; pressing 9 on an ember correctly resolves to cryo. The skeptic already ruled out digits as the pad solution.\n\nFolding CRYO back to LASER does not break scanner-originated cryo. The sim re-evaluates near the ember and upgrades back to CRYO. Folding DRESSING back to GEL is also safe. The sim re-evaluates near the dressing station and restores DRESSING. When only dressings remain, returning DRESSING immediately still works.\n\nrust\nlet ring = match ring {\n    CRYO => LASER,\n    DRESSING => GEL,\n    DEFIB | TOOL_KEEP => return ring,\n    other => other,\n};\n\nFolding tools in the simulation prevents sticky-locking CRYO. The shell emits the contextual result, and the sim re-evaluates it. If the shell uses a stale position while the sim has a new one, the sim wins. Tests that send CRYO still resolve to clear because the sim upgrades back to CRYO when aiming at an ember.\n\nThe test must send the contextual return value, which is tool 8. The simulation folds this into laser and then upgrades back to cryo near an ember. The test will also assert that a raw laser ring clears, proving the auto-swap inside hands() works for pre-resolved tools too.\n\nThe test exercises the real simulation path by setting the tool to contextual_tool's return and stepping the live sim. This validates the shipped code flow, not just source-level shell logic. product.rs calls the same functions, so the test covers the actual runtime behavior.\n\nA single tick with raw LASER aimed at an ember will assert the tool becomes CRYO, proving hands() auto-swaps. The test can then drive the clear using only ring tools 0-7, letting the simulation handle the swap. SurgeonInput.tool must only be set to 8 or 9 as the return value of contextual_tool.\n\nThe test will include caps and panic messages with hazard debug. A step-by-step suture simulation is being traced to verify stitch detection and engagement logic.\n\nA three-tick suture sequence is confirmed. The second tick triggers a stitch immediately because the needle and side signs differ, finishing in two samples. After suture completes, the stage advances to 1 and the bone becomes dead.\n\nRotating during forceps must stop once the auxiliary value drops below 12. A single tick can overshoot from positive 38 to negative, which breaks the work condition and halts the operation. The loop should only rotate while `aux.abs() >= 12` and then hold forceps.\n\nCap loops will be capped at 600 ticks and assert. A `find` helper for hazards is being drafted, but it cannot hold a reference across `step` because the simulation mutates state; the author is switching to copying the needed hazard fields into a local struct instead.\n\nThe author finalized the hazard lookup helpers: `live` clones an alive hazard by kind, and `by_id` retrieves a specific one. They confirmed that killing only a dressing station leaves IV untouched, so phase clearing does not trigger prematurely. They also noted that `HazardView` lacks a `Default` implementation, which may affect test setup. With these details settled, they are now proceeding to write the files.\n\nThe author will construct the `HazardView` struct by filling in its fields directly. They are adding a new test module named `tests` inside `pav_view/surgery/mod.rs`, and the test filter will be `surgery::tests`. They confirmed that `pav_view` exposes `surgery` as public, so the test path resolves correctly.\n\nThe author will run the new pav_core test first for faster iteration, then the view test, then the full suite, and finally pack. They will confirm engine-link exists before each cargo command and avoid overlapping builds. A scratch directory will hold test output. After the suite and packing finish, targets will be deleted because HTML changes require re-packing and re-running hub tests and file:// launches.\n\nThe author mapped out a strict seven-step verification chain: write code, run targeted cargo tests for the new surgery and view tests, run the full suite, pack WASM, delete targets before Chromium launches, run hub four queries plus two-page vitals, and finally file:// launches if the pack changed. They noted that product.rs still maps digits 8 and 9 to tool 8, which is harmless because the pad path does not use them.\n\nThe author is reviewing `product.rs` to write the exact replacement for `tools_from_hands`. They confirmed `glam` is a direct dependency and will use `glam::Vec2`. The existing octant logic will be copied exactly from the pad left stick. They are also checking whether the pad left stick Y axis is flipped before the octant calculation.\n\nThe pad left stick Y axis is not flipped before the octant calculation, so the existing `octant(pad.left)` call will remain unchanged. For the view test, a coarse plate emits roughly 1344 vertices, which fits comfortably within the expected bounds of 100 to 8000. A full non-quiet emit would produce about 100,000 vertices, but that is acceptable in a unit test and can be used to verify the quiet path is much smaller.\n\nThe view test will assert that the quiet scene contains vertices and SDFs, with fewer than 8000 vertices to confirm the stall mesh was skipped. It will place an ember at (-1.2, 0.8) and a cursor at (2.0, -1.0), then verify both positions appear in the SDF list.\n\nMode 8 will be tested alongside mode 3 in a single emit, since the host runs quiet mode 3 while the mirror uses mode 8. Tray SDFs at z=5.4 will still appear in mode 3, preserving cursor and ember visibility. The plate function writes vertices into `DynamicMesh.data.vertices`. The comment in `frame.rs` will be updated to describe the hub copy as a coarse field without shadow or post-processing. The author also noted that `AIHub.server` might still call `emit` if it has a canvas.\n\nThe coarse plate is small enough to run safely on the server iframe without stalling. A 48×28 grid with only a few hazards produces roughly 1344 vertices, and the per-hazard math is trivial. The harness server scenario passed before with no drawing at all, so adding this cheap field should still finish within the 20-tick limit.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8cdea11b-2fed-48ae-9bdf-bd4f99bfa1e6-79","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_core/tests/surgery.rs","offset":150,"limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8cdea11b-2fed-48ae-9bdf-bd4f99bfa1e6-80","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: 