{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_856","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Checking whether the shell is already in the work directory.\n\nConfirmed the working directory is work. The .harbor-target folder was deleted and will be recreated by Cargo. I will run the targeted test for pav_core with a long timeout, since compiling from scratch may take several minutes.\n\nI will set the test timeout to 240 seconds. If compilation is still running, I will poll for completion.\n\nThe empty target means dependencies will rebuild from scratch, potentially taking 5–10 minutes. I am weighing a 10-minute background block against a monitor script to track progress.\n\nI will run the cargo test in the background and tail its output with a monitor. This lets me review code while compilation proceeds.\n\nI will run the targeted test for `shipped_mapping_clears_an_ember_and_a_dressing` in `pav_core` with a single thread and four build jobs. The output goes to `scratch/map-test.txt`. I am writing a monitor script that polls the cargo process PID every five seconds until it exits.\n\nThe tool returns a task ID rather than a process PID, so I will capture the PID by having the background command write its own PID to a file. I will also use block_until_ms 0 and poll the log for \"test result:\" or \"error[\" to detect completion.\n\nI will run the cargo command directly with block_until_ms 0 so it stays in the background. A monitor script will poll the log file for a line starting with EXIT: and report DONE or FAILED once compilation finishes.\n\nThe monitor script will check the log every 15 seconds for \"error\" lines. It will print FAILED immediately if compilation errors appear, rather than waiting for the process to exit.\n\nI will wait for the cargo process to exit before reporting a failure. Early detection of compile errors would leave cargo running in the background, so I will poll silently until the EXIT line appears.\n\nI will run the mapping test first, then the view test, and finally the full suite. I am checking that rustc is available and no other cargo process is using the target directory.\n\nI am checking for running cargo processes before compiling. I reviewed the frost loop logic and confirmed that contextual_tool returns CRYO while holding the ember, so the assertion holds correctly. The stage advancement after release is also verified.\n\nRelease inside the frost loop breaks without checking stage. If the ember is killed by overheat, alive_id becomes None and the panic correctly signals that frost did not hold.\n\nContextual_tool returns SCALPEL for the scalpel on the ember stage 1. The scanner and laser inputs are properly set to scalpel via mapped. Grab returns FORCEPS at the center with ring forceps.\n\nThe pull point at 0.9 is outside the 0.5 radius, so contextual_tool still returns FORCEPS and the grab clears correctly. The focus selection logic keeps the original ember when it is grabbed, even if another stage-0 ember is closer. The same behavior applies to the bone case.\n\nThe suture needle lands correctly on the side of the bone. The nearest hazard is the bone, not the IV. The bone aux value stays at 38 because spawn does not overwrite it.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The mapping and the hub draw are in. Next I’ll run the new case from a fresh scene and fix whatever the sim actually does."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-24920b4f-ecd3-42df-8ebc-285f00982cc2-121","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"mkdir -p /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer && cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/engine-link && CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/.harbor-target CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo test -p pav_core --test surgery shipped_mapping_clears_an_ember_and_a