{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_895","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Beginning the host-leave scenario by starting the round.\n\nThe host leaves roughly 1.5 seconds after play begins, about a second after the countdown ends. The log shows the host left at 16.23 seconds, which aligns closely with the expected timing.\n\nThe host-leave scenario shows zero game traffic during the first 1.5 seconds of play. Both remaining players sent and received zero packets because the host was removed before any snapshots or inputs were exchanged.\n\nInvestigating why the first 1.5 seconds of play produce no packets while longer games do. Possible causes include WebGPU startup delays, late hub round boot, client input gating, or the coarse plate making each frame exceed 1.5 seconds.\n\nThe coarse plate and full scene setup likely make the first frame after boot extremely slow. Pipeline compilation and heavy rendering probably exceed the 1.5-second warmup window, so the host leaves before any packets are sent or received.\n\nThe host-leave check runs immediately after the host cell is removed, before the second play phase begins. The p4 scenario waits until after that second phase to verify traffic. This timing difference explains why the shorter warmup produces no packets while longer games do.\n\nThe traffic check occurs ten seconds after the host leaves. If the host never sent a snapshot during the first 1.5 seconds, clients likely never send inputs and the round becomes void. The first ~1.5 seconds of actual play is the only window for bidirectional traffic.\n\nThe randomly() delay uses wall-clock time, not frame time, so the 1.5-second warmup does occur. The key question is when hub.play becomes true: at the start message or after the countdown. If play begins immediately at 10.84, there is roughly 5.4 seconds before the host leaves, which would explain why traffic can flow during longer games.\n\nThe scenario might be frozen rather than merely slow. Four simultaneous WebGPU contexts drawing heavy plates could stall the animation frame loop for over five seconds. The p4 test passed with identical hardware, so the failure likely stems from a frame stall under load.\n\nThe host-leave scenario removes the host after only 1.5 seconds of play, leaving no room for a slow first frame to catch up. The p4 scenario keeps the host for the full round, so traffic eventually flows once the host stays. The puzzle is why p4 passes while host-leave fails when both share the same brief warmup window.\n\nClients also sent no input during the 1.5-second warmup and the 10-second post-leave phase. This suggests the game loop never started sending inputs, or stopped immediately after the host left.\n\nThe zero traffic likely stems from a frozen game loop across all four copies. GPU load may stall the animation frame for over five seconds, so nothing is sent during the brief warmup. After the host leaves, three copies remain, but the freeze persists.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-2ebe3bb2-a100-4c32-9d29-34bc7290cc10-6","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_app/src/hub.rs","offset":180,"limit":180}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-2ebe3bb2-a100-4c32-9d29-34bc7290cc10-7","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"stats\\.sent|k === 'g'|play\\b|onStart|seeded","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/web/hub_shim.js"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-2ebe3bb2-a100-4c32-9d29-34bc7290cc10-8","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"sent \\+|stats\\.sent|kind === 'g'|k:'g'|function poke|playMs|TIMINGS","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v