{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_900","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"AIHub.time() returns the current time minus goAt.\n\ngoAt is set when the start message arrives. play begins once time() reaches zero. The host leaves roughly 2.5 seconds after countdown ends, so the game must send its first message within that window. A blocked main thread could delay the send. gfx.draw of the quiet scene might stall the thread on swiftshader, though it is typically synchronous in wasm.\n\nIf the host's first frame after t>=0 is stuck inside boot_hub_round/pump, send_snap never fires. The host iframe could be destroyed while that frame still runs, blocking the send. Clients would also freeze in their first heavy pump before the 'end' message arrives. The contextual_tool addition is cheap and plate rendering happens after send.\n\nThe frame loop runs draw at the end of each animation frame, so the send should fire before the next frame begins. If boot is slow enough to kill the iframe mid-boot, the new code path might be causing a WASM trap or panic during hub initialization that kills the frame loop entirely.\n\np4 passed with the same boot path, so hub initialization is not the culprit. The failure is a race condition: packets flow after the first ~2.5 seconds of play but not before. The host leaves at 2.5 seconds, which is too short, while p4's 3-second delay works. Both send_input and send_snap should fire immediately on the first frame once play begins, before drawing.\n\nThe 2.5-second gap likely means no animation frame runs at all right after play begins. The previous frame's draw may still be blocked in the GPU. The first frame that should start sending is also stuck inside boot or pump before it can send, and the host iframe is destroyed while that frame is still running. In p4, that same first frame eventually finishes and sends, so the check passes after about 4.5 seconds of play. The leave check at 2.5 seconds kills the iframe during that blocked first frame.\n\nThe 3-second delay likely comes from the previous frame's draw of the quiet plate. If that draw blocks for roughly 3 seconds, the next frame cannot start until it finishes. The play-detecting frame is therefore delayed past the 2.5-second leave threshold.\n\nThe 3-second draw delay likely comes from the full 420x240 plate being built rather than the intended 48x28 quiet scene. Shadows may still be active, or the quiet flag might not be set on the frame that gets rendered. I need to trace how SurgeryFrame.quiet is copied from hub_quiet and how apply_look handles it.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-eb521ae6-a213-4157-854b-737496e0ecc7-19","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"quiet","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates","glob":"*.rs"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3765,"output_tokens":1295,"cache_read_input_tokens":63744,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"9fb7b9f8-8858-4b25-8ddf-f06ba60c1d13"}
