k-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a%2Fwork/01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2/terminal/call-2afae021-9b27-4848-ae0c-b6ac3c5d6a99-51.log\",\"total_bytes\":7204}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"559929f6-1fcc-490f-b464-5844e7b49a87"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_916","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All three remaining iframes have `isHost: False`.\n\nThe host iframe was removed. The remaining clients sent only two messages each, both after the host had already left. The first input arrived at 30900ms, but hub time was already 9.8 seconds. The bridge likely rejected these late messages.\n\nThe host never sent a snapshot. Console logs show no iframe 0 activity at all, since the iframe was destroyed before any sends could be captured.\n\nThe 9.8 second delay is caused by iframe boot time. The WebGPU adapter initializes serially and takes over five seconds per extra iframe. Some copies only finish booting at 13.5 seconds of page time. The harness waits for all copies to report ready before starting the round.\n\nThe round began at page time ~21.1s. The host left at ~23.6s, yet clients waited until hubT=9.8 to send. They had nine seconds of idle hub time after the host departed with no simulation running.\n\nThe frame loop may have been stuck for 9.8 seconds after goAt, producing only one play frame. The boot-to-go gap of about 7.5 seconds was likely spent drawing the card and countdown. If each frame takes two to three seconds, the clients could not have kept up with the 2.5 second window. Earlier card timers were accurate, so the main thread was not blocked continuously. The adapter initialization is what appears to be causing the stall.\n\nThe host never called AIHub.send before being removed. The old build sent at roughly one second of play, but the current plate makes each frame so expensive that the first play frame does not complete until ten seconds of hub time. Hub time advances on wall-clock performance.now(), not on frames.\n\nThe ten-second stall may come from four iframes each building a 48×28 mesh and drawing on SwiftShader. The first operation frame's pipeline could also be the bottleneck. A 420×240 render was ruled out because hub_quiet is set to true during boot.\n\nA bug could let the first operation frame render the full 420×240 plate if `hub_quiet` is not set before publish in the same tick. That would mean roughly 100,000 vertices per frame across four contexts. The logs say release build, so 50–200 ms per frame seems unlikely. Ten seconds is too long for that alone. The real stall may be a failure to keep `hub_quiet` true. I need to verify the running game actually uses quiet mode.\n\nThe __game status probe does not expose the quiet flag, so a rebuild is needed to verify it. Re-examining the code path shows that saw_card sets hub_quiet via exec, which on wasm publishes a frame immediately and should keep subsequent pumps in quiet mode. boot_hub_round also re-sets it.\n\nA more likely culprit is a stale view frame from before quiet was enabled. Building that single 420×240 plate could block the thread for ten seconds while the host leaves.\n\nThe vertex shader is unlikely to be the bottleneck. Ten thousand powf calls in title mode would take only about one hundred milliseconds, not ten seconds.\n\nA better explanation is SwiftShader software rasterization. Rendering roughly two hundred thousand triangles per frame on a CPU-bound backend could take several seconds. With four iframes sharing that CPU load, the total stall reaches ten to twenty seconds.\n\nThe ten-second stall likely comes from the countdown frame drawing the full plate. That frame starts before play begins but finishes only after ten seconds, so the first play frame cannot start until the host has already left. The send therefore arrives on the next frame, which explains the nine-point-eight-second delay.\n\nThe old code skipped the mesh entirely when quiet mode was active, sending one packet. The new code draws a 48×28 plate and sends zero, with the first send arriving at nine-point-eight seconds. That is roughly seventy-five times more vertices than the old coarse plate, so the stall cannot be the mesh cost alone. The vertex shader loops over every hazard for every vertex, which may explain the quadratic slowdown.\n\nThe hazard count is not the bottleneck. The suite passed, so thousands of hazards are not spawning. The renderer needs inspection.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8f671ee4-ba6c-427a-8c88-042ee0842558-52","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_view/src/build.rs","offset":1420,"limit":80}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8f671ee4-ba6c-427a-8c88-042ee0842558-53","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"fn draw|dynamic","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_render/src/renderer.rs"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2973,"output_tokens":2287,"cache_read_input_tokens":115456,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"2addca19-b3a2-471e-9d70-5882f455cf38"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-8f671ee4-ba6c-427a-8c88-042ee0842558-52","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1420→                            style_of(o.visual.look, settings.style),\\n                            settings.particles,\\n                        );\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n        if let Some(creature) = crate::creatures::interpolate(prev, curr, alpha) {\\n            puppets.push(creature.id.0 + 2);\\n            if let Err(error) = crate::creatures::emit(&mut scene, &creature, style_of(Look::Lit, settings.style)) {\\n1430→                eprintln!(\\\"creature preview: {error}\\\");\\n            }\\n        }\\n        self.emit_carry.retain(|(id, _), _| live.contains(id));\\n        if let Some(g) = &curr.game {\\n            self.arpg.emit(&mut scene, curr, g, alpha, time, dt, settings.particles, cam_fwd);\\n        }\\n        // Projectiles: drawn where they are between ticks (they move in straight lines).\\n        let back = (1.0 - alpha) * curr.dt;\\n        for p in &curr.projectiles {\\n1440→            let mut sd = SdfInstance::sphere(p.pos - p.vel * back, p.radius, v3(p.color));\\n            sd.style = Style::Unlit;\\n            sd.emissive = 0.6;\\n            sd.flags = rs::flags::NO_SHADOW | rs::flags::NO_CUT;\\n            sd.group = 0x7f1;\\n            scene.sdfs.push(sd);\\n        }\\n        self.emit_effects(&mut scene);\\n        scenery.sort_unstable();\\n        mark_objects(&mut scene, env_end, &scenery);\\n1450→        if settings.style == StyleOverride::PerObject {\\n            restyle_parts(&mut scene, settings.style_objects, settings.style_environment);\\n        }\\n        if f.pixel_art > 1.0 {\\n            let hero = curr.player.map(|p| p.0 + 2);\\n            mark_pixel_art(&mut scene, |g, flags| match f.pixel_target {\\n                PixelTarget::All => true,\\n                PixelTarget::Characters => puppets.contains(&g),\\n                PixelTarget::Hero => Some(g) == hero,\\n                PixelTarget::Others => puppets.contains(&g) && Some(g) != hero,\\n1460→                PixelTarget::World => !puppets.contains(&g),\\n                PixelTarget::Entity => g == f.pixel_entity as u32 + 2,\\n                PixelTarget::Objects => flags & rs::flags::OBJECT != 0,\\n                PixelTarget::Environment => flags & rs::flags::OBJECT == 0,\\n            });\\n        }\\n        lock(&self.materials).attach(&mut scene);\\n        if let Some(op) = curr.surgery.as_deref() {\\n            crate::surgery::emit(&mut scene, op);\\n            crate::surgery::apply_look(&mut scene, rig, aspect, &op.camera, op.quiet);\\n1470→        }\\n        scene\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n/// Flags the characters and objects: every instance emitted after the environment (`from`:\\n/// meshes, SDFs, dynamic meshes) except fixed scenery (outline groups in `scenery`, sorted).\\nfn mark_objects(scene: &mut Scene, from: (usize, usize, usize), scenery: &[u32]) {\\n    let object = |g: u32| scenery.binary_search(&g).is_err();\\n    for m in &mut scene.meshes[from.0..] {\\n1480→        if object(m.group) {\\n            m.flags |= rs::flags::OBJECT;\\n        }\\n    }\\n    for s in &mut scene.sdfs[from.1..] {\\n        if object(s.group) {\\n            s.flags |= rs::flags::OBJECT;\\n        }\\n    }\\n    for d in &mut scene.dynamic[from.2..] {\\n1490→        if object(d.group) {\\n            d.flags |= rs::flags::OBJECT;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n/// Gives the characters and objects, and the environment, a surface style of their own\\n/// (unlit markers, decals and glows keep theirs).\\nfn restyle_parts(scene: &mut Scene, objects: StyleOverride, environment: StyleOverride) {\\n    if objects == StyleOverride::PerObject && environment == StyleOverride::PerObject {\",\"content_concise\":\"1420→                            style_of(o.visual.look, settings.s