            model: mat(m.transform),\\n                color: m.color.extend(m.emissive).to_array(),\\n                params: [m.style as u32, m.flags, m.group, self.materials.param(m.material)],\\n                ext: m.ext.extend(0.0).to_array(),\\n            });\\n            match batches.last_mut() {\\n                Some(b) if b.0 == m.mesh => b.2 = idx + 1,\\n                _ => batches.push((m.mesh, idx, idx + 1, 0, 0)),\\n            }\\n        }\\n        // Dynamic meshes: one instance each (identity transform), after the regular ones.\\n        let mut dyn_draws: Vec<(u32, u32, i32, u32, bool)> = Vec::new(); // first index, count, base vertex, instance, shadow\\n        {\\n            let mut verts: Vec<Vertex> = Vec::new();\\n            let mut idx: Vec<u32> = Vec::new();\\n            for d in &scene.dynamic {\\n                if d.data.indices.is_empty() {\\n                    continue;\\n                }\\n                let inst = gpu_meshes.len() as u32;\\n                gpu_meshes.push(GpuMeshInstance {\\n                    model: mat(Mat4::IDENTITY),\\n                    color: d.color.extend(d.emissive).to_array(),\\n                    params: [d.style as u32, d.flags, d.group, self.materials.param(d.material)],\\n                    ext: [0.0; 4],\\n                });\\n                dyn_draws.push((\\n                    idx.len() as u32,\\n                    d.data.indices.len() as u32,\\n                    verts.len() as i32,\\n                    inst,\\n                    d.flags & crate::scene::flags::NO_SHADOW == 0,\\n                ));\\n                verts.extend_from_slice(&d.data.vertices);\\n                idx.extend_from_slice(&d.data.indices);\\n            }\\n            if !dyn_draws.is_empty() {\\n                self.dyn_vertices.write(&self.device, &self.queue, bytemuck::cast_slice(&verts));\\n                self.dyn_indices.write(&self.device, &self.queue, bytemuck::cast_slice(&idx));\\n            }\\n        }\\n        let main_batches = batches.len() + dyn_draws.len();\\n        let main_triangles =\\n            batches.iter().map(|b| (b.2 - b.1) as usize * self.meshes[&b.0].index_count as usize / 3).sum::<usize>()\\n                + dyn_draws.iter().map(|d| d.1 as usize / 3).sum::<usize>();\\n\",\"total_lines\":1521}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"ceb20798-fcb9-48c7-ad7a-aff3a35537f7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_919","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The early return in build() when surgery is quiet is the correct fix.\n\nWorld geometry and shadows are the real bottleneck. The old path was fast enough for one packet because it only built the world. Adding the plate pushed it over. Skipping the world and using a small plate should fix this. The hub view will show only the operative plate, which is acceptable for a surgery game.\n\nThe plate will remain visible because the dungeon is skipped, not because emit returns early. World meshes are still submitted and shadow-cashed before apply_look clears shadows, so returning before adding them avoids that cost entirely. The implementation adds a quiet-surgery guard to build() that emits only the operative plate.\n\nThe guard belongs at the start of build(), before any heavy work. The 48×28 plate is tiny—only 1,344 vertices and 7,614 indices—so its geometry is not the bottleneck. The 9.8-second probe time must come from something else.\n\nThe 9.8-second probe is likely a one-time pipeline stall at the transition, not a recurring per-frame cost. Adding the plate should not turn a fast frame into a ten-second one unless the first dynamic mesh triggers a shader compile or buffer reallocation that stalls SwiftShader across all contexts. The thirty-millisecond boot renderer time does not explain a ten-second frame.\n\nThe first frames before saw_card is set are likely the culprit. During that mode title phase, emit still draws the full plate even though hub_quiet is not yet active. The quiet plate only appears after saw_card triggers on the first card frame, roughly ten seconds into play. That means the initial stall is caused by the heavy full-plate geometry, not the small quiet plate.\n\nThe pipeline should compile during the card phase before play begins, so the first draw ought to be fast. The 9.8-second delay must therefore come from something specific to OPERATION mode itself, not from the plate geometry.\n\nOperation mode's instanced SDFs and code-blue simulation are too cheap to explain a ten-second frame. The real culprit may be the first operation frame itself: boot_hub_round sets hub_quiet and mode OP, but the render frame could still be from before that call, meaning the full plate is drawn instead of the quiet one.\n\nThe send happens before the draw, so the first play frame should reach the host at hubT≈0 even if the draw itself takes ten seconds. The actual delay likely comes from the previous countdown frame blocking the pipeline for 9.8 seconds, because it also draws the quiet plate. Earlier card frames were fast enough for one-second timers, so this stall is probably specific to the probe load.\n\nThe 9.8-second delay is likely a single blocked countdown frame rather than a recurring per-frame cost. GPU device loss, an SDF transition, or a mode-change mesh generation could explain the stall. The paper() and egui surgery::draw paths are ruled out as unlikely culprits. Another possibility is that `host.pump` catch-up is compounding the delay by running multiple ticks at once.\n\nThe shadow pass is skipped when quiet, but the world meshes are still submitted to the main pass. Even without shadows, a large mesh count could stall SwiftShader. Surgery tick CPU overhead is negligible.\n\nThe 9.8-second probe delay is the gap between goAt (21.1 s) and send time (30.9 s). The old build already rendered the same world successfully, so the world geometry is not the new bottleneck. A logic bug that keeps play false until a later trigger is now being considered.\n\nThe play flag is set correctly at hubT 0, so a logic bug is not the cause. The fix is to make drawing fast by skipping world geometry and tray SDFs when quiet, and shrinking the plate. Sending the snapshot earlier at the top of surgery_frame was considered but rejected because it cannot outpace a blocked draw.\n\nThe quiet plate will shrink to 16×8, yielding 128 vertices. Hazard paint and the normal second pass will be skipped when quiet. Tray tools will also be omitted in that mode.\n\nThe quiet plate will be drawn as a flat two-tone grid on the CPU, skipping vertex_paint entirely. Dynamic meshes will use the NO_SHADOW flag. The early return in build() is safe because emit does not touch materials and apply_look handles lights.\n\nThe early return will be placed immediately after time computation and before library synchronization. It checks `curr.surgery.is_some_and(|op| op.quiet)` to build a minimal scene with only the camera, time, and emitted plate geometry, then returns it directly. The `is_some_and` method is available since Rust 1.70, so no version check is needed.\n\nThe quiet guard now uses `as_deref().filter(|op| op.quiet)` instead of `is_some_and`. Tray SDFs are skipped in instruments when quiet, but the cursor SDF is still drawn for mode 3. The plate builder may also be simplified to skip the normal pass and hazard loop.\n\nThe 128-vertex hazard loop is fast enough, so vertex_paint will remain to preserve the tissue look. The view test calls emit() directly and is unaffected by the build() change. A unit test for the quiet-world skip could be added to pav_view.\n\nThe existing emit test is sufficient, so no extra build() unit test will be added. The next step is to repack WASM and rerun the leave probe, hub four, cursor, and file launch probes. A short deviation clause will be appended to PLAN.md’s third bullet to document that hub frames skip the demo world.\n\nThe early return skips `begin_frame` and `last_time` updates, but the hub stays quiet for the entire session so this is harmless. The clear color is already set by `apply_look`, and the product.rs scene builder path is unaffected.\n\nThe early return keeps CPU build time minimal because only the quiet plate geometry is generated. I will run the view test directly rather than the full six-minute suite, since the suite already includes it and no tests were added or changed.\n\nThe full suite is unnecessary because it tests native code and the packed HTML changes. I will run only the view test, which rebuilds pav_view and its dependencies and takes two to four minutes. The WASM pack also rebuilds wasm and cannot share the target directory with the native build.\n\nThe native test and WASM pack can run sequentially because they use separate target directories. The target will be deleted before the browser launch. Egui draw is ruled out as the bottleneck. A send_snap call at the very start of surgery_frame was considered to send the previous snapshot before any work, but it would not help if the long frame is the countdown frame that began before play.\n\nThe early return makes the countdown frame fast, so the next frame starts within the window. The network send is already placed before scene build and pump, so no earlier insertion is needed.\n\nThe snapshot send will move to immediately after boot and before pump, using the frame already published during boot. This matches how clients send inputs. A shim-level keepalive was considered but rejected to avoid changing protocol behavior.\n\nThe quiet plate will be 16×8, giving 128 vertices and a span from -11 to 11. The early return in build.rs must sit at the very top, immediately after scene creation, because too much work runs before the surgery emit. I will also update the instruments tray guard and the PLAN deviation sentence.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-dbdb0453-0caa-4dfa-8b07-ff78664351ab-58","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"## Deviations","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/PLAN.md","-A":8}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1555,"output_tokens":3909,"cache_read_input_tokens":124288,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"231856c1-2a33-4625-88c8-3732499eda05"}
