\"exit_code\":0,\"command\":\"export TMPDIR=/tmp/hbr-pw PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64\\ncd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer\\nxvfb-run -a -s \\\"-screen 0 1280x800x24\\\" python3 -u hub_probe.py 37019\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Probe when host-leave copies first send\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a%2Fwork/01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2/terminal/call-8bb7ffa8-15cb-4165-a87f-0f5514a90355-88.log\",\"total_bytes\":8876}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"a13fed1b-382c-42a7-9da5-e7babd059a7f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_941","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The probe run passed successfully.\n\nThe probe passed, but the hub_four leave run failed with zero traffic. This suggests a flaky failure. The probe's iframe 0 is not the host and sends inputs first.\n\nThe fix works but is flaky. Timing differences between runs cause the failure. The hub_four run ran after p4 while memory was fragmented. The probe ran alone after p4's browser closed and passed. Re-running leave several times will measure the flake rate.\n\nThe probe's first send arrived at 652 ms, but the failed run left at roughly 2.4 s. This gap of over a second is too wide for reliable detection. The first send must occur well before that window even when four WebGPU contexts boot slowly.\n\nThe probe's frames were only about 150 ms apart, but a single frame in the failing run took over two seconds. That gap is too large to fit inside the one-second send window. Adapter initialization delays may also be pushing the first packet past the deadline.\n\np4 sent 24 packets in the same run, confirming frames are faster than before. Yet the leave still produced zero sends. The bridge never received a send at all. This rules out network loss and points to a deeper cause.\n\nsession.js dropping sends before they reach the bridge was ruled out. The probe wraps AIHub.send and counts calls before the original call, so it would have caught any drops. Because the probe passed, the bridge did receive those packets. The failure is purely a timing issue: the first send simply arrived too late in the failed run. The next step is to make the send happen reliably earlier.\n\nThe Rust side only sends during `surgery_frame`, so a stalled animation frame blocks the first packet. The shim cannot build a snapshot without Rust, which creates a deadlock at startup. The fix is to send immediately when time crosses zero, request redraws more aggressively, and emit on the first poll even if the previous frame is still drawing.\n\nsetTimeout(0) cannot break a stalled draw. Yielding in wgpu is impractical. The game should skip drawing until the first snapshot is sent, but card-drawing frames still run and delay the send. The failed run likely had frames longer than 2.4 seconds or stopped ticking.\n\nThe failed leave log shows card messages arriving roughly once per second. This means the main thread is not blocked for two seconds, so the stall must be specific to the operation scene's first pipeline in SwiftShader. The probe's frames were only about 150 ms, but the bad run likely hit a slow first draw in that scene.\n\nThe first frame before hub_quiet is set may be building the full world and taking many seconds. That single frame could span the entire card and play window, delaying the send until after the leave deadline.\n\nThe first draw should be cheap because the world is already quiet. The gap between 650 ms and zero sends in the same run is unexplained.\n\nA race where the host leaves before clients finish booting was ruled out, because all copies reported ready and ticked during the card phase. The probe used the same send code and succeeded at 650 ms, so the failure is not caused by a broken send path or early returns. Network loss also does not explain the missing packets.\n\nThe probe sent at 650 ms and the check passed, but in the failing hub_four run clients sent nothing before leave at roughly 2.4 s. This confirms the failure is timing-dependent rather than a race condition. I will re-run the leave test alone twice to measure the flake rate.\n\np4's leave run succeeded with 24 packets. The timing matched the expected window closely. This confirms the send path works when timing is right. The hub_four failure is a cold-start flake. Re-running the four queries will verify this. If one still fails, a more aggressive send strategy is needed.\n\np4 accumulated 24 packets over roughly five seconds of play, while the leave test only allows about 2.4 seconds before voiding stops counting. This suggests a startup delay of around 2.5 seconds before the first send fires. The probe contradicts this theory because it sent at 650 ms when running alone, but the failing hub_four run was the second browser in a row and may have suffered from different timing or memory fragmentation.\n\nMemory pressure may explain the flaky failure. The leave test will be re-run now that the machine is idle. If it passes, all four queries will be run together for a clean log; if it fails again, deeper investigation is needed.\n\nA more aggressive fix is to have the shim send a minimal snapshot from JavaScript the instant AIHub fires 'start', bypassing Rust entirely. This would guarantee traffic immediately and satisfy the harness check without waiting for the first render frame.\n\nThe harness only checks that traffic flows both ways, not that snapshots are valid. A dummy JSON message would therefore satisfy the leave check without real gameplay data. The two-page vitals check still requires genuine snapshots.\n\nThe shim can post a real input message via setTimeout when the start countdown elapses, bypassing the animation frame. The host has no snapshot to send yet, so it must wait for Rust.\n\nsetTimeout cannot break a synchronous main-thread stall. The only fix is to prevent the frame loop from blocking at all. The probe passed because its frames were only 150 ms, but the failing run likely hit a slow first draw that exceeded the window. I need to check whether that failure's frames were unusually long.\n\nThe probe's 150 ms frames likely reflect egui and wgpu present overhead on a small plate. Four serialized iframes should total about 600 ms per round, still well under the 2.4 s window. Shader compilation during the card phase should absorb any startup delay, leaving play frames fast. The failure remains unexplained.\n\nThe packed HTML file was confirmed to be in place, so the new build is definitely being served. p4's traffic increased from 15 to 24 packets, which suggests the faster build is active, yet leave still shows zero sends. This inconsistency within the same build needs explanation. I will re-run leave twice; if both pass, I will run the full four queries again and also test hub_cursor and file launch. A code bug that drops sends only when the host leaves quickly seems unlikely.\n\nThe probe passed with a different seed, so the failure is not seed-specific. No JavaScript exceptions were logged. A Rust panic that aborts the frame loop without throwing a window error could explain the missing sends, but p4 also ran code blue with a different seed and sent 24 packets successfully, which weakens that theory.\n\nA shim-side polling loop and a Web Worker were both considered as ways to bypass the main-thread stall, but neither works because the snapshot lives on the main thread. The probe's 150 ms frames suggest the failure is likely a flaky GPU or memory artifact from running p4 immediately before.\n\nA possible fix is to set play = true when the start event fires, rather than waiting for time() >= 0. This would queue the first snapshot during the three-second countdown, giving the host roughly four and a half seconds to send before leaving. The old code delayed play until time() >= 0 so the round would not simulate before the match officially began.\n\nThe fix is to change the play-start condition in hub.rs from `t >= 0.0` to `t.is_finite()`. This makes play begin as soon as the start message arrives, three seconds before the round officially starts. The world will boot then and snapshots can be queued during the countdown.\n\nSimulating during the countdown is acceptable. The hub clock would be three seconds ahead of the official start, but for a four-minute round that is negligible. Clients and host will all begin together when their staggered start messages arrive.\n\nThe harness would not complain about early simulation. It only checks traffic and void on leave, so starting the clock three seconds early is safe.\n\nA better approach is to split the play flag into two: one for sending snapshots and one for running the simulation. The host could queue a snapshot immediately when the start event arrives, but only begin simulating once time() reaches zero. This avoids starting the operation timer three seconds early while still guaranteeing an early send.\n\nThe simplest robust fix is to set play to true as soon as the start event arrives, but pause the simulation until time() reaches zero. This guarantees an early snapshot while keeping the operation timer synchronized with the official round start.\n\nThe pause logic in `hub.rs` is `hold = paused || hub.ended || (hub.active && !hub.play)`. If `play` is set to true as soon as the start event arrives, the simulation will be paused until `time() >= 0`, which is exactly what is needed. The only remaining change is to allow `send_snap` to fire while paused, so snapshots can still be queued during the countdown.\n\nThe concrete implementation introduces an `armed` flag that becomes true as soon as the start event arrives. Snapshot and input sends will check `!self.armed` instead of `!self.play`, so traffic can queue during the countdown. The simulation itself remains paused until `t >= 0`, when `play` finally enables boot and real operation snapshots begin flowing.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Host-leave is a race: the probe run sent at 0.65s and passed, the suite run sent nothing before the host left. I’ll start game traffic when the countdown begins, so a slow first operation frame cannot miss that window."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-ed987b0b-8e19-471b-a949-4ba5f6af8d6c-89","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_app/src/hub.rs","offset":70,"limit":100}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4524,"output_tokens":4972,"cache_read_input_tokens":150912,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"2049fd2f-abc3-4da7-a577-e022b0d2d572"}
