{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_965","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The cursor check failed. The host is in mode 3 with vitals 69.\n\nThe client is in mode 8 with vitals stuck at 80 and timer 0. The host vitals remain latched at 69.6 for four seconds, so the simulation is not advancing. The condition requiring host mode 3, client mode 8, and timer above 200 appears to be blocking updates.\n\nThe timer advanced from 699 to 4902, but those are wall-clock milliseconds from AIHub.time(), not frame time. Status values never changed, so the simulation is frozen after the first operation frame. The draw loop may be hanging or dying once play begins.\n\nboot_mirror sets timer 240 and heart 70, yet the client status still shows vitals 80 and timer 0. The status JSON falls back to operation state when net_vitals is missing, which only arrives after a successful host send. This suggests the mirror snapshot never applied, or the simulation paused before it could update.\n\nThe client status is likely stale because the frame loop froze after the first draw. GPU deadlock would prevent the draw function from returning, leaving status values frozen while AIHub.time() continues advancing in JavaScript.\n\nThe frozen status and advancing timer prove the iframe main thread is not blocked. JavaScript remains responsive, so the draw loop is likely not deadlocking. The simulation is simply not advancing because surgery_frame is no longer being called. The next step is to determine why requestAnimationFrame stopped firing.\n\nThe redraw loop should continue because about_to_wait requests a new frame after every draw. The frozen status despite a live JavaScript thread points to a winit-specific issue where the event loop stops requesting frames.\n\nThe first play frame's draw call may never return. Playwright evaluate would block if the iframe main thread were deadlocked, but it does not. This suggests each iframe has its own event loop, and the parent can still reach into the iframe contentWindow while the iframe thread is stuck.\n\nThe last host frame recorded mode 3, vitals 69.6, and timer 239.9, confirming one operation tick had completed before the loop froze. The focus is now on why requestAnimationFrame stopped scheduling frames after that single operation.\n\nThree specific causes are being considered for the frozen draw loop. Skipping the swapchain during countdown may leave it in a bad state, causing the first real draw to fail or surface loss. The redraw request might also be dropped if the window is not properly redrawn. Another possibility is that the browser throttles requestAnimationFrame because the canvas stays blank during the countdown, though this is puzzling since mode 3 was reached.\n\nThe countdown frames executed successfully. The first draw frame completed and published its status, but the next frame never ran. A panic during that second draw is now suspected as the cause of the loop freeze.\n\nThe egui state and font installation are unlikely culprits. The surface may have been left in an error state after the first draw, causing the second frame to fail. I will check how gfx.draw handles lost surfaces and whether a missing redraw request is the real cause.\n\nThe cursor test requires matching client snapshots and host cursor movement. The host is frozen, so it cannot send new snapshots, leaving the client stuck on initial values. The next step is to examine how winit wasm handles ControlFlow::Wait when a frame takes a long time, since the first operation draw may remain in progress from the browser's perspective while the parent polls.\n\nSkipping the draw call may leave the input queue full, causing winit to stop. The redraw request during countdown might also pile up and make the loop think it is idle. The first real draw could be triggering a quit or window error. Using `curr` and `prev` only inside the draw branch is harmless.\n\nThe host latched vitals at 69.6 and timer at 239.9 because the frame loop froze after the first draw. The client never applied the snapshot, so it remains stuck on boot_mirror values of vitals 80 and timer 0. Mode 8 is not boot_mirror's output; that snapshot would have timer 240 and vitals near 70, not zero.\n\nThe client mode 8 status with vitals 80 and timer 0 does not match boot_mirror's expected values of 70 and 240. The 80 vitals likely come from the card default, not the operation state. This suggests note_mode may have set the screen while op.mode remained at 8, leaving the status snapshot inconsistent.\n\nThe client status falls back to operation state when net_vitals is missing. That state shows mode 8, vitals 80, and timer 0. The snapshot timer was likely reset to zero by apply_mirror.\n\nThe host sends card snapshots during countdown. The client applies them via apply_mirror, which sets mode 8 and copies the snapshot's vitals and timer. This overwrites the real operation state with countdown values. The host later boots the real operation and sends genuine snapshots, but the client has already stopped updating. Both sides freeze for four seconds after that point.\n\nSkipping all GPU presents during countdown likely kills the frame loop once the first real draw runs. A cheap clear-and-egui present should keep the swapchain healthy without the heavy scene. The four-second freeze probably comes from the first play draw being too heavy, causing the script to time out.\n\nThe browser likely throttled requestAnimationFrame during the countdown because no frames were painted. One transition frame then ran and scheduled another, but the heavy draw blocked that next frame and froze the loop.\n\nSkipping all draws is the root cause of the cursor freeze. The leave test passed with cheap frames, but continuous frames are needed after play starts. The winit loop should keep requesting redraws unless the canvas is hidden.\n\nDrawing will be restored for all frames. The countdown send and quiet-world skip remain. A minimal clear every countdown frame should keep the swapchain warm without breaking the ongoing simulation. I am checking whether gfx.draw can accept an empty scene.\n\nA cheap clear-and-egui present during countdown would keep the swapchain warm but likely reduce packet throughput. The cursor test proves that skipping draws during countdown does not prevent the loop from freezing once play begins, so the first real draw is still the culprit.\n\nThe first draw succeeded, so the surface is not stale. A panic during the second draw is now suspected. The egui state may be corrupted or oversized after skipped frames. The plan is to call a lightweight empty-draw every skipped frame while keeping sends, though this might slow the leave test again.\n\nI will explicitly request a redraw at the end of surgery_frame whenever the draw is skipped, in case about_to_wait is not reached. I am also checking the wasm event loop for any path that skips the redraw request. The next step is to test an empty scene every countdown frame while keeping real draws during play; if the cursor still freezes, the bug is isolated to the play draw itself.\n\nI will call a minimal present every countdown frame so the window does not freeze. The plan is to build an empty Scene with default values during countdown and the real scene during play, always drawing it with egui so the card remains visible. I need to verify that Scene is in scope in product.rs.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a085b14b-66ee-49c4-8353-ca3c96c442a0-120","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_app/src/product.rs","limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a085b14b-66ee-49c4-8353-ca3c96c442a0-121","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"struct Scene|pub use.*Scene","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-mat