{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_972","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Checking the wgpu version and web surface present implementation.\n\nThe proposed fix is to always call egui and gfx.draw, but pass an empty Scene during the countdown pass instead of skipping present. The concern is that presenting an empty scene on the first countdown frame might kill the loop immediately. However, sends happen before draw, so the first countdown frame would still send traffic before the present kills the loop.\n\nIf the first present kills the loop, empty scene countdowns would freeze after one snapshot and fail both cursor and host-leave tests. Solo play presents from the first frame without issue. The bug likely involves skipped presents rather than the initial present itself.\n\nThe old quiet plate presented every frame and kept the loop alive. Pack4 skipped presents and then the first present killed it. This suggests a gap in presents breaks something, but continuous presents work. The theory is that wgpu's web surface needs its internal animation frame request to be primed by calling get_current_texture.\n\nThe root cause is a conflict between winit and wgpu's animation frame requests. When get_current_texture runs after the canvas has been idle, it cancels winit's pending redraw and breaks the game loop. Presenting an empty scene every frame during countdown prevents this gap and keeps the loop alive.\n\nEmpty scenes are cheap enough to keep countdown frames fast and preserve the host-leave margin. The old 16×8 plate was too slow, but rerun3 tested it with the demo world already skipped. That rerun occurred after quiet-world skip and armed-during-countdown, yet before skip-GPU.\n\nRe-reading the rerun logs shows that fixes 1 and 2 both still failed with host-leave. Only fix 3, which skips the entire scene build, egui, and gfx.draw during countdown, passed all tests including host-leave.\n\nThe 16×8 plate without the demo world was still too slow for host-leave. An empty scene plus egui might be cheaper, but egui font atlas upload on the first frame could stall. The code installs fonts only once when `app.harbor.font` is false.\n\nThe first play frame completed successfully, so the font install and full scene build are not the cause of the loop death. The failure is confirmed to be the first present after a gap cancelling the animation frame request. The fix is to present an empty scene during countdown to prime the cycle before play, then draw the full scene only during active play.\n\nThe full scene on the first play frame might be too heavy. A timeout in get_current_texture could stop the event loop even though acquire() returns None and about_to_wait still requests redraws. I will read wgpu's web source to verify this. Another option is to stop relying on request_redraw for the hub simulation.\n\nA JS setInterval or requestAnimationFrame could drive the frame loop independently of winit's animation frame, preventing a present from cancelling it. That would be a larger change.\n\nAlternatively, adding a setTimeout fallback inside about_to_wait would restart request_redraw if the animation frame is cancelled. This safety net would fix the stall regardless of the exact root cause. The current code already calls request_redraw on every wait.\n\nA cancelled animation frame leaves the event loop waiting forever, since about_to_wait only runs after events are processed. A setTimeout fallback via wasm_bindgen can call request_redraw to recover.\n\nLeaking a closure every frame is unsafe. A thread-local reused closure would be better. The empty scene during countdown is the cleaner fix and matches the working solo path. The setTimeout fallback is only a backup if empty presents fail.\n\nThe cursor test requires roughly 700 ms of play before it passes. Countdown lasts about three seconds, so the full scene on the first play frame may be the real bottleneck, not the missing countdown presents.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a3574139-6f0f-4842-9e4d-72798d69004e-16","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"fn apply_mirror|fn boot_mirror|fn boot_hub_round","glob":"**/surgery/**/*.rs"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a3574139-6f0f-4842-9e4d-72798d69004e-17","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/Cargo.lock","offset":3620,"limit":30}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a3574139-6f0f-4842-9e4d-72798d69004e-18","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/Cargo.lock","offset":4050,"limit":20}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3550,"output_tokens":1994,"cache_read_input_tokens":47104,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"a2c128f8-b055-4ed8-89a4-6abd46281696"}
