geInterface for WebBufferMappedRange {\\n    fn len(&self) -> usize {\\n        self.get_temporary_mapping().len()\\n    }\\n\\n    #[inline]\\n    unsafe fn read_slice(&self) -> &[u8] {\\n        self.get_temporary_mapping()\\n    }\\n\\n    #[inline]\\n    unsafe fn write_slice(&mut self) -> WriteOnly<'_, [u8]> {\\n        self.temporary_mapping_modified = true;\\n        self.get_temporary_mapping();\\n        let t: &mut Vec<u8> = self.temporary_mapping.get_mut().unwrap();\\n        WriteOnly::from_mut(t)\\n    }\\n\\n    #[inline]\\n    fn as_uint8array(&self) -> &js_sys::Uint8Array {\\n        &self.actual_mapping\\n    }\\n}\\nimpl Drop for WebBufferMappedRange {\\n    fn drop(&mut self) {\\n        if !self.temporary_mapping_modified {\\n            // For efficiency, skip the copy if it is not needed.\\n            // This is also how we skip copying back on *read-only* mappings.\\n            return;\\n        }\\n\\n\",\"total_lines\":4489}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"e9d16da7-a0a0-415f-8871-b1da0d68b0e9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_977","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to understand the winit Wait and request_redraw flow more carefully.\n\nWhen ControlFlow::Wait is active, the runner processes pending redraws and emits RedrawRequested. The app then calls window.request_redraw(), which cancels any existing animation frame and schedules a new one. Finally, apply_control_flow sets the state to Wait.\n\nThe animation loop should continue indefinitely if about_to_wait calls request_redraw. The cursor test stopped after the first play frame instead. A panic in surgery_frame on the second frame could be the cause, since the first frame succeeded.\n\nThe panic likely occurs on the second frame rather than the first. Suspects include egui state.take(), renderer.render, or the surgery simulation tick. The host status may be frozen, which would stop the host loop entirely.\n\nThe host vitals and timer are latched in 500ms buckets. They remain frozen because the client is not sending updates that change the bucket.\n\nThe host status only updates when send_snap succeeds and the 500ms bucket changes. send_snap runs at 15 Hz, so the latch advances every half second if the loop is running.\n\nThe frozen vitals and timer across many 500ms buckets point to three possibilities. The event loop stopped entirely. send_snap returned false every time. Or the simulation is not actually advancing, since the timer should tick downward if it were running.\n\nFrozen vitals and timer do not prove the frame loop stopped. The simulation itself may be paused or holding the timer. A single tick of roughly 0.1 seconds has already occurred.\n\nThe simulation is not paused or ended, so it should tick normally. The first frame's 0.1 second dt likely came from the last countdown frame. Later frames with tiny dt values would not freeze the timer at 239.9 for four seconds.\n\nThe cursor remained at x=0 while ArrowRight was held. This is stronger evidence that the simulation is not stepping than frozen vitals alone. The canvas may have never received focus, so input never reached the simulation even if the loop were running. The test method itself works when the game loops, as shown by an older successful run.\n\nThe frozen timer and cursor together confirm the simulation loop stopped. If input were simply blocked, the timer would still tick downward. I am now checking whether status_json uses a live or latched timer value.\n\nA 1400-byte payload limit could explain the frozen status. If the operation snapshot exceeds that size, send_snap returns false without updating the latched vitals or timer. The simulation might still be running, but the client would never receive fresh updates.\n\nThe first snapshot was likely a small card update during countdown. The successful send of the operation snapshot at play start confirms the payload was under 1400 bytes. If later snapshots exceed that limit, send_snap returns false and the latch freezes, but the simulation could still be running. The test reads cursor position from status JSON rather than the screen.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-226f24a7-fb0f-45ff-8868-c4afb02ad8cc-29","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"fn status_json|cursor","glob":"**/pav_app/src/product.rs"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-226f24a7-fb0f-45ff-8868-c4afb02ad8cc-30","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"cursor|MATCH|vitals|ArrowRight|mode","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer/hub_cursor.py"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3559,"output_tokens":1901,"cache_read_input_tokens":61184,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"875e0e30-4bc1-4036-a445-1b7a2d6ee7d0"}
