{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_980","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The game has not yet booted, so no status is available.\n\nFrames run during the countdown. The host enters mode 3 at t=194 with timer 239.9 and vitals 69.6. The client stays in mode 8 with timer 0 and vitals 80. Status updates every ~500ms while hub time changes. The sample at t=699 captures the pre-freeze state. Everything freezes until t=4902.\n\nThe brief string includes hub time, so it changes every poll even while the game is frozen. Printing does not prove the game updated between t4 and t8. The first real status at t4 with mode 7/8 and vitals 80 is the actual proof.\n\nThe host ran at least one frame after play started. The client may have run countdown frames but never applied an operation snapshot. The log cannot prove whether the countdown continued between t4 and t8.\n\nThe host-leave test passed with 40 sent messages per client. At 15 Hz this equals roughly 2.7 seconds of traffic. The countdown lasts 3 seconds. This confirms the frame loop ran continuously through the entire countdown.\n\nThe host-leave test ends when the host is removed, not when play begins. The loop might die at the first PLAY present, which occurs after the host-leave window. The traffic recorded during that window likely comes from the countdown rather than from the play phase.\n\nThe host-leave test passes because its 40-message threshold is satisfied by countdown traffic alone. The loop likely dies at the start of play, so the check sees only pre-play messages and passes. Cursor fails because it requires actual play-time simulation.\n\nThe first draw frame kills the loop. Empty presents during countdown might prevent this death, but only if the death is triggered by the first present ever. If the death is instead caused by the first heavy present or the first present after a gap, empty countdown presents will not help. Blocking the first present ever would kill the loop at the very start of countdown and break host-leave.\n\nEmpty countdown presents could prime the swapchain and let the heavy present survive. The first operation frame renders a full scene including plate, instruments, hazards, and egui. If that draw panics or cancels the animation frame, the loop dies before the cursor test can run.\n\nDrawing the full plate every frame does not kill the loop. The host-leave test passed because the loop kept running and sent messages later. The death is specifically the first present after frames that skipped presenting. The rerun probe only reported the first send, so it cannot prove the loop survived.\n\nSolo play draws every frame from the start and works, though this was not re-verified on pack4. The cursor log shows the loop dies after a single present following skipped frames. A Chrome bug in wgpu's `getCurrentTexture()` is suspected as the cause.\n\nWebGPU requires the surface texture to be presented before the next `getCurrentTexture` call. If the GPU is still busy from a heavy frame, that call throws an error. The current code catches this and returns None, so the loop should survive. The real danger is an unhandled exception in JavaScript that bypasses the catch block.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-28696876-b3b6-4876-bb9a-b5d112f6060c-34","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"fn pre_present_notify","path":"/home/micha/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/winit-0.30.13"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-28696876-b3b6-4876-bb9a-b5d112f6060c-35","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"present|getCurrentTexture|requestAnimationFrame","path":"/home/micha/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/wgpu-30.0.1/src/backend/webgpu.rs","head_limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1432,"output_tokens":1746,"cache_read_input_tokens":71552,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"6624c846-ff42-4484-b332-70a6bef8af94"}
