,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":3,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_render/src/renderer.rs\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":906,\"content\":\"    fn ensure_targets(&mut self, size: (u32, u32)) {\"},{\"line_number\":1007,\"content\":\"    pub fn internal_size(&self, size: (u32, u32)) -> (u32, u32) {\"},{\"line_number\":1025,\"content\":\"        let size = self.internal_size(full);\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"d2ed1c48-6ab3-42b0-a2e5-52934da3631f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_986","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Beginning a robust fix.\n\nA JS setInterval could replace the split approach to trigger redraws when frames stall. Rendering an empty scene during countdown would initialize the GPU early but risks host-leave. The rAF death theory is being re-examined: it should continue unless a JS exception in gfx.draw unwinds through WASM after status is published.\n\nThe draw call returned normally because status was updated. The rAF callback should have completed and scheduled the next frame. The cause of the missing next frame is still unknown.\n\nA Chrome WebGPU bug may be the culprit. Calling requestAnimationFrame inside getCurrentTexture() ties the next frame to the GPU frame. If present() fails asynchronously, Chrome cancels pending rAFs and they never fire again. A validation error on the quiet scene could explain why submit() succeeds while present() stalls.\n\nA watchdog that only ticks the simulation without touching the GPU would survive a wedged present call. This is the reliable fix for cursor and host-leave. An empty scene might still error if the renderer assumes meshes exist. An animated visual field cannot be shown while the GPU is wedged.\n\nThe plan is to combine both fixes. A setInterval watchdog will request redraws when frames stall past 80 ms, and if the frame remains stale, it will directly pump the simulation without drawing. The developer is now looking at the App structure to find a safe way to call into the simulation. A cleaner approach would be to stop relying on winit's rAF as the sole clock.\n\nThe developer sketched a concrete implementation. A JS setInterval will call `surgery_frame` directly through the global App pointer, bypassing winit's rAF entirely. This interval cannot be suspended by the same Chrome WebGPU bug that kills rAF, so it will keep driving the simulation even if present() wedges.\n\nThe setInterval approach has a risk. If `getCurrentTexture` blocks until the previous frame finishes, the interval callback would freeze the main thread and break polling. A JS exception from that call would be caught by wgpu and return Lost, but blocking is more dangerous.\n\nThe test can still poll, so the loop stopped before the second draw. A setInterval that calls surgery_frame directly will bypass rAF entirely, ensuring the next frame runs even if getCurrentTexture blocks. The developer will install this once on wasm after gfx exists, using a 16 ms interval.\n\nThe rAF path will be kept alongside the setInterval watchdog. A re-entry guard will prevent nested calls. During countdown the interval still drives the simulation without drawing, keeping the scene fast. Once play begins, drawing resumes. The interval keeps calling `surgery_frame` even if the animation frame dies, fixing the cursor and preserving host-leave. Both paths may fire together, but the real-time delta makes this safe.\n\nThe IN_FRAME guard prevents nested calls if winit events re-enter the loop during draw or egui. The interval cannot overlap with the rAF path because JavaScript is single-threaded.\n\nThe App pointer is stable for the program lifetime because `EventLoop::run_app` keeps it on the stack. The developer will capture this pointer once inside `surgery_frame` and store it in a thread-local variable so the setInterval watchdog can safely call into the simulation.\n\nThe developer is now writing the actual setInterval watchdog code. It uses three thread-local cells: one to track whether the interval is already installed, one to store the App pointer, and one to guard against re-entry. The closure checks the IN_FRAME flag before entering the simulation, and it exits early if the App pointer is null.\n\nThe interval is set to 32 ms. The developer noticed that `surgery_frame` is called both by the interval and by rAF, so the IN_FRAME guard must be placed at the very start of the function.\n\nThe IN_FRAME guard must be set at the very start of surgery_frame so both the interval and rAF paths are protected. Because JavaScript is single-threaded, the two paths cannot interleave mid-function; they only run sequentially when one returns. Nested calls are the real risk if something inside the function triggers a redraw synchronously. The winit event loop queues redraws during AboutToWait instead of processing them immediately, which prevents nesting.\n\nThe interval and rAF paths cannot re-enter `surgery_frame` on their own. Combined, they may produce up to 90 ticks per second, but the simulation clamps delta time to 0.1 seconds and rate-limits sends to 15 Hz. During countdown, extra frames could increase host-leave traffic.\n\nA new concern is that if the first `gfx.draw` call wedges while returning normally, the interval will fire again 32 ms later and attempt another draw. This risks repeated GPU calls until the present stall clears.\n\nThe developer will set the IN_FRAME guard inside `surgery_frame` itself so the interval path is also protected. If `getCurrentTexture` blocks on the main thread, the interval will freeze and the test will show a timeout or frozen polls; that failure can be skipped or capped with a draw rate limit. A last-call timestamp was considered but rejected as unnecessary.\n\nA drop guard will reset the IN_FRAME flag if `surgery_frame` panics, preventing the interval from stalling. The interval path does not need to check for a missing `gfx` context because `surgery_frame` already returns early in that case. State handling during interval-driven frames should behave identically to rAF frames. The developer is now weighing whether to keep empty presents during countdown.\n\nHeavy draws will remain skipped during countdown so host-leave stays fast. The interval will keep driving play frames after the first heavy draw, and any blocking on that second draw will be caught by the test. The quiet scene already limits draw cost during hub play, so only the initial shader compile is expensive; subsequent interval frames should be cheaper if they run. The developer is now checking product.rs imports and wasm_bindgen availability in pav_app.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-02846e83-1a78-45c3-a5aa-0b6dfe231f1d-49","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"build-web|CARGO_TARGET_DIR|engine-link","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/tmp/grok-goal-907ccca5ce3b/implementer","glob":"*.txt","head_limit":30}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-02846e83-1a78-45c3-a5aa-0b6dfe231f1d-50","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_app/src/product.rs","limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-02846e83-1a78-45c3-a5aa-0b6dfe231f1d-51","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"build-web","glob":"**/*.{md,sh}","head_limit":20}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3246,"output_tokens":2816,"cache_read_input_tokens":88320,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"496ea35f-ab75-4dca-8907-8cc5e876bf3f"}
