{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-2017546c-05ac-4f35-9920-6d9dc362585e-52","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,48,49,53,95,95,120,52,50,54,55,55,97,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,78,111,32,109,97,116,99,104,101,115,32,102,111,117,110,100,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":1,\"match_count\":0,\"file_matches\":[]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-2017546c-05ac-4f35-9920-6d9dc362585e-53","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"790→PAD = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n(() => { const buttons = Array.from({length: 17}, () => ({pressed: false, touched: false, value: 0}));\\n  const pad = {id: 'DualSense Wireless Controller (STANDARD GAMEPAD)', index: 0, connected: true,\\n               mapping: 'standard', timestamp: 0, axes: [0,0,0,0], buttons};\\n  window.__pad = pad; navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad, null, null, null]; })();\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\npage.add_init_script(PAD)                       # then: page.evaluate(\\\"__pad.axes[0]=1; __pad.timestamp++\\\")\\n```\\nStandard mapping: 0 Cross, 1 Circle, 2 Square, 3 Triangle, 4 L1, 5 R1, 6 L2, 7 R2, 8 Create, 9 Options, 10 L3, 11 R3, 12–15 D-pad, 16 PS; axes 0–1 left stick, 2–3 right. Set `value` as well as `pressed` for triggers. Add a second pad at index 1 for co-op.\\n\\n800→```python\\nTAP = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n(() => { const real = AudioNode.prototype.connect; window.__an = [];\\n  AudioNode.prototype.connect = function (dest, ...rest) { const r = real.call(this, dest, ...rest);\\n    if (dest instanceof AudioDestinationNode) { const c = this.context;\\n      if (!c.__an) { c.__an = c.createAnalyser(); c.__an.fftSize = 2048; window.__an.push(c.__an); } real.call(this, c.__an); }\\n    return r; };\\n  window.__peak = () => { let m = 0; for (const a of window.__an) { const b = new Float32Array(a.fftSize);\\n    a.getFloatTimeDomainData(b); for (const v of b) m = Math.max(m, Math.abs(v)); } return m; }; })();\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n810→```\\n✔ This read a peak of 0.65 from the engine's cpal output during play.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- Case and story tables live in Rust (`content.rs`, `story.rs`), not toml. The sim reads that one table. A toml loader would be a second copy of the same data.\\n- Cargo cannot put the target directory on a path that contains a colon. The dev folder name has one, so web and test builds set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` to `work/.harbor-target` and run from the `engine-link` symlink. That directory is deleted after the final pack.\\n- Hub snapshots still go out at about 15 Hz, cursors included. Vitals and the timer in that snapshot stay on the value from the start of a 500 ms hub-clock bucket, and the host's status reports that sent value only after the send succeeds. A client drops a snapshot from an older bucket. That is what makes both pages report the same vitals at a comparison point.\\n- Hub and mirror copies draw the operative field on a coarse plate and leave shadows, bloom, and GI off. They do not draw the demo world. A 420×240 plate on top of that world stalled the software-GPU iframe. Hands and snapshots start when the countdown starts, so a slow first operation frame still produces traffic before a host-leave. Cryoprobe and Dressing are not ring slots: Laser or Scanner on an ember that wants the probe, and any ring tool on a closure, resolve to those tools.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"790→PAD = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n(() => { const buttons = Array.from({length: 17}, () => ({pressed: false, touched: false, value: 0}));\\n  const pad = {id: 'DualSense Wireless Controller (STANDARD GAMEPAD)', index: 0, connected: true,\\n               mapping: 'standard', timestamp: 0, axes: [0,0,0,0], buttons};\\n  window.__pad = pad; navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad, null, null, null]; })();\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\npage.add_init_script(PAD)                       # then: page.evaluate(\\\"__pad.axes[0]=1; __pad.timestamp++\\\")\\n```\\nStandard mapping: 0 Cross, 1 Circle, 2 Square, 3 Triangle, 4 L1, 5 R1, 6 L2, 7 R2, 8 Create, 9 Options, 10 L3, 11 R3, 12–15 D-pad, 16 PS; axes 0–1 left stick, 2–3 right. Set `value` as well as `pressed` for triggers. Add a second pad at index 1 for co-op.\\n\\n800→```python\\nTAP = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n(() => { const real = AudioNode.prototype.connect; window.__an = [];\\n  AudioNode.prototype.connect = function (dest, ...rest) { const r = real.call(this, dest, ...rest);\\n    if (dest instanceof AudioDestinationNode) { const c = this.context;\\n      if (!c.__an) { c.__an = c.createAnalyser(); c.__an.fftSize = 2048; window.__an.push(c.__an); } real.call(this, c.__an); }\\n    return r; };\\n  window.__peak = () => { let m = 0; for (const a of window.__an) { const b = new Float32Array(a.fftSize);\\n    a.getFloatTimeDomainData(b); for (const v of b) m = Math.max(m, Math.abs(v)); } return m; }; })();\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n810→```\\n✔ This read a peak of 0.65 from the engine's cpal output during play.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- Case and story tables live in Rust (`content.rs`, `story.rs`), not toml. The sim reads that one table. A toml loader would be a second copy of the same data.\\n- Cargo cannot put the target directory on a path that contains a colon. The dev folder name has one, so web and test builds set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` to `work/.harbor-target` and run from the `engine-link` symlink. That directory is deleted after the final pack.\\n- Hub snapshots still go out at about 15 Hz, cursors included. Vitals and the timer in that snapshot stay on the value from the start of a 500 ms hub-clock bucket, and the host's status reports that sent value only after the send succeeds. A client drops a snapshot from an older bucket. That is what makes both pages report the same vitals at a comparison point.\\n- Hub and mirror copies draw the operative field on a coarse plate and leave shadows, bloom, and GI off. They do not draw the demo world. A 420×240 plate on top of that world stalled the software-GPU iframe. Hands and snapshots start when the countdown starts, so a slow first operation frame still produces traffic before a host-leave. Cryoprobe and Dressing are not ring slots: Laser or Scanner on an ember that wants the probe, and any ring tool on a closure, resolve to those tools.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":790,\"limit\":50,\"raw_output\":\"PAD = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n(() => { const buttons = Array.from({length: 17}, () => ({pressed: false, touched: false, value: 0}));\\n  const pad = {id: 'DualSense Wireless Controller (STANDARD GAMEPAD)', index: 0, connected: true,\\n               mapping: 'standard', timestamp: 0, axes: [0,0,0,0], buttons};\\n  window.__pad = pad; navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad, null, null, null]; })();\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\npage.add_init_script(PAD)                       # then: page.evaluate(\\\"__pad.axes[0]=1; __pad.timestamp++\\\")\\n```\\nStandard mapping: 0 Cross, 1 Circle, 2 Square, 3 Triangle, 4 L1, 5 R1, 6 L2, 7 R2, 8 Create, 9 Options, 10 L3, 11 R3, 12–15 D-pad, 16 PS; axes 0–1 left stick, 2–3 right. Set `value` as well as `pressed` for triggers. Add a second pad at index 1 for co-op.\\n\\n```python\\nTAP = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n(() => { const real = AudioNode.prototype.connect; window.__an = [];\\n  AudioNode.prototype.connect = function (dest, ...rest) { const r = real.call(this, dest, ...rest);\\n    if (dest instanceof AudioDestinationNode) { const c = this.context;\\n      if (!c.__an) { c.__an = c.createAnalyser(); c.__an.fftSize = 2048; window.__an.push(c.__an); } real.call(this, c.__an); }\\n    return r; };\\n  window.__peak = () => { let m = 0; for (const a of window.__an) { const b = new Float32Array(a.fftSize);\\n    a.getFloatTimeDomainData(b); for (const v of b) m = Math.max(m, Math.abs(v)); } return m; }; })();\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n```\\n✔ This read a peak of 0.65 from the engine's cpal output during play.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- Case and story tables live in Rust (`content.rs`, `story.rs`), not toml. The sim reads that one table. A toml loader would be a second copy of the same data.\\n- Cargo cannot put the target directory on a path that contains a colon. The dev folder name has one, so web and test builds set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` to `work/.harbor-target` and run from the `engine-link` symlink. That directory is deleted after the final pack.\\n- Hub snapshots still go out at about 15 Hz, cursors included. Vitals and the timer in that snapshot stay on the value from the start of a 500 ms hub-clock bucket, and the host's status reports that sent value only after the send succeeds. A client drops a snapshot from an older bucket. That is what makes both pages report the same vitals at a comparison point.\\n- Hub and mirror copies draw the operative field on a coarse plate and leave shadows, bloom, and GI off. They do not draw the demo world. A 420×240 plate on top of that world stalled the software-GPU iframe. Hands and snapshots start when the countdown starts, so a slow first operation frame still produces traffic before a host-leave. Cryoprobe and Dressing are not ring slots: Laser or Scanner on an ember that wants the probe, and any ring tool on a closure, resolve to those tools.\\n\",\"total_lines\":818}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-2017546c-05ac-4f35-9920-6d9dc362585e-54","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Builds the browser version (WebAssembly + WebGPU). Output: target/web/{index.html,manifest.webmanifest,shardfall.js,shardfall_bg.wasm}\\n# Serve that folder over HTTP (e.g. `python3 -m http.server -d target/web`) and open it in Chrome or Edge.\\n# --single-file writes only target/web/<title>.html (shardfall.html): one page with the glue and gzipped wasm inside\\n#   that runs from file:// or any host (scripts/pack_single_html.py; also --title, --description, --theme-color,\\n#   --scene NAME for the start scene when the address names none, --out PATH).\\n# Needs: rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown\\n#        cargo install wasm-bindgen-cli --version <the wasm-bindgen version in Cargo.lock>\\nset -euo pipefail\\n10→caller=$PWD\\ncd \\\"$(dirname \\\"$0\\\")/..\\\"\\nsingle=false\\npack=()\\nwhile (($#)); do\\n\\tcase $1 in\\n\\t--single-file) single=true ;;\\n\\t--title | --description | --theme-color | --scene | --out)\\n\\t\\t(($# > 1)) || { echo \\\"build-web.sh: $1 needs a value\\\" >&2; exit 2; }\\n\\t\\tvalue=$2\\n20→\\t\\t[[ $1 == --out && $value != /* ]] && value=$caller/$value\\n\\t\\tpack+=(\\\"$1\\\" \\\"$value\\\")\\n\\t\\tshift\\n\\t\\t;;\\n\\t*) echo \\\"build-web.sh: unknown option $1 (options are listed at the top of $0)\\\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\\n\\tesac\\n\\tshift\\ndone\\nif ((${#pack[@]})) && ! $single; then\\n\\techo \\\"build-web.sh: ${pack[0]} needs --single-file\\\" >&2\\n30→\\texit 2\\nfi\\ncargo build --locked --profile dist --target wasm32-unknown-unknown -p pav_app\\nwasm=${CARGO_TARGET_DIR:-target}/wasm32-unknown-unknown/dist/shardfall.wasm\\nout=target/web\\nif $single; then\\n\\tbindgen=$(mktemp -d)\\n\\ttrap 'rm -rf \\\"$bindgen\\\"' EXIT\\n\\twasm-bindgen --target web --no-typescript --out-dir \\\"$bindgen\\\" --out-name shardfall \\\"$wasm\\\"\\n\\tpython3 -I scripts/pack_single_html.py \\\"$bindgen\\\" \\\"${pack[@]}\\\"\\n40→\\texit\\nfi\\nrm -rf \\\"$out\\\"\\nmkdir -p \\\"$out\\\"\\nwasm-bindgen --target web --no-typescript --out-dir \\\"$out\\\" --out-name shardfall \\\"$wasm\\\"\\ncp web/index.html web/manifest.webmanifest \\\"$out/\\\"\\nls -la \\\"$out\\\"\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Builds the browser version (WebAssembly + WebGPU). Output: target/web/{index.html,manifest.webmanifest,shardfall.js,shardfall_bg.wasm}\\n# Serve that folder over HTTP (e.g. `python3 -m http.server -d target/web`) and open it in Chrome or Edge.\\n# --single-file writes only target/web/<title>.html (shardfall.html): one page with the glue and gzipped wasm inside\\n#   that runs from file:// or any host (scripts/pack_single_html.py; also --title, --description, --theme-color,\\n#   --scene NAME for the start scene when the address names none, --out PATH).\\n# Needs: rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown\\n#        cargo install wasm-bindgen-cli --version <the wasm-bindgen version in Cargo.lock>\\nset -euo pipefail\\n10→caller=$PWD\\ncd \\\"$(dirname \\\"$0\\\")/..\\\"\\nsingle=false\\npack=()\\nwhile (($#)); do\\n\\tcase $1 in\\n\\t--single-file) single=true ;;\\n\\t--title | --description | --theme-color | --scene | --out)\\n\\t\\t(($# > 1)) || { echo \\\"build-web.sh: $1 needs a value\\\" >&2; exit 2; }\\n\\t\\tvalue=$2\\n20→\\t\\t[[ $1 == --out && $value != /* ]] && value=$caller/$value\\n\\t\\tpack+=(\\\"$1\\\" \\\"$value\\\")\\n\\t\\tshift\\n\\t\\t;;\\n\\t*) echo \\\"build-web.sh: unknown option $1 (options are listed at the top of $0)\\\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\\n\\tesac\\n\\tshift\\ndone\\nif ((${#pack[@]})) && ! $single; then\\n\\techo \\\"build-web.sh: ${pack[0]} needs --single-file\\\" >&2\\n30→\\texit 2\\nfi\\ncargo build --locked --profile dist --target wasm32-unknown-unknown -p pav_app\\nwasm=${CARGO_TARGET_DIR:-target}/wasm32-unknown-unknown/dist/shardfall.wasm\\nout=target/web\\nif $single; then\\n\\tbindgen=$(mktemp -d)\\n\\ttrap 'rm -rf \\\"$bindgen\\\"' EXIT\\n\\twasm-bindgen --target web --no-typescript --out-dir \\\"$bindgen\\\" --out-name shardfall \\\"$wasm\\\"\\n\\tpython3 -I scripts/pack_single_html.py \\\"$bindgen\\\" \\\"${pack[@]}\\\"\\n40→\\texit\\nfi\\nrm -rf \\\"$out\\\"\\nmkdir -p \\\"$out\\\"\\nwasm-bindgen --target web --no-typescript --out-dir \\\"$out\\\" --out-name shardfall \\\"$wasm\\\"\\ncp web/index.html web/manifest.webmanifest \\\"$out/\\\"\\nls -la \\\"$out\\\"\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/scripts/build-web.sh\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Builds the browser version (WebAssembly + WebGPU). Output: target/web/{index.html,manifest.webmanifest,shardfall.js,shardfall_bg.wasm}\\n# Serve that folder over HTTP (e.g. `python3 -m http.server -d target/web`) and open it in Chrome or Edge.\\n# --single-file writes only target/web/<title>.html (shardfall.html): one page with the glue and gzipped wasm inside\\n#   that runs from file:// or any host (scripts/pack_single_html.py; also --title, --description, --theme-color,\\n#   --scene NAME for the start scene when the address names none, --out PATH).\\n# Needs: rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown\\n#        cargo install wasm-bindgen-cli --version <the wasm-bindgen version in Cargo.lock>\\nset -euo pipefail\\ncaller=$PWD\\ncd \\\"$(dirname \\\"$0\\\")/..\\\"\\nsingle=false\\npack=()\\nwhile (($#)); do\\n\\tcase $1 in\\n\\t--single-file) single=true ;;\\n\\t--title | --description | --theme-color | --scene | --out)\\n\\t\\t(($# > 1)) || { echo \\\"build-web.sh: $1 needs a value\\\" >&2; exit 2; }\\n\\t\\tvalue=$2\\n\\t\\t[[ $1 == --out && $value != /* ]] && value=$caller/$value\\n\\t\\tpack+=(\\\"$1\\\" \\\"$value\\\")\\n\\t\\tshift\\n\\t\\t;;\\n\\t*) echo \\\"build-web.sh: unknown option $1 (options are listed at the top of $0)\\\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\\n\\tesac\\n\\tshift\\ndone\\nif ((${#pack[@]})) && ! $single; then\\n\\techo \\\"build-web.sh: ${pack[0]} needs --single-file\\\" >&2\\n\\texit 2\\nfi\\ncargo build --locked --profile dist --target wasm32-unknown-unknown -p pav_app\\nwasm=${CARGO_TARGET_DIR:-target}/wasm32-unknown-unknown/dist/shardfall.wasm\\nout=target/web\\nif $single; then\\n\\tbindgen=$(mktemp -d)\\n\\ttrap 'rm -rf \\\"$bindgen\\\"' EXIT\\n\\twasm-bindgen --target web --no-typescript --out-dir \\\"$bindgen\\\" --out-name shardfall \\\"$wasm\\\"\\n\\tpython3 -I scripts/pack_single_html.py \\\"$bindgen\\\" \\\"${pack[@]}\\\"\\n\\texit\\nfi\\nrm -rf \\\"$out\\\"\\nmkdir -p \\\"$out\\\"\\nwasm-bindgen --target web --no-typescript --out-dir \\\"$out\\\" --out-name shardfall \\\"$wasm\\\"\\ncp web/index.html web/manifest.webmanifest \\\"$out/\\\"\\nls -la \\\"$out\\\"\\n\",\"total_lines\":46}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"4b58acd1-cb1e-44f5-9249-161ac51c4942"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_988","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The build-web.sh script hardcodes the `--locked` flag.\n\nThe script will not be modified. The Cargo.lock is in sync, so the internal `--locked` flag remains safe.\n\nThe pack4 command pattern is being reconstructed from session history.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-cc22c228-8e45-49e7-88c8-fe4d71c15782-55","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/PLAN.md","offset":660,"limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-cc22c228-8e45-49e7-88c8-fe4d71c15782-56","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"build-web|harbor-target|scene surgery","path":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a%2Fwork/01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2/compaction","glob":"INDEX.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-cc22c228-8e45-49e7-88c8-fe4d71c15782-57","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/TASK.md","limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1857,"output_tokens":576,"cache_read_input_tokens":96128,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"b7571f23-4b19-4ec8-b370-2296af53c7fa"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-cc22c228-8e45-49e7-88c8-fe4d71c15782-55","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"660→\\n## 12. Build order\\n\\nKeep it playable after every step. Build the slot and frame architecture from step 1 so co-op needs no rewrite.\\n\\n1. **Seams.** Set up (§10.1). Scene `surgery/case/0`, `SurgeryFrame`, product frame path, camera shot, plate rendering, one surgeon, forceps on a hook,\\n   rating text, vitals, heart clock. Bot wins it; tests run; browser boots with the shim's session client in place. Check the look at 1920×1080.\\n2. **Hands.** All instruments and hazards, ring, aim assist, chain, results, death and retry, tray, full HUD, cues. Cases 0–2 as data. Bot, coverage and balance tools.\\n3. **Shell.** Title, menus, pause, settings, save, prompts and glyphs, 10-foot style, touch-only, attract, dev mode. Browser tests.\\n4. **Theatre and sound.** Paper rigs, sets, story player, voices, music with buses; story for Cases 0–3.\\n670→5. **Strains and gifts.** Introit and Hush, Lacrimosa, Echo and tremor, bone reassembly, Dies Irae and Cryoprobe, Libera Me and Duet. Cases 3–7 with their stories.\\n6. **Together.** Multi-pad local co-op, Code Blue, partner bot, hub bridge, card, mirror scene, network. Hub tests.\\n7. **Finish.** Balance every case. Polish passes: an audio mix pass, camera punches, particles, transitions. Designer guide. Final package.\\n\\nIf you run short, a smaller finished game beats a bigger unfinished one. Cut in this order: fewer sets, then fewer waves in a strain, then Case 4 or 5's\\nlength. **Never cut:** any tool, gift or screen in this plan, or Case 3 and Case 7.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. Finish line\\n680→\\n**Package.** One self-contained HTML at the path your TASK.md names, built with `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene surgery/title --title … --description …\\n--theme-color … --out …`, with fonts and everything else embedded. It must run from `file://` in Chrome or Edge; browsers without WebGPU get the page's clear message\\n(call `AIHub.fail` in a session). Delete `engine/target` when finished, so only source remains in your dev folder.\\n\\n**Feedback.** Keep notes as you go on what tests caught and what you read or changed in the engine: your TASK.md asks for counts and specifics at the end. Follow its exit\\ninterview shape exactly and check it parses. Suggested catalogue values (name and tagline are yours): type `Game`; genre `Simulation`; source as your TASK.md's; series `Trauma Center`; players `1–4`; controls such as \\\"Right stick or mouse aims, R2 or left click operates, left stick or 1–8 picks tools\\\"; tags from `3d, story, co-op,\\narcade, boss fights, comedy`. Regenerate the About block from those answers and rebuild last, then rerun the web tests.\\n\\n**Done means** no TODOs, placeholders or stubs; every test passes; the campaign and every case can be finished by a person with a pad, or with a mouse.\\n690→\\n### Requirements map\\n\\n| Your TASK.md asks for | Where |\\n|---|---|\\n| Genre and perspective | §1 |\\n| Title riff chosen by RNG, single constant | Top note; you do this |\\n| Single HTML package, dev subfolder | §10.1–10.2, §13 |\\n| Build on the engine, change anything needed | §10 |\\n| Art and animation on the engine's native style, new assets | §6, §7 |\",\"content_concise\":\"660→\\n## 12. Build order\\n\\nKeep it playable after every step. Build the slot and frame architecture from step 1 so co-op needs no rewrite.\\n\\n1. **Seams.** Set up (§10.1). Scene `surgery/case/0`, `SurgeryFrame`, product frame path, camera shot, plate rendering, one surgeon, forceps on a hook,\\n   rating text, vitals, heart clock. Bot wins it; tests run; browser boots with the shim's session client in place. Check the look at 1920×1080.\\n2. **Hands.** All instruments and hazards, ring, aim assist, chain, results, death and retry, tray, full HUD, cues. Cases 0–2 as data. Bot, coverage and balance tools.\\n3. **Shell.** Title, menus, pause, settings, save, prompts and glyphs, 10-foot style, touch-only, attract, dev mode. Browser tests.\\n4. **Theatre and sound.** Paper rigs, sets, story player, voices, music with buses; story for Cases 0–3.\\n670→5. **Strains and gifts.** Introit and Hush, Lacrimosa, Echo and tremor, bone reassembly, Dies Irae and Cryoprobe, Libera Me and Duet. Cases 3–7 with their stories.\\n6. **Together.** Multi-pad local co-op, Code Blue, partner bot, hub bridge, card, mirror scene, network. Hub tests.\\n7. **Finish.** Balance every case. Polish passes: an audio mix pass, camera punches, particles, transitions. Designer guide. Final package.\\n\\nIf you run short, a smaller finished game beats a bigger unfinished one. Cut in this order: fewer sets, then fewer waves in a strain, then Case 4 or 5's\\nlength. **Never cut:** any tool, gift or screen in this plan, or Case 3 and Case 7.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. Finish line\\n680→\\n**Package.** One self-contained HTML at the path your TASK.md names, built with `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene surgery/title --title … --description …\\n--theme-color … --out …`, with fonts and everything else embedded. It must run from `file://` in Chrome or Edge; browsers without WebGPU get the page's clear message\\n(call `AIHub.fail` in a session). Delete `engine/target` when finished, so only source remains in your dev folder.\\n\\n**Feedback.** Keep notes as you go on what tests caught and what you read or changed in the engine: your TASK.md asks for counts and specifics at the end. Follow its exit\\ninterview shape exactly and check it parses. Suggested catalogue values (name and tagline are yours): type `Game`; genre `Simulation`; source as your TASK.md's; series `Trauma Center`; players `1–4`; controls such as \\\"Right stick or mouse aims, R2 or left click operates, left stick or 1–8 picks tools\\\"; tags from `3d, story, co-op,\\narcade, boss fights, comedy`. Regenerate the About block from those answers and rebuild last, then rerun the web tests.\\n\\n**Done means** no TODOs, placeholders or stubs; every test passes; the campaign and every case can be finished by a person with a pad, or with a mouse.\\n690→\\n### Requirements map\\n\\n| Your TASK.md asks for | Where |\\n|---|---|\\n| Genre and perspective | §1 |\\n| Title riff chosen by RNG, single constant | Top note; you do this |\\n| Single HTML package, dev subfolder | §10.1–10.2, §13 |\\n| Build on the engine, change anything needed | §10 |\\n| Art and animation on the engine's native style, new assets | §6, §7 |\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":660,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"\\n## 12. Build order\\n\\nKeep it playable after every step. Build the slot and frame architecture from step 1 so co-op needs no rewrite.\\n\\n1. **Seams.** Set up (§10.1). Scene `surgery/case/0`, `SurgeryFrame`, product frame path, camera shot, plate rendering, one surgeon, forceps on a hook,\\n   rating text, vitals, heart clock. Bot wins it; tests run; browser boots with the shim's session client in place. Check the look at 1920×1080.\\n2. **Hands.** All instruments and hazards, ring, aim assist, chain, results, death and retry, tray, full HUD, cues. Cases 0–2 as data. Bot, coverage and balance tools.\\n3. **Shell.** Title, menus, pause, settings, save, prompts and glyphs, 10-foot style, touch-only, attract, dev mode. Browser tests.\\n4. **Theatre and sound.** Paper rigs, sets, story player, voices, music with buses; story for Cases 0–3.\\n5. **Strains and gifts.** Introit and Hush, Lacrimosa, Echo and tremor, bone reassembly, Dies Irae and Cryoprobe, Libera Me and Duet. Cases 3–7 with their stories.\\n6. **Together.** Multi-pad local co-op, Code Blue, partner bot, hub bridge, card, mirror scene, network. Hub tests.\\n7. **Finish.** Balance every case. Polish passes: an audio mix pass, camera punches, particles, transitions. Designer guide. Final package.\\n\\nIf you run short, a smaller finished game beats a bigger unfinished one. Cut in this order: fewer sets, then fewer waves in a strain, then Case 4 or 5's\\nlength. **Never cut:** any tool, gift or screen in this plan, or Case 3 and Case 7.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. Finish line\\n\\n**Package.** One self-contained HTML at the path your TASK.md names, built with `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene surgery/title --title … --description …\\n--theme-color … --out …`, with fonts and everything else embedded. It must run from `file://` in Chrome or Edge; browsers without WebGPU get the page's clear message\\n(call `AIHub.fail` in a session). Delete `engine/target` when finished, so only source remains in your dev folder.\\n\\n**Feedback.** Keep notes as you go on what tests caught and what you read or changed in the engine: your TASK.md asks for counts and specifics at the end. Follow its exit\\ninterview shape exactly and check it parses. Suggested catalogue values (name and tagline are yours): type `Game`; genre `Simulation`; source as your TASK.md's; series `Trauma Center`; players `1–4`; controls such as \\\"Right stick or mouse aims, R2 or left click operates, left stick or 1–8 picks tools\\\"; tags from `3d, story, co-op,\\narcade, boss fights, comedy`. Regenerate the About block from those answers and rebuild last, then rerun the web tests.\\n\\n**Done means** no TODOs, placeholders or stubs; every test passes; the campaign and every case can be finished by a person with a pad, or with a mouse.\\n\\n### Requirements map\\n\\n| Your TASK.md asks for | Where |\\n|---|---|\\n| Genre and perspective | §1 |\\n| Title riff chosen by RNG, single constant | Top note; you do this |\\n| Single HTML package, dev subfolder | §10.1–10.2, §13 |\\n| Build on the engine, change anything needed | §10 |\\n| Art and animation on the engine's native style, new assets | §6, §7 |\\n\",\"total_lines\":818}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-cc22c228-8e45-49e7-88c8-fe4d71c15782-56","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,48,49,53,95,95,120,52,50,54,55,55,97,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,78,111,32,109,97,116,99,104,101,115,32,102,111,117,110,100,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":1,\"match_count\":0,\"file_matches\":[]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-cc22c228-8e45-49e7-88c8-fe4d71c15782-57","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→SOURCE: Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall/blob/main/START_HERE.md, making a new game on its engine. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\",\"content_concise\":\"1→SOURCE: Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall/blob/main/START_HERE.md, making a new game on its engine. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/TASK.md\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"SOURCE: Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall/blob/main/START_HERE.md, making a new game on its engine. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\nScope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\",\"total_lines\":151}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"f1ea9f94-634b-4736-899c-3a714bf8af0c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_989","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I will implement the WASM tick interval and add a tiny clear to countdown frames so the GPU path stays alive.\n\nThe interval is the key fix. I verified that rAF and setInterval cannot race since the code is single-threaded, and the App pointer remains valid across calls.\n\nI will guard `surgery_frame` with an `IN_FRAME` flag to prevent re-entry. A `Guard` struct will clear the flag on drop.\n\nThe early return for an already-running frame must happen before the Guard is created. Otherwise the guard would clear IN_FRAME while the outer call still holds it.\n\nThe `FrameGuard` struct will clear `IN_FRAME` on drop. It will live in the same thread-local module as the tick installer so it can access the flag.\n\nThe fallback interval will fire every 32 milliseconds. This provides a 30fps safety net if the animation frame loop stalls.\n\nThe interval will not break the 15Hz send cap. Extra frames still sum to real time, so the rate limit stays satisfied.\n\nA panic from the interval firing during a winit callback is impossible because setInterval cannot trigger during synchronous WASM calls.\n\nThe interval only fires when the stack is empty, so it cannot race with the rAF callback. I will skip the tiny countdown clear for now to avoid changing host-leave timing. The fallback interval will keep play frames alive if rAF dies after the first draw.\n\nThe interval will call `surgery_frame` every 32 ms, which re-draws the quiet scene. Fast draws keep the loop alive and the cursor responsive. Slow draws are tolerable because the interval is independent of rAF. A 5-second draw would block both the interval and the test.\n\nThe first draw returned in under 500 ms, so the stall was only a missing reschedule. The interval will fix that. I am also checking whether calling `surgery_frame` outside the winit event loop breaks egui state or wgpu surface acquisition.\n\nWebGPU `getCurrentTexture` requires a requestAnimationFrame callback. The interval may fail to acquire the surface, so draws could skip present. The simulation still ticks and sends updates. The cursor remains responsive even if the picture freezes.\n\nThe interval callback will also call `request_redraw` to keep the animation frame loop armed. This may double-step if the redraw processes synchronously.\n\nCalling `request_redraw` from the interval risks a double frame. The redraw event may process synchronously and enter `surgery_frame` before the interval callback finishes. This would run two frames per tick, which is wasteful but harmless.\n\nI realized `request_redraw` only schedules the canvas animation frame. It does not invoke `surgery_frame` directly. If the interval calls it, the loop revives cleanly without double-stepping.\n\nI realized that `Window::request_redraw` only schedules the canvas animation frame. It does not run the frame itself. If the browser stops firing rAF, calling it from the interval would do nothing. I must call `surgery_frame` directly from the interval to keep the simulation alive. I will still call `request_redraw` so the loop is armed when the browser eventually resumes rAF.\n\nSolo file:// play is unaffected because the interval only adds extra frames there. The fallback loop is safe even if both rAF and the interval fire.\n\nI will restrict the fallback interval to hub-active sessions only. During solo play, rAF already drives the loop, so extra frames from the interval would be redundant. The interval callback will check `hub.active` before calling `surgery_frame`, preventing double-processing and unnecessary ticks.\n\nPolling the harbor link inside the interval would duplicate event processing, since rAF already polls it. Instead, I will gate the interval on time: it only calls `surgery_frame` if the last frame was more than 50 ms ago. A healthy rAF loop keeps frames every ~16 ms, so the interval stays silent and avoids double-polling.\n\nThe interval will be installed at the end of the first `surgery_frame`, so `LAST_MS` is already initialized and cannot trigger an early spurious frame.\n\nI will set `LAST_MS` at the start of `surgery_frame` so a long frame cannot be re-entered by a queued timer. I am now writing the code and updating PLAN.md to note that countdown frames do not present on software GPU, and that the 32 ms fallback timer keeps stepping the simulation if the display callback stalls after the first operation.\n\nI will wrap the interval callback in a panic guard. A renderer panic would otherwise abort the WASM instance and stop the timer.\n\nI will place the interval installer near `install_hook` in `product.rs`. I need to add `wasm_bindgen::JsCast` and clone the winit `Window`.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a70e341b-bf65-4910-b323-442edfedcee9-58","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224015__x42677a/work/Hands of Harbor: Second Opinion-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224015/engine/crates/pav_app/src/product.rs","offset":790,"limit":90}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3666,"output_tokens":3110,"cache_read_input_tokens":97920,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"1f6d6474-60b3-4048-9d55-bb298743b24a"}
