       (Some(st), Some(g)) => st.on_window_event(&g.window, &event),\\n        _ => false,\\n    };\\n    let _ = consumed;\\n    match event {\\n        WindowEvent::CloseRequested => el.exit(),\\n        WindowEvent::Resized(sz) => {\\n            if let Some(g) = &mut app.gfx {\\n                g.resize(sz.width, sz.height);\\n            }\\n        }\\n        WindowEvent::RedrawRequested => {\\n            surgery_frame(app);\\n            if app.quit {\\n                el.exit();\\n            }\\n        }\\n        WindowEvent::KeyboardInput { event, .. } => {\\n            #[cfg(target_arch = \\\"wasm32\\\")]\\n            app.resume_audio();\\n            if let PhysicalKey::Code(code) = event.physical_key {\\n\",\"total_lines\":930}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"7ab54788-1192-4651-989b-c3c65ab7eff2"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_990","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I will implement the tick driver.\n\nCountdown frames will skip heavy GPU work while play frames draw. I verified that getCurrentTexture failures outside rAF won't break simulation updates, and that overlapping interval and animation frame calls are safe. I'll mark LAST_MS at the start of each frame to avoid stale checks mid-frame, add a note in the draw comment, update PLAN.md, and then build.\n\nThe deleted targets require a full WASM rebuild. This will take several minutes. I'll run it in the background and monitor for completion.\n\nI'll verify that engine-link exists and points at the dev folder. I'm also tracing how build-web.sh resolves relative output paths to ensure the HTML output lands in the correct location.\n\nThe build script resolves relative --out paths against the caller directory. Invoking it from the work folder will place the HTML output there.\n\nThe target directory path is valid and contains no colon. I will not pass --locked as an extra argument because the build script already includes it. The wasm32 toolchain is present and functional, so I will leave RUSTUP_HOME unchanged. I am also verifying whether a rust-toolchain file exists.\n\nThe raw pointer is read from thread_local on each call rather than captured. I am writing the WASM tick driver with `note_frame_time`, `frame_now_ms`, and `install_tick` helpers.\n\nThe tick callback skips frames when another frame is already running, the last frame was less than 50 ms ago, or the app pointer is null. It then proceeds to update the simulation.\n\nThe tick callback fires every 32 ms. It calls `surgery_frame` on the app pointer. The frame guard uses a `FrameGuard` struct with a `Drop` impl to clear the in-frame flag.\n\nThe `FrameGuard` is created immediately after `install_tick`, so the in-frame flag clears before the JavaScript event loop resumes. This prevents the interval from re-entering while the frame is still running. The interval callback cannot be re-entered synchronously because it only fires after returning to JavaScript.\n\nThe reentry guard will be placed after the gfx and host null checks so early returns do not set the in-frame flag. I am also verifying that every return path drops the guard.\n\nThe frame guard is safe even without early returns because it drops at the end of the function. Thread locals are cfg-gated so they only exist on wasm. The tick installer only runs once; later calls simply update the last-frame timestamp and app pointer.\n\nI corrected the tick installer to check TICK_ON before looking up the window, so a missing window won't leave the flag stuck. I'll add the new thread locals to the existing block using `Cell::new(std::ptr::null_mut())`.\n\nI will declare the new thread locals as `const { Cell::new(...) }` so they compile on both native and wasm. They will be unused on native, which is harmless.\n\nI will cfg the tick machinery so the new thread locals and helpers live only inside wasm functions. I also need to add a safety comment for the raw pointer dereference, update the draw skip comment to explain the timer, and verify whether key events still reach winit when the animation frame loop is dead.\n\nI checked whether keyboard events still reach winit when the animation frame loop is frozen. They do: DOM listeners call runner.send_event, and because the runner is not borrowed during Wait, those events process immediately and can wake the loop and request redraws. The test dispatches keydown after the freeze, so it should still trigger a redraw.\n\nThe test dispatches the key event on the iframe window, not the canvas. If winit listens for it on the canvas, the event might never reach the listener. The cursor did not move, so the key event likely never reached winit. This does not disprove the theory that the animation frame loop died.\n\nThe key event likely reached winit and would have restarted the loop via about_to_wait. Its absence suggests the loop is wedged and send_events never runs.\n\nThe interval-driven frame would read harbor.keys even if the animation frame never fires. The key event must have reached winit and been stored for that to matter.\n\nThe key event reaches winit and stores keys in harbor.keys even when the animation frame loop is dead. The simulation simply does not step to consume them. The interval-driven surgery_frame will read those keys and move the cursor.\n\nThe test holds keydown for the polling period without keyup, so the key stays held and compose() will see it on subsequent frames. I will update PLAN.md and check disk space, running Chromium processes, and the HTTP server before starting the build.\n\nI will start the full WASM rebuild in the background with a long timeout, since it may take five to fifteen minutes. I can then continue working while the build completes.\n\nI will set block_until_ms to 0 and monitor the build log so I wake when it finishes. I am writing the log to the scratch directory. I have started the background build command with the single-file flag and scene options.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0606c153-99ea-4647-ab0e-3d0a3d6f911c-59","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"description|theme-color|og:description","glob":"Hands of Harbor*.html","head_limit":20}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0606c153-99ea-4647-ab0e-3d0a3d6f911c-60","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"argparse|description|theme","glob":"**/pack_single_html.py"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4192,"output_tokens":3282,"cache_read_input_tokens":101504,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa38-de6f-7dc0-ae0d-e0f717ed75f2","uuid":"aa95aee8-9df8-4db7-8905-454233ffd9b4"}
